<div class="page shell">
  <section class="letter">
    <p>
      This is Clawd. He sits on a screen about the size of a matchbox and spends
      his day watching Claude Code work — typing when it types, climbing when it
      reads, holding up a sign when it wants your permission for something. When
      nothing has happened for a while he falls asleep. He is, in the most
      literal sense, a tiny animal that reacts to your terminal.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every frame is drawn on your Mac and pushed down a USB cable as a
      rectangle of changed pixels. The panel itself holds no pictures at all —
      it just paints what it is told, eight times a second, forever.
    </p>

    <span class="slot">
      <b>⌁ bill of materials</b>
      One ESP32-C6. One 1.47-inch panel. A USB-C cable. Some very small
      screws. Deez nuts.
    </span>

    <p>
      He knows it is your birthday, by the way. There is a date in his
      configuration and he checks it every morning. Today he is saying something
      he does not say on any other day of the year.
    </p>

    <span class="slot">
      <b>⌁ obligatory</b>
      What's the difference between a joke and three dicks?<br />
      Your mum can't take a joke.
    </span>

    <p class="sign">— with love, and a slightly excessive amount of build tooling</p>

    <p class="next">
      <a href="/j/setup/">Setting it up →</a>
      <span>Twenty minutes, most of it one download.</span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="gallery">
    <h2>Everything he does</h2>
    <p>
      Every one of them tied to something real. These are the actual files the
      panel draws from, running at the size they were drawn at — every limb
      moves on a whole pixel, because at this scale half a pixel is a smudge.
    </p>
    <p class="motion" hidden>
      <span
        >Your device asks for less motion, so they are holding still.</span
      >
      <button type="button">Play them anyway</button>
    </p>

