<script>
  // Each animation gets its own shadow root. They were authored independently
  // and every one of them defines `#torso`, `#left-arm` and a `step` keyframe —
  // inlined together in one document they would overwrite each other. A shadow
  // root scopes both the ids and the stylesheet, and unlike an <img> or an
  // <object> it still runs the CSS animation inside.
  const clips = {"typing":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"2\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: typing (Edit / Write / NotebookEdit)\n\n    Hammering at a laptop hard enough to shake. The claws alternate — on every\n    frame one is down on the keys and the other is up — the body trembles and\n    settles into the work over the loop, the eyes read a line left to right and\n    snap back, and output rises off both claws and cools as it goes.\n\n    Loop: 2s at 8fps = 16 frames. Every period divides 2s exactly — 0.5s, 1s,\n    2s — so frame 16 is frame 0 and the wrap cannot seam. Nothing runs at\n    0.125s, which is the frame interval itself and would sample at one fixed\n    phase forever; 0.125s appears only as a *delay*, where it is the thing that\n    puts the two claws on opposite beats.\n\n    Two seconds rather than one because the eye track needs the room. A line\n    scan is five holds and a carriage return, and at a one-second loop each\n    hold is a single frame — the eyes read as vibrating rather than reading.\n\n    ── Why every transform lands on a whole device pixel ──────────────────\n\n    The previous version leaned on the palette snap in `tools/svg2frames.ts` to\n    harden rotated and eased edges after the fact. It does not do that. It\n    resolves a soft edge onto *the nearest declared colour*, which is not\n    necessarily either of the two the edge is between. An edge of #DE886D at\n    20–30% coverage over black composites to about (49,30,24); its squared\n    distance to the laptop's cold #30363B is 1802, against 3877 to black and\n    6188 to the warm #6F4436 that was added to rescue it. So every softened\n    body edge in the frame snapped grey, and Clawd wore a cold rim across the\n    top of his head on three frames of every loop. Snapping resolves an edge;\n    it does not choose the side you meant.\n\n    Landing on the grid is a weaker constraint than it sounds, because only 16\n    frames exist. The panel plays the rasterised sequence, so the only values a\n    track ever takes are the 16 it is sampled at, and an easing curve is just\n    one way of choosing those 16 values. Writing them out is another, and it is\n    the one that can also put them on the grid. Every track below is authored\n    as holds spanning the sample points, so the timing function never runs and\n    `linear` says that honestly. 0.5s tracks get keyframes at 0/25/50/75/100%\n    (the sample points of a 0.5s period at 8fps); 1s tracks at multiples of\n    12.5%; 2s tracks at multiples of 6.25%, written as ranges.\n\n    Two things fall out of that, and both are also what\n    `docs/ANIMATION.md`'s element table already asked for:\n\n      - **Nothing rotates.** A rotated edge is diagonal and cannot land on the\n        grid at any scale. On the claws it was worse than a lost invariant: the\n        shoulder pivot puts the claw tip near the *bottom* of its arc, where\n        motion is horizontal, so the -74deg..-99deg sweep moved the visible\n        silhouette 4 device pixels sideways against 0.8 down. It read as a fin\n        flaring in and out, not as a tap.\n      - **Nothing on the body scales.** `scale(1.02, 0.975)` about the hips\n        leaves the torso 88.4 device pixels wide and its top edge on a fraction\n        of a row, so both flanks and the whole 88px top edge fringe at once.\n        The torso translates instead, always downward; the legs sit outside\n        the moving group, so the hips sink into them and never lift off them.\n\n    ── What the tap can and cannot sell ──────────────────────────────────\n\n    The claws do not disappear behind the lid, and no arrangement of this\n    geometry can make them. The visible claw is only ever the part sticking out\n    past the torso — a claw pixel inside x 2..13 is the same colour as the\n    torso behind it — and the lid starts at x=2.25, inside that span. So the\n    tap sells height, not occlusion: on every frame one claw's lowest row is at\n    least six device pixels below the lid's top row (160) and the other's is\n    above it. Contact is the rule `gym` established instead — the lid is drawn\n    over the lower torso, the claws are column-adjacent to the torso, and claw\n    and lid share rows on every strike.\n\n    ── Structure ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Transforms compose by nesting rather than by stacking declarations on one\n    element, because the `animation` shorthand REPLACES: two `animation:` rules\n    matching the same element means the later one silently wins and the earlier\n    animation never runs. That bug shipped twice in `idle.svg`. Every element\n    below carries at most one `animation` declaration, and anything that needs\n    two motions gets a wrapper group for the second.\n\n    Wrapper groups are `#fx-` prefixed and transparent, but this file is not a\n    pure wrapping of `assets/clawd/base.svg` and the tree says so in two\n    places. Every id, rect, coordinate and fill is base.svg's, and both colour\n    groups — `#body-color-group` and `#eyes-color-group` — are still single\n    elements carrying a single fill. What differs is nesting order: the\n    legs are listed before `#torso` rather than after it (they are the same\n    fill and do not overlap, so paint order is not observable), and\n    `#eyes-color-group` is a *child* of `#body-color-group` here rather than\n    its sibling. Both follow from the same constraint: the legs must stay\n    outside the group that moves while the eyes must stay inside it, and there\n    is only one `#body-color-group` to put them on opposite sides of.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n\n      /* ── the body tracks, slow outside fast ──────────────────────────\n         #fx-hunch is the slow one: over the loop he settles one device pixel\n         into the machine and comes back up. #fx-jitter is the fast one nested\n         inside it, dipping to a different depth on every frame. Both translate\n         downward only, in whole device pixels, so they compose to a whole\n         device pixel and the hips never lift off the legs.\n\n         Sum of the two, in device pixels down, frame 0..15:\n         1 0 2 1 2 1 3 1 2 1 3 1 2 0 2 0 — it changes on all 16 frames, and\n         the deepest dip is 3. That last number is a budget, not an\n         observation: the eye rects bottom out at row 151 and the laptop's\n         topmost row is 158, so a dip of 3 leaves three device pixels of body\n         between the eyes and the machine. Deepen it past 6 and the eyes start\n         being eaten on the dips, which is the bug the lid height was chosen to\n         avoid in the first place. */\n      #fx-hunch {\n        animation: hunch 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n      #fx-jitter {\n        animation: key-strike 0.5s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* ── the arms ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         #fx-tap is the strike, at 0.5s: down 10 device pixels on one beat and\n         7 on the next, up 4 in between, so the pattern does not settle into a\n         metronome. The right claw taps on the opposite beat via a -0.125s\n         delay — exactly one frame — folded into the shorthand rather than\n         declared separately, so no later rule can drop it. Both use the same\n         keyframes; the delay is the whole difference.\n\n         #fx-shoulder is a 2s drift of one device pixel, and the two arms drift\n         on different phases. It is small, and its job is arithmetic rather\n         than expressive: without it the 1s eye track and the 0.5s tap make the\n         second half of the loop repeat the first, and the arms hold only 8\n         distinct height pairs across 16 frames. With it they hold 14. */\n      #fx-shoulder-left {\n        animation: reach-left 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n      #fx-shoulder-right {\n        animation: reach-right 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n      #fx-tap-left {\n        animation: tap 0.5s infinite linear;\n      }\n      #fx-tap-right {\n        animation: tap 0.5s -0.125s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* ── the eyes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         The scan is on the wrapper and the blink is on each eye rect, so the\n         two compose instead of one replacing the other. Every hold spans a\n         frame boundary, which is why the percentages look arbitrary: samples\n         land at multiples of 12.5% of the 1s scan, and a hold that started at\n         a sample instead of before it would be caught mid-ramp. */\n      #fx-eye-scan {\n        animation: scan-line 1s infinite linear;\n      }\n      /* A blink is the eye squashing about its own centre, never the eye\n         disappearing. scaleY(0.25) on a 2-unit eye is half a unit — four whole\n         device pixels, since the eye's centre line at y=9 is on the grid and\n         quarter of a unit either side of it is too — so the shut eye is still\n         a drawn line. A hidden eye reads as a dropped frame. `linear`: a lid\n         that accelerates reads as a wince. */\n      #left-eye {\n        transform-origin: 4.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n      #right-eye {\n        transform-origin: 10.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* ── the screen ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         Two shades swapping rather than one fading. An intermediate alpha\n         composites to an intermediate colour, and the snapper resolves that to\n         whichever declared colour happens to be nearest — which is not\n         necessarily either of the two you meant. */\n      #logo-lit {\n        animation: logo-lit 1s infinite linear;\n      }\n      #logo-dim {\n        animation: logo-dim 1s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* ── the output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         Each bit is two rects at the same place on the same rise: a hot one\n         visible for the first half of the climb and a cool one for the second.\n         The handover is the fade — a real fade would blend green into black\n         and the snapper would land the result on the laptop's grey, because\n         that is genuinely the nearest declared colour to it. Declaring a\n         cooler green as its own fill is what makes the tone available instead\n         of manufactured.\n\n         `#2EA043` and not the arithmetic midpoint of green and black. The\n         midpoint of *any* two palette colours is a magnet for every other\n         ramp: `#3F7343` was tried, and because it also sits near the middle of\n         the body-against-black ramp it captured the torso's top edge and drew\n         a green hairline across Clawd's head on every frame the body squashed.\n         Pick a tone that belongs to its own hue, not one that splits a gap.\n\n         `linear`, and every opacity percentage on a frame boundary: a timing\n         function applies per keyframe interval, so easing here would sample an\n         opacity ramp partway and put a half-lit bit on screen. The rise is\n         12 units over 2s, which at 8fps and 8 device pixels per unit is 6\n         whole pixels a frame. */\n      #fx-bits-hot rect {\n        animation: bit-hot 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n      #fx-bits-cool rect {\n        animation: bit-cool 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n      /* One delay per bit, applied to both halves of it so the handover stays\n         put. Written out rather than strided: an nth-child stride hands the\n         left and right streams the same delay set and the two sides then rise\n         as exact mirror images of each other. Delays are all multiples of the\n         0.125s frame interval, so every bit samples at a clean phase. */\n      #fx-bits-hot rect:nth-child(1),\n      #fx-bits-cool rect:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: 0s; }\n      #fx-bits-hot rect:nth-child(2),\n      #fx-bits-cool rect:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: -0.75s; }\n      #fx-bits-hot rect:nth-child(3),\n      #fx-bits-cool rect:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: -1.5s; }\n      #fx-bits-hot rect:nth-child(4),\n      #fx-bits-cool rect:nth-child(4) { animation-delay: -0.25s; }\n      #fx-bits-hot rect:nth-child(5),\n      #fx-bits-cool rect:nth-child(5) { animation-delay: -1s; }\n      #fx-bits-hot rect:nth-child(6),\n      #fx-bits-cool rect:nth-child(6) { animation-delay: -1.75s; }\n\n      /* ── keyframes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         Every value below is a multiple of 0.125px — one device pixel at the\n         8x render scale — and every hold spans at least one sample point. There\n         used to be two scale factors here as the exception, belonging to the\n         shadow rect; that rect and its track are gone, so the rule holds\n         without exception now. */\n\n\n      /* Down one pixel for frames 3..12, level either side of that. Sampled at\n         multiples of 6.25%, so the holds are written to straddle those. */\n      @keyframes hunch {\n        0%,  17%  { transform: translateY(0); }\n        18%, 80%  { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        81%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }\n      }\n\n      /* Four dips of different depths per half second — 1, 0, 2, 0 device\n         pixels. Strikes alternate every frame, so he never fully comes back\n         up; he trembles at four different heights instead, which reads as\n         effort rather than as a bounce. */\n      @keyframes key-strike {\n        0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        25%      { transform: translateY(0); }\n        50%      { transform: translateY(0.25px); }\n        75%      { transform: translateY(0); }\n      }\n\n      /* Lift, strike, lift, strike shallower. Up 4 device pixels off the base\n         pose, down 10 and then 7 from it. */\n      @keyframes tap {\n        0%, 100% { transform: translateY(-0.5px); }\n        25%      { transform: translateY(1.25px); }\n        50%      { transform: translateY(-0.5px); }\n        75%      { transform: translateY(0.875px); }\n      }\n\n      /* The shoulder drift. Non-alternating with matching 0% and 100%\n         keyframes, which is the other way to keep a 2s track seamless. The\n         left shoulder is down for frames 7..13, the right for frames 0..3 and\n         12..15 — deliberately different phases, so the two arms never repeat a\n         pair of heights they have already held. */\n      @keyframes reach-left {\n        0%,  43%  { transform: translateY(0); }\n        44%, 87%  { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        88%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }\n      }\n      @keyframes reach-right {\n        0%,  24%  { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        25%, 74%  { transform: translateY(0); }\n        75%, 100% { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Five holds across the line and a carriage return. Sampled at 0, 12.5,\n         25, 37.5, 50, 62.5, 75 and 87.5% that reads -1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 1, 1,\n         -1, -1: he reaches the end of the line, dwells on it, and snaps back. */\n      @keyframes scan-line {\n        0%,   6%  { transform: translateX(-1px); }\n        7%,  19%  { transform: translateX(-0.5px); }\n        20%, 31%  { transform: translateX(0); }\n        32%, 44%  { transform: translateX(0.5px); }\n        45%, 69%  { transform: translateX(1px); }\n        70%, 100% { transform: translateX(-1px); }\n      }\n\n      /* One frame shut, once per loop — frame 12, the 75% sample. Held either\n         side of it so neither neighbour catches the ramp. */\n      @keyframes blink {\n        0%,  70%  { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        71%, 80%  { transform: scaleY(0.25); }\n        81%, 100% { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes logo-lit {\n        0%, 43.75% { opacity: 1; }\n        50%, 100%  { opacity: 0; }\n      }\n      @keyframes logo-dim {\n        0%, 43.75% { opacity: 0; }\n        50%, 100%  { opacity: 1; }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes bit-hot {\n        0%      { transform: translateY(0); opacity: 1; }\n        43.75%  { opacity: 1; }\n        50%     { opacity: 0; }\n        100%    { transform: translateY(-12px); opacity: 0; }\n      }\n      @keyframes bit-cool {\n        0%      { transform: translateY(0); opacity: 0; }\n        43.75%  { opacity: 0; }\n        50%     { opacity: 1; }\n        81.25%  { opacity: 1; }\n        87.5%   { opacity: 0; }\n        100%    { transform: translateY(-12px); opacity: 0; }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg, wrapped but not moved ──── -->\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <!-- Planted. The legs sit outside every animated wrapper, so the body\n           can dip against them instead of sliding as one rigid block. Only\n           their bottom unit clears the laptop, which is the part that matters:\n           four feet under the machine, not moving, while everything above them\n           shakes. -->\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n\n      <g id=\"fx-hunch\">\n        <g id=\"fx-jitter\">\n          <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n\n          <g id=\"fx-shoulder-left\">\n            <g id=\"fx-tap-left\">\n              <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <\/g>\n          <\/g>\n          <g id=\"fx-shoulder-right\">\n            <g id=\"fx-tap-right\">\n              <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <\/g>\n          <\/g>\n\n          <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n            <g id=\"fx-eye-scan\">\n              <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n              <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <\/g>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- ── the laptop, in front of Clawd ─────────────────────────────────── -->\n  <!-- Seen from behind the lid, the way upstream frames it: the machine faces\n       him, so what we see is the back of the screen. That is what makes the lid\n       the natural home for a pack's logo — it is on screen whenever Claude is\n       writing code, rather than once on a splash nobody looks at twice.\n\n       Drawn after #master-group so it occludes the lower torso and the tops of\n       the legs, which is what puts it in front. It does not tremble with him,\n       because it is sitting on a desk.\n\n       The lid top at y=11 is the load-bearing number. The eyes sit two units\n       above it and the body dips into it, so the gap is what the dip depth is\n       budgeted against: at the deepest frame the eye rects end on device row\n       154 and the laptop's topmost row is 158. Three device pixels, on every\n       frame — small, and it is the reason #fx-hunch and #fx-jitter are capped\n       where they are rather than a property that survives deepening them. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-laptop\">\n    <!-- Two device pixels of warm dark where the machine meets him — the\n         occlusion a pixel artist would hand-place under any object resting\n         against a body. It also puts a tone halfway down the ramp from the\n         body colour to black, so that any body edge that does soften has\n         somewhere warm to land instead of reaching for the screen's grey.\n         That is insurance, not the mechanism: what actually keeps the grey rim\n         off his head is that no body edge softens in the first place. -->\n    <g id=\"fx-laptop-shade\" fill=\"#6F4436\">\n      <rect x=\"2.25\" y=\"10.75\" width=\"10.5\" height=\"0.25\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-laptop-screen\" fill=\"#30363B\">\n      <rect x=\"2.25\" y=\"11\" width=\"10.5\" height=\"2.5\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-laptop-body\" fill=\"#8B949E\">\n      <rect x=\"1.75\" y=\"13.5\" width=\"11.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- Where a pixelated logo goes. The two rects carry their own fills rather\n       than inheriting a group fill, because a mark is replaced wholesale\n       rather than tinted.\n\n       **`#logo-lit` and `#logo-dim` are load-bearing ids, and a careless\n       replacement kills the pulse silently.** They are the only elements the\n       two `#logo-*` rules in the stylesheet above match, and the two of them alternating on a 1s loop is what\n       makes the screen read as lit rather than painted. Paste in a set of\n       rects and both ids go with them: `svg2frames` reports two fewer\n       animations and does not warn, every gate stays green, and the lid goes\n       static. To keep the pulse, emit the mark twice — once inside each of\n       these two elements, which are `<rect>`s today and would have to become\n       `<g>`s carrying the lit and dim fills — because `tools/logo2pixel.ts --format rects` produces one\n       silhouette with no fill of its own, not a two-tone picture.\n\n       **No pack does any of this today.** Sprites are baked to fixed RGB565\n       and no pack palette reaches one; `packages/packs` has no logo field.\n       Getting a mark here means editing this file and re-baking, and for\n       personal artwork that has to stay a *private* bake, because the repo is\n       public. Note what that leaves dirty: this file and\n       `packages/renderer/src/sprites/typing.data.ts`, which is tracked, is\n       base64, and carries a fingerprint of this SVG that `bake-sprites.test.ts`\n       checks — so the suite passes while both are modified, and a mark hidden\n       in base64 is invisible to review. `BUILD_PLAN.md` Stage 5 carries the\n       pack-field route that avoids all of this. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-laptop-logo\">\n    <rect id=\"logo-lit\" x=\"7\" y=\"11.75\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#58A6FF\"/>\n    <rect id=\"logo-dim\" x=\"7\" y=\"11.75\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#1F6FEB\"/>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- ── additive effects: not part of the base geometry ───────────────── -->\n  <!-- Two streams, one off each claw, in the columns outside the torso. The\n       torso spans device columns 40..127; the left stream ends at column 37\n       and the right starts at column 130, so a bit is never inside the torso\n       and never 4-adjacent to it either. That second half is the part an\n       earlier version got wrong: the columns were nominally clear, but a bit\n       at x=1 ends exactly where the torso begins and welds itself to the\n       corner. Two device pixels of clearance is what makes it float.\n\n       They do start on the claws, and that is the point — a stream that\n       emerges from a moving part reads as output, and one that appears in\n       empty air reads as confetti.\n\n       Twelve rects, not twelve bits: the hot group and the cool group hold the\n       same six positions, and a matching pair shares a delay so the cool one\n       takes over exactly where the hot one stops. Rise is 12 units from about\n       y=9.5, and the cool half retires at 87.5% of its cycle — topmost visible\n       pixel is y=-0.5, well inside the y=-4 line that landscape crops to. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-data-bits\">\n    <g id=\"fx-bits-hot\" fill=\"#7EE787\">\n      <rect x=\"0.5\" y=\"9.5\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0.75\" y=\"9.25\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0.25\" y=\"9.75\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13.25\" y=\"9.25\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"9.5\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13.75\" y=\"9.75\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-bits-cool\" fill=\"#2EA043\">\n      <rect x=\"0.5\" y=\"9.5\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0.75\" y=\"9.25\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0.25\" y=\"9.75\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13.25\" y=\"9.25\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"9.5\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13.75\" y=\"9.75\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","bouldering":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"4\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: bouldering (Read)\n\n    Grep and Glob appear zero times in 45,000 real tool calls, so Read is the\n    whole trigger — see BUILD_PLAN.md. At 17.6% it is the second-busiest screen\n    after `gym`, which means it gets watched, and neither earlier version\n    survived being watched.\n\n    ── Why this file was rebuilt, twice ───────────────────────────────────\n\n    The first version banned rotation, non-uniform scale and easing outright,\n    so nothing could reach for anything. Clawd hung motionless in front of a\n    moving wall and the note in the file admitted it.\n\n    The second lifted the ban and overshot into a nine-stop IK table —\n    `rotate()` plus `scaleX` up to 2.136 — solving each claw onto a named hold\n    every frame. Stretched to twice its length a 2x2 claw stops being a claw\n    and becomes a tab, and a tab parked flat against the torso edge at eye\n    height is a snout: for half the loop the sprite read as a pig facing left.\n    The other half read as a startled face, because an additive mouth scaled to\n    a 27x14 black hole sat low on the torso — three times the width of an eye —\n    with both claws splayed sideways beside it. The torso rotated 2deg, which\n    staircased an 88x56 slab into a parallelogram. The feet moved 8 device\n    pixels a loop against the wall's 64: the hands tracked the rock and the\n    feet skated.\n\n    None of that machinery is here. There is no mouth, because two eye holes\n    plus a third dark shape is a face. Nothing rotates but the two claws, and\n    they rotate about their own centres. The torso and the legs only translate.\n    `scaleX` appears nowhere. What replaced the IK table is one number.\n\n    ── One number holds the scene together ────────────────────────────────\n\n    The wall falls a quarter of a unit per frame — two device pixels, a whole\n    number, two units a second — and everything in contact with it falls at\n    exactly that rate:\n\n      the wall        0.25 a frame, all 32 frames\n      a gripping claw 0.25 a frame, the 12 frames it is on the hold\n      a weighted foot 0.25 a frame, the 4 frames it is planted\n\n    Because a claw and the rock descend at the same speed, whatever the claw\n    grabs stays under it for the whole pull without anything being solved: the\n    claw's lower corner sits about half a unit inside the hold's band at the\n    grab and is still there at the release. Nothing slides, because nothing is\n    aimed.\n\n    The same arithmetic lays out the route, backwards. A hand is on for 12\n    frames and off for 4, so it returns to the same place on screen every 16 —\n    by which time the wall has moved 4 units. Four units is therefore the hold\n    spacing, and each hand climbs exactly one hold a cycle. Because a column\n    alternates a 2x1 jug with a smaller crimp every 4 units, alternate grabs\n    take a jug and then a crimp, which is what a route looks like and what\n    keeps a column from reading as a dotted line.\n\n    ── Where a claw may be ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    At the side of the torso and inside the torso's own vertical band, always.\n    The claw's centre travels from y=7.6 down to y=10.6 and overshoots back to\n    7.5 as it settles, so its topmost corner is never higher than y=6.10 and\n    its lowest never deeper than y=12.0, against a torso occupying 6 to 13 —\n    and on the two frames a cycle where the claw is highest the bob has lifted\n    the torso's edge two device pixels further out of its way.\n\n    That bound is the finding this rebuild cost. The first attempt had both\n    claws reaching overhead, two units above the top edge at the corners, and\n    at true size it read as a pig — not faintly, not arguably.\n    Clawd is drawn front-on, and a protrusion at the top corner of a wide slab\n    is an ear whatever angle it is turned to. Two claws out at the *sides* is\n    the pose the geometry already reads as: `idle` parks them there and reads\n    as a crab. So the reach is vertical inside that band, three units of it,\n    and the two sides alternate — one claw high on its hold while the other is\n    low and finishing its pull.\n\n    The turn stays between 36 and 58deg, where a 2x2 block renders as a\n    diamond and is its own shape. At 0deg it is the flat tab that read as a\n    snout and at 90deg it is a square again, so both ends of the range are\n    kept a good 30deg clear. The diamond crosses the torso edge over about\n    eight device pixels and protrudes eighteen — connected at every stop of the\n    loop, and never a lump on the silhouette.\n\n    ── The beats, per 16-frame cycle ──────────────────────────────────────\n\n      f0-f12   the left claw is on its hold and rides it down\n      f12-f16  it snatches up, overshoots by a device pixel and settles onto\n               the next hold — 4 units higher in wall coordinates, and so in\n               exactly the same place on screen\n      f4-f8    the right claw takes its own four frames off, half a cycle out\n               of step, and is on the rock for the other twelve\n\n    One hand is always on the rock and for half the cycle both are: the four\n    frames either claw is off are four the other is well into its pull.\n\n    **The body moves between the hands.** Two nested tracks, both translation:\n    `#climb-sway` leans three device pixels toward whichever claw is high and\n    freshly loaded, once per stride; `#climb-bob` inside it drops one pixel as\n    a grip loads and lifts two through the pull, once per pull. The arms sit\n    inside the sway and outside the bob, which is the whole point of splitting\n    them — a lean that left the arms behind would peel a claw off a torso edge\n    it overlaps by four pixels, while a bob that carried them would move the\n    sprite as a rigid block and drag the grip off the hold.\n\n    **The feet step.** They are why this reads as a wall rather than a\n    backdrop: a climber whose rock moves while his feet do not is standing\n    still. Each leg rides down a whole unit at wall speed over four frames,\n    snaps up in two and waits two to be placed again — a 1s cycle, left pair\n    against right pair at half a cycle, inner and outer one frame apart so four\n    identical stubs never read as one wide foot. Travel is upward from the base\n    position only. A leg is 2 units long and welded to the torso base at y=13,\n    so translating one *down* opens a gap at the hip, and a limb detached from\n    the body is the one misreading that costs something on a panel which also\n    shows real display faults.\n\n    **The gaze leads the reach.** The eyes sit a quarter-unit high throughout —\n    he is looking up the wall — and shift half a unit toward whichever claw is\n    off the rock, arriving two frames before that claw leaves the rock. They never squash: an\n    expression here would be bought at the price of a frame with an eye\n    missing, and a missing eye reads as a dropped frame.\n\n    ── The wall ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Kept from the previous version, because the wall was never what was wrong.\n    Panel joints every 4 units with the vertical seam alternating side each\n    course, so it reads as bonded sheets of ply rather than as ruled paper.\n    The joints are also what stop the holds reading as falling confetti —\n    without a surface he is a character in empty space with blocks going past.\n\n    **Drawn on a filled rock face, which reverses an earlier decision.** That\n    decision was measured rather than guessed — a filled slate face cost\n    22,235 B/s against 21,792 for none at the time, a 2% difference. That is not\n    this decision's cost: 21,792 was measured before the violet hold undersides\n    were deleted, and unfilled today is 19,992, so filling it is +2,576 B/s or\n    +13% — and it\n    was dropped because at 172px a slate field flattened the scene and because\n    every other screen was on black, so one filled background read as the odd\n    one out.\n\n    Both halves of that stopped being true. No screen is on black any more:\n    `packages/renderer/src/environment.ts` puts a rock pool behind every\n    animation, and unfilled, this one let the sky through its joints — it read\n    as wires strung across the horizon with purple notes pegged to them, with\n    Clawd on sand below the sea line. And the face is not slate now but the\n    sand's own family, which is what stops it flattening: it reads as a sea\n    cliff at the edge of the beach he lives on rather than a gym wall pasted\n    over it. Cost is 22,568 B/s, 4% of the measured link.\n\n    The prediction that a filled face would mute the joints and stop the holds\n    popping came half true — the joints are muted, the holds are not — and the\n    joint tone stays anyway. Measured after the RGB565 round trip the face is\n    (74,65,58) and the joint (49,53,58): a 5.4% step measured against full\n    scale, which is 20.9% measured against the face — the smaller number was\n    quoted here first and it was the one that made the argument, which is the\n    wrong way round. Luminance does carry them. What hue adds is that the\n    per-channel delta is +25R/+12G/0B: a cool line on a warm field, so they stay\n    legible without competing with the holds. Muted is also right now — they read as\n    bedding planes in rock, which is the scene, rather than as ply seams, which\n    was the gym. A darker, warmer joint would cost a new palette entry, and\n    §Palette snapping has caught this file twice already.\n\n    **The scroll must tile.** Each hold column repeats every 8 units, the joint\n    stagger every 8, and the wall travels exactly 8 units a loop, so the last\n    frame is one ordinary step from the first. Any other pairing of period and\n    travel jumps once per loop, which is exactly the cadence at which the eye\n    notices. Verified by rendering two loops and hashing: t=0 and t=4s are\n    byte-identical.\n\n    **No shadow, and this file is the reason the renderer has to be told.**\n    Every other animation stands him on a floor; here he is on a wall, and a\n    floor shadow under a climber is worse than no shadow — which is what the\n    plan asked for, what an early version quietly overrode by scaling it into a\n    slab on the wall, and what a *second* version overrode again when the\n    shadow moved into the renderer and was drawn for everything.\n\n    That second attempt is worth recording because the evidence for it looked\n    fine and was measuring the wrong thing. It rested on his feet being on the\n    same line as the other animations' — which is true, his lowest body pixel\n    is unit 14.875 like theirs — argued from \"unit 15.13\", which is frame 0's\n    lowest *drawn* pixel and is a wall hold. The loop maximum of 15.625 is the\n    joint band. Both numbers are scenery.\n\n    What rules it out is that his legs leave the ground line on 24 frames of\n    32, by up to six device pixels, and that the joint band scrolls through the\n    shadow row and covers 89% of it on four frames in every thirty-two — so it\n    blinked twice a loop.\n\n    `castsShadow` in packages/renderer/src/environment.ts is what holds the\n    line now, and it names this animation.\n\n    `data-safe-area=\"ignore\"` exempts the wall from the landscape safe-area\n    check: its tiles run past the crop and lose nothing, because the pattern\n    repeats. The marker sits on the clip path as well as on the group, since\n    `topmostVisibleUnit` walks every `rect` in the document including the one\n    inside `<clipPath>`, and an unmarked clip rect reports a bounding box at\n    the origin and trips the check. That clip used to inset the wall\n    from the frame — 2 device pixels left, right and bottom, 4 at the top — so\n    no joint scrolling in ever landed in row 0. It is the viewBox exactly now,\n    which means it clips nothing — the root `<svg>` already bounds the viewport\n    — and it is kept only because `topmostVisibleUnit` walks every `rect` in\n    the document and the marker above is what exempts these. Treat it as inert.\n    It runs to the frame edge now, and the\n    inset would be a defect rather than a guard: against a black stage a joint\n    reaching row 0 was a bar ending in nothing, and against a full-bleed rock\n    it is the wall continuing past the frame, which is what a cliff does. What\n    keeps a joint from ending in air is `#rock-face`, not this.\n\n    Every element below carries exactly one `animation` declaration in exactly\n    one rule. The shorthand replaces rather than merges, and an earlier idle\n    shipped a part named in two rules: it silently lost the first animation and\n    hung eight device pixels off the body. Concurrent motion is nested instead\n    — `#arm-left-reach` translates and `#claw-left-turn` inside it rotates — so\n    the transforms compose rather than compete.\n\n    Loop: 4s at 8fps = 32 frames. Three periods, 4s, 2s and 1s, each dividing\n    the loop a whole number of times; no track alternates, so the even-count\n    rule does not arise. One frame is 6.25% of a 2s track and 12.5% of a 1s\n    one, and every keyframe below is a whole multiple of its own track's frame.\n    Nothing animates opacity, so no frame can sample a half-alpha pixel the\n    palette snap would have to invent a colour for.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <!-- Marked ignore for the same reason the group is: this rect is real to\n         `querySelectorAll('rect')` even though it never paints. -->\n    <clipPath id=\"wall-crop\" data-safe-area=\"ignore\">\n      <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-9\" width=\"21\" height=\"25\"/>\n    <\/clipPath>\n\n    <!-- The rock's own silhouette. Applied to the texture group\n         *inside* the scroll, so it travels with it and the joints\n         and holds can never leave the rock. -->\n    <clipPath id=\"rock-face\" data-safe-area=\"ignore\">\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-17\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-15\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-13\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-10\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-9\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-7\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-5\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-2\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-1\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"1\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"3\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"6\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"7\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"9\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"11\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"14\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"15\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"17\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"19\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"22\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"23\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"25\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"27\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"30\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/clipPath>\n\n    <style>\n      /* ── the wall ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         Flat, and linear on purpose. The claws and the feet track it by\n         matching its speed exactly, so any easing here would have to be solved\n         back into every limb keyframe to keep the grips from sliding. A\n         following camera pans steadily anyway; the surge lives in the body. */\n      #fx-wall-scroll {\n        animation: wall-scroll 4s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* ── the climber ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         The lean, once per stride, wrapping everything including the arms.\n         Translation only, here and in the bob: a 2.5deg rotation of an 88x56\n         slab was tried in `idle` and staircased into a parallelogram, and\n         non-uniform scale on a part this size walks its edges a whole pixel\n         between frames. A body this large leans by moving, not by turning. */\n      #climb-sway {\n        animation: climb-sway 2s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* The pull, once a second, inside the lean. Torso, legs and eyes only —\n         the arms are outside it so the hands hold their holds while the body\n         travels past them, which is what a pull is. */\n      #climb-bob {\n        animation: climb-bob 1s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* ── the arms ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         Two tracks each, nested. The outer one carries the claw up and down\n         the wall; the inner one turns it about its own centre. The turn never\n         leaves the 36-58deg band, where a 2x2 block renders as a diamond: at\n         0deg it is the flat tab against the torso edge that read as a snout,\n         and at 90deg it is a square again. */\n      #arm-left-reach {\n        animation: reach-left 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #claw-left-turn {\n        transform-origin: 1px 10px; /* the left claw's own centre */\n        animation: turn-left 2s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* Half a cycle behind, so one hand is always on the rock. The delay\n         rides inside the shorthand, which resets a separate\n         `animation-delay`. */\n      #arm-right-reach {\n        animation: reach-right 2s -1s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #claw-right-turn {\n        transform-origin: 14px 10px; /* the right claw's own centre */\n        animation: turn-right 2s -1s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* ── the face ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         One track, and no strain: the eyes hold their shape for the whole\n         loop. A squashed eye here would buy an expression at the price of\n         frames with an eye missing, which read as dropped frames. */\n      #eyes-look {\n        animation: spot-the-hold 2s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* ── the legs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         One cycle shared by all four, put out of phase by delay rather than by\n         four sets of keyframes. Left pair against right pair at half a cycle;\n         outer behind inner by a single frame. */\n      #step-inner-left {\n        animation: step 1s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #step-outer-left {\n        animation: step 1s -0.125s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #step-inner-right {\n        animation: step 1s -0.5s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #step-outer-right {\n        animation: step 1s -0.625s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* ── keyframes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\n      /* 8 units a loop: one joint-stagger period, two hold periods. */\n      @keyframes wall-scroll {\n        from { transform: translateY(0); }\n        to   { transform: translateY(8px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Three device pixels toward whichever claw is high and freshly loaded,\n         once per stride. The arms ride inside this one — a lean that left them\n         behind would peel a claw off the torso edge, which is 4 device pixels\n         of overlap and cannot spare 3. */\n      @keyframes climb-sway {\n        0%   { transform: translateX(-0.375px); }\n        50%  { transform: translateX(0.375px); }\n        100% { transform: translateX(-0.375px); }\n      }\n\n      /* One device pixel down as the grip loads, two up through the pull. */\n      @keyframes climb-bob {\n        0%    { transform: translateY(0); }\n        25%   { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        62.5% { transform: translateY(-0.25px); }\n        100%  { transform: translateY(0); }\n      }\n\n      /* Twelve frames on the hold, falling 0.25 a frame — the wall's own\n         speed, stated once as a straight line between two stops rather than as\n         twelve solved positions. Then four frames off it: up, a device pixel\n         past, and settled onto the next hold, which is 4 units higher in wall\n         coordinates and so in exactly the same place on screen. The claw's x\n         never changes; the lean carries it sideways instead. */\n      @keyframes reach-left {\n        0%     { transform: translate(0.1px, -2.4px); }\n        75%    { transform: translate(0.1px, 0.6px);\n                 animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(.2,.75,.3,1); }\n        81.25% { transform: translate(0.1px, -1.1px); }\n        87.5%  { transform: translate(0.1px, -2.5px); }\n        100%   { transform: translate(0.1px, -2.4px); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes turn-left {\n        0%     { transform: rotate(46deg); }\n        75%    { transform: rotate(36deg); }\n        81.25% { transform: rotate(58deg); }\n        87.5%  { transform: rotate(52deg); }\n        100%   { transform: rotate(46deg); }\n      }\n\n      /* The same arm mirrored about x=7.5. The delay supplies the phase. */\n      @keyframes reach-right {\n        0%     { transform: translate(-0.1px, -2.4px); }\n        75%    { transform: translate(-0.1px, 0.6px);\n                 animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(.2,.75,.3,1); }\n        81.25% { transform: translate(-0.1px, -1.1px); }\n        87.5%  { transform: translate(-0.1px, -2.5px); }\n        100%   { transform: translate(-0.1px, -2.4px); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes turn-right {\n        0%     { transform: rotate(-46deg); }\n        75%    { transform: rotate(-36deg); }\n        81.25% { transform: rotate(-58deg); }\n        87.5%  { transform: rotate(-52deg); }\n        100%   { transform: rotate(-46deg); }\n      }\n\n      /* Toward whichever claw is off the rock: right while the right hand\n         swings, left while the left one does, two frames before it goes. Half\n         a unit each way — the nearest claw corner stays a full unit clear of\n         the nearest eye, so this cannot walk an eye into an arm. */\n      @keyframes spot-the-hold {\n        0%     { transform: translate(-0.5px, -0.25px); }\n        12.5%  { transform: translate(0.5px, -0.25px); }\n        50%    { transform: translate(0.5px, -0.25px); }\n        62.5%  { transform: translate(-0.5px, -0.25px); }\n        100%   { transform: translate(-0.5px, -0.25px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Weighted and riding the wall down for four frames, snapped up in two,\n         held tucked for two while the other side takes the load. Up from the\n         base position only — a leg translated below its own rect parts from\n         the hips, and 2 units of leg cannot spare any of them. */\n      @keyframes step {\n        0%   { transform: translateY(-1px); }\n        50%  { transform: translateY(0);\n               animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.3,1); }\n        75%  { transform: translateY(-1px); }\n        100% { transform: translateY(-1px); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── the wall, drawn behind Clawd so a hold passes under a claw rather\n       than across it. Joints every 4 units with the seam alternating side; a\n       hold every 4 in each column, the columns staggered so the two never line\n       up. Both patterns repeat every 8 units — the scroll distance.\n       ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->\n  <g id=\"fx-wall\" clip-path=\"url(#wall-crop)\" data-safe-area=\"ignore\">\n    <!-- The rock this is a face of.\n\n         Without it the wall is joints and holds over whatever is behind, and\n         once the rock pool was wired on that was the sky: it read as wires\n         strung across the horizon with purple notes pegged to them, and Clawd\n         standing on sand below the sea line. Two places at once, which\n         `docs/ANIMATION.md` §Clawd lives somewhere warns about — a sky makes\n         sense without the character and a climbing wall does not.\n\n         An opaque face makes it one place. The tone is the sand's family\n         rather than a gym's slate, so it reads as a sea cliff at the edge of\n         the beach he lives on rather than an indoor wall pasted over it, and\n         a crab climbing a rock in his own rock pool is the better joke anyway.\n         Judged composed, against all four skies; suppressing the environment\n         for this animation was the alternative and left half the panel empty.\n\n         The right edge steps because a flat one reads as a box. That is\n         `paintRock`'s own trick in `packages/renderer/src/environment.ts` —\n         \"a stepped silhouette, so a rock reads as rock rather than as a box\" —\n         and the steps are whole units so none of them fringes against the sky.\n\n         Three things a first version got wrong, all the same mistake — it was\n         an opaque backdrop where it needed to be the wall's silhouette. The\n         joints and holds were clipped to the stage rather than to the rock,\n         so joint courses still ran off it into the sky — a review reconstructed\n         the attempt and measured 144 to 224 pixels a frame depending on the\n         configuration. The defect this exists to remove, shrunk rather than\n         gone. The holds never escaped in any version: their maximum x is 15.5\n         and the narrowest face band reaches 17, so they cannot. Figures of\n         1,576 and 128 were quoted here and are wrong — both came from counting\n         pixels beyond the rightmost *face-coloured* pixel on each row, which\n         the joints themselves cover, so the method reports escapes where the\n         rock is merely painted over.\n         The face sat outside `#fx-wall-scroll`, so its edge was nailed to the\n         screen while the texture slid past it, which reads as a window and not\n         as rock. And the crop's two-pixel inset — a virtue on black, where it\n         kept joints out of row 0 — left a strip of sky down the left and\n         across the top, so the rock could not run off the frame the way a\n         cliff must.\n\n         So: the face lives inside the scroll, the texture is clipped to the\n         face's own silhouette, and the crop runs to the frame edge. The\n         silhouette repeats every 8 units because that is the scroll distance,\n         and spans y -17 to 31 so the travel never reveals an end.\n\n         **What the new colour sits between.** §Palette snapping asks this\n         before one is added and it was not asked at the time. Measured after,\n         over all 32 frames, counting wall-coloured pixels strictly inside the\n         torso: 632 pixels of #4A4038 arrive at the antialiased eye edge, where\n         a black-over-peach blend now lands nearer the face than to black,\n         peach or the joint grey. On `main` those same pixels snapped to\n         #30363D, so the artefact count is unchanged at about 62 a frame —\n         about half changed hue from cool grey to warm brown, and the torso's\n         top edge now snaps to the colour it is actually sitting on, which is\n         an improvement. Benign, and recorded because the rule is to ask. -->\n    <g id=\"fx-wall-scroll\">\n      <g id=\"fx-wall-face\" fill=\"#4A4038\">\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-17\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-15\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-13\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-10\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-9\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-7\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-5\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-2\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-1\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"1\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"3\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"6\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"7\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"9\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"11\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"14\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"15\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"17\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"19\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"22\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"23\" width=\"22\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"25\" width=\"21\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"27\" width=\"22\" height=\"3\"/>\n        <rect x=\"-4\" y=\"30\" width=\"21\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <\/g>\n      <g id=\"fx-wall-texture\" clip-path=\"url(#rock-face)\">\n        <!-- Panel courses. Each joint is broken by the vertical seam between two\n             sheets of ply, and the seam alternates side every course, so the\n             stagger repeats every 8 units — the scroll distance. -->\n        <g id=\"fx-wall-joints\" fill=\"#30363D\">\n          <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-14\" width=\"8.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"6.5\" y=\"-14\" width=\"11.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-10\" width=\"13.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"11.5\" y=\"-10\" width=\"6.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-6\" width=\"8.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"6.5\" y=\"-6\" width=\"11.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-2\" width=\"13.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"11.5\" y=\"-2\" width=\"6.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"2\" width=\"8.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"6.5\" y=\"2\" width=\"11.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"6\" width=\"13.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"11.5\" y=\"6\" width=\"6.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"10\" width=\"8.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"6.5\" y=\"10\" width=\"11.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-3\" y=\"14\" width=\"13.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"11.5\" y=\"14\" width=\"6.5\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n        <\/g>\n\n        <!-- The route is solved from the claws rather than the other way round.\n             The left claw's lower corner is at y=9.01 when it grabs at t=0 with\n             the wall at offset 0, so a hold band opens at 8.5 to catch it; the\n             right grabs at t=1s with the wall at offset 2, so its band opens at\n             6.5 in wall coordinates. Everything else is those two positions every\n             4 units, alternating a 2x1 jug with a 1.5x0.75 crimp — which makes\n             each column repeat every 8, and gives each hand a jug one cycle and\n             a crimp the next. -->\n        <g id=\"fx-wall-holds\">\n          <!-- The holds were originally drawn with a shaded underside in\n               #4C3475, to read as bolted proud of the wall rather than painted\n               on it. It was removed, and the reason generalises: every colour a\n               file declares becomes a snap target for every *other* colour's\n               antialiased edges. #4C3475 sits between peach and black, so a\n               torso edge pixel at 55-65% coverage landed nearer to it than to\n               the wall grey, and painted a one-pixel violet line across 80 of\n               the torso's 88 pixels for 250ms, twice a loop. Flat holds are a\n               cheaper price than a violet rim on his head — and removing it also\n               took the animation from 21792 B/s to 19992. -->\n          <g fill=\"#A371F7\">\n            <rect x=\"-0.5\" y=\"-15.5\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n            <rect x=\"-0.5\" y=\"-7.5\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n            <rect x=\"-0.5\" y=\"0.5\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n            <rect x=\"-0.5\" y=\"8.5\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n\n            <rect x=\"0\" y=\"-11.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"0.75\"/>\n            <rect x=\"0\" y=\"-3.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"0.75\"/>\n            <rect x=\"0\" y=\"4.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"0.75\"/>\n            <rect x=\"0\" y=\"12.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"0.75\"/>\n\n            <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"-13.5\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n            <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"-5.5\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n            <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"2.5\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n            <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"10.5\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n\n            <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"-9.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"0.75\"/>\n            <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"-1.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"0.75\"/>\n            <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"6.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"0.75\"/>\n            <rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"14.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"0.75\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg, less its shadow ───────────\n       No shadow element at all — see the note at the top of this file. The\n       renderer draws the ground shadow now and skips it here, because he is\n       on a wall. -->\n\n  <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n    <!-- The arms sit inside #climb-sway and outside #climb-bob, so they lean\n         with the body but hold still while it pulls past them. Drawn before\n         the torso, so where a claw overlaps the\n         body the torso covers it; both are the same fill, so the join is\n         seamless either way. -->\n    <g id=\"climb-sway\">\n      <g id=\"arm-left-reach\">\n        <g id=\"claw-left-turn\">\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"arm-right-reach\">\n        <g id=\"claw-right-turn\">\n          <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"climb-bob\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n\n        <g id=\"legs-group\">\n          <g id=\"step-outer-left\">\n            <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n          <g id=\"step-inner-left\">\n            <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n          <g id=\"step-inner-right\">\n            <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n          <g id=\"step-outer-right\">\n            <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n\n        <!-- Nested inside the body colour group rather than beside it, because\n             the eyes must ride the bob with the torso. Editing the two group\n             fills is an authoring change and a re-bake, not anything a pack\n             does — see `docs/ANIMATION.md`.\n\n             Note this file is one of the easy ones — its legs sit inside the\n             body group, as do `asleep`'s and `typing`'s, so a single fill\n             covers them. Most animations declare the body colour a second\n             time outside the group: ten on `#legs-group`, and two more where\n             grepping for legs would miss it (`overheated`'s `#arm-fan`,\n             `permission-sign`'s `#right-arm-joint`). Run the grep in\n             `docs/ANIMATION.md` rather than trusting a heuristic. -->\n        <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n          <g id=\"eyes-look\">\n            <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","gym":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"3\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: gym (Bash)\n\n    Bash is 63.9% of all tool calls, so after `idle` this is the animation seen\n    most. It has to survive being watched, which the first version did not: it\n    was eight frames of a barbell sliding up and down a body that never reacted\n    to it. That was not a failure of care, it was the old contract — rotation,\n    non-uniform scale and easing were all banned to keep every edge on a whole\n    device pixel, and with those gone there is nothing left to act with.\n\n    The ban is lifted for *small parts turned far*. `tools/svg2frames.ts` snaps\n    antialiased pixels back onto the declared palette after rasterising, which\n    is what upstream clawd-tank does and why theirs move like they have mass.\n    Snapping removes intermediate colours; it does not fix sub-art-pixel\n    geometry, so a large slab turned a few degrees still staircases. Nothing\n    here rotates except the two 2x2 claws, which turn far enough that the\n    result is a shape.\n\n    The beats — one rep per loop, 24 frames:\n\n      f0-f2    racked at the chest, sinking into the dip\n      f3       the bottom of the dip: knees folded, bar sunk with the shoulders\n      f4       the bar breaks off the chest\n      f5-f12   the drive, building off the chest and grinding into lockout\n      f12-f15  the strain hold overhead, the bar shivering in his grip\n      f16-f21  the descent, accelerating, a drop of sweat flicked off his brow\n      f21      the rack — hardest squash in the loop\n      f22-f23  rebound and settle\n\n    **Every frame is a keyframe stop.** Twenty-five stops per track, one per\n    rendered frame plus the wrap, so the declared value *is* the rendered\n    value and every track is `linear`. This is not verbosity for its own sake.\n    Two whole classes of defect live in the gap between stops: an eased\n    interpolation next to a hand-placed stop produced a fast-slow-fast stutter\n    off the chest, and a bar landing on a fractional device row rendered 7px\n    thick on some frames and 8px on others — flicker on the brightest, longest\n    element on the panel. With a stop on every frame the drive ramp is stated\n    outright (1, 3, 6, 9, 11, 10, 8, 6, 3 device pixels: monotonically up,\n    then monotonically down) and every `translateY` on the bar is a whole\n    multiple of 0.125 units = one device pixel.\n\n    Three things carry the lift:\n\n    **The arms actually press, and the hands stay on the bar.** Each arm is its\n    base 2x2 rect on its own shoulder pivot — (2,10) and (13,10) — rotating up\n    while `scaleX` extends it along its own axis. Rotation plus axial scale is\n    a limb: 2 units thick throughout, 1.7 long at the rack and 5.7 at lockout.\n    That growth is the elbow, since this geometry has no elbow to bend.\n\n    Length is *derived from* angle, not chosen beside it: `L cos A` is held at\n    the constant 1.65, so the claw's far end sits at x = 0.35 on every frame of\n    the rep and only the angle changes. That constant is the whole fix for a\n    real defect — an independently chosen length let the hand swing outboard of\n    the plate on ten of twenty-four frames, up to a full art unit past the end\n    of the bar, and at true size those frames read as two orange fins flanking\n    the steel rather than as hands holding it. Holding the grip fixed keeps\n    the rotated claw's outer corner between 3.7 and 9.1 device pixels inside\n    the plate's outer edge on every frame, so the widest points of the sprite\n    are always the plates.\n\n    The angle tops out at 73deg rather than going vertical, and that is the\n    difference between a press and a shrug. The shoulder sits at the torso's\n    own edge, so an arm rotated to 90deg sweeps *inward* and vanishes into the\n    silhouette — the first attempt did exactly that and read as sloped\n    shoulders with a bar resting on his head.\n\n    **The bar tracks the claws by arithmetic, not by eye.** Solved the other\n    way round, in fact: the bar's travel is picked first, as whole device\n    pixels, and the arm angle for each frame falls out of it as\n    `tan A = (2.5 - 3 sy + d - tb) / (1.65 sy)` once the body's squash `sy` and\n    knee bend `d` have had their say. Contact is therefore exact — the claw's\n    far-end centre lands on the shaft's centre line to within 0.016 of a device\n    pixel on all 24 frames — instead of holding to a tolerance. Racked, the\n    shaft crosses the mid-torso and the plates overlap the claws; a prop that\n    never touches the character is not being held by it.\n\n    **The legs fold instead of standing still.** They are their own group\n    pivoting at the feet (7.5,15), compressing to 70% at the bottom of the dip.\n    The body's `translateY` is derived from that compression — leg top is\n    `15 - 2k`, body base is `13 + d`, so `d = 2 - 2k` — which is what keeps the\n    hips welded to the knees through a move that shortens both, and it is the\n    reason the torso can never lift off its own legs. The feet never leave\n    y=15.\n\n    Three seconds, not one. The bar travels 7.1 units — 57 device pixels — and\n    a sixteen-frame loop spent 27 of them in a single step, which reads as a\n    teleport rather than a lift. Twenty-four frames put the peak at 14 pixels,\n    on the descent, where gravity is doing the work.\n\n    The eyes are asymmetric, after upstream: the left stays at its base height\n    and the right rides up to 1.2 units higher for as long as the bar is in\n    front of the face. That split is load-bearing, not decoration — the shaft\n    is 1 unit thick and on one row it covered both eyes at once and left a\n    blank forehead. Separated, the shaft can only ever be in front of one of\n    them.\n\n    The other half of that invariant is height. A squashed eye is *shorter*\n    than the shaft, so an eye squinting under load while the bar crosses it is\n    not squashed, it is deleted — which happened, twice a rep, and read as a\n    hole in the face. So the eye the bar is about to cross opens instead: the\n    measured clear run of every eye on every frame is at least 3.6 device\n    pixels, and the squints are spent where the bar is at the chest and\n    crossing nothing. Both eyes squash about their own centres and neither\n    track touches opacity.\n\n    Every element below carries exactly one `animation` declaration in exactly\n    one rule. The shorthand replaces rather than merges, and idle once shipped\n    a part listed in two rules — it silently lost the first animation and hung\n    eight device pixels off the body. Where two motions are needed they nest in\n    groups (`#fx-barbell` travels, `#fx-barbell-strain` inside it shivers) so\n    the transforms compose instead of competing.\n\n    Loop: 3s at 8fps = 24 frames. Every stop is a multiple of 1/24, so each\n    lands on a frame boundary. The two opacity tracks only ever step between\n    stops exactly one frame apart, which is what keeps a half-alpha pixel — and\n    so a colour the artwork never declared — out of the palette snap.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* ── the load path ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\n      #body-group {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px; /* torso base — the hips */\n        animation: press-body 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #legs-group {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 15px; /* the floor, between his feet */\n        animation: press-legs 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #shoulder-left {\n        transform-origin: 2px 10px; /* left shoulder joint */\n        animation: press-arm-left 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #shoulder-right {\n        transform-origin: 13px 10px; /* right shoulder joint */\n        animation: press-arm-right 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #fx-barbell {\n        animation: press-bar 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* Nested inside the travelling bar so the shiver composes with the\n         travel. It translates rather than tilts: the shaft is 17 units by 1,\n         the largest slab on the panel, and a slab that size turned even a\n         degree and a half staircases its long edges — the exact failure the\n         palette snap does not fix. A whole-pixel tremble does the same job\n         and every edge stays straight. */\n      #fx-barbell-strain {\n        animation: press-bar-strain 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n\n      /* ── the face ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         Two tracks, not one, and they are not just squinting by different\n         amounts: they sit at different heights. The bar is 1 unit thick and\n         has to pass in front of the face twice a rep, and with both eyes on\n         the same row it covered both at once — a blank forehead with a girder\n         across it, which reads as a rendering fault rather than as a lift.\n         Clamped low and raised high, the shaft can only ever be over one of\n         them, so there is always a whole eye somewhere.\n\n         The second rule is subtler and cost a blocker to learn: an eye\n         squashed to a lid is *shorter* than the 1-unit shaft, so on the frame\n         the bar crosses it, a squinting eye is not squinting, it is gone. So\n         the eye the bar is about to reach opens rather than closes — which is\n         also what a face does when a loaded bar comes past it — and the\n         squints are spent at the rack and at lockout, where the shaft is\n         nowhere near the eye line. Every eye on every frame has at least 3.6\n         device pixels of unbroken clear run; the worst before this rule was\n         zero. Both squash about their own centres, on `linear`, because a lid\n         that accelerates reads as a wince. */\n\n      #left-eye {\n        transform-origin: 4.5px 9px; /* left eye centre */\n        animation: press-eye-left 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #right-eye {\n        transform-origin: 10.5px 9px; /* right eye centre */\n        animation: press-eye-right 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* Clawd's base geometry has no mouth. This is the additive grimace\n         upstream's yawn taught us — drawn only while he is under the bar, and\n         switched between stops one frame apart so it never renders at a half\n         alpha the palette snap would have to invent a colour for. */\n      #fx-grimace {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 11.5px;\n        animation: press-grimace 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* One drop, once per rep, and only on the way down. At lockout the bar\n         fills the whole band above his head and there is nowhere for a drop to\n         go that is not behind a plate. */\n      #fx-sweat {\n        animation: press-sweat 3s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* ── keyframes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         One stop per rendered frame on every track on the load path. Read any\n         one column down and it is the same rep, frame for frame. */\n\n      @keyframes press-body {\n        0%         { transform: translateY(0.02px) scale(1.015, 0.985); }\n        4.166667%  { transform: translateY(0.04px) scale(1.025, 0.975); }\n        8.333333%  { transform: translateY(0.28px) scale(1.055, 0.93); }\n        12.5%      { transform: translateY(0.58px) scale(1.075, 0.9); }\n        16.666667% { transform: translateY(0.42px) scale(1.055, 0.925); }\n        20.833333% { transform: translateY(0.26px) scale(1.03, 0.955); }\n        25%        { transform: translateY(0.15px) scale(1.012, 0.98); }\n        29.166667% { transform: translateY(0.07px) scale(1.002, 0.998); }\n        33.333333% { transform: translateY(0.01px) scale(0.99, 1.015); }\n        37.5%      { transform: translateY(-0.02px) scale(0.982, 1.03); }\n        41.666667% { transform: translateY(-0.044px) scale(0.978, 1.042); }\n        45.833333% { transform: translateY(-0.064px) scale(0.974, 1.052); }\n        50%        { transform: translateY(-0.08px) scale(0.97, 1.06); }\n        54.166667% { transform: translateY(-0.044px) scale(0.982, 1.046); }\n        58.333333% { transform: translateY(-0.072px) scale(0.972, 1.057); }\n        62.5%      { transform: translateY(-0.04px) scale(0.984, 1.044); }\n        66.666667% { transform: translateY(-0.06px) scale(0.976, 1.05); }\n        70.833333% { transform: translateY(-0.01px) scale(0.995, 1.032); }\n        75%        { transform: translateY(0.02px) scale(1.005, 1.015); }\n        79.166667% { transform: translateY(0.05px) scale(1.015, 0.998); }\n        83.333333% { transform: translateY(0.2px) scale(1.04, 0.958); }\n        87.5%      { transform: translateY(0.6px) scale(1.08, 0.905); }\n        91.666667% { transform: translateY(0.19px) scale(1.005, 0.995); }\n        95.833333% { transform: translateY(0.1px) scale(1.012, 0.978); }\n        100%       { transform: translateY(0.02px) scale(1.015, 0.985); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes press-legs {\n        0%         { transform: scale(1.015, 0.99); }\n        4.166667%  { transform: scale(1.025, 0.98); }\n        8.333333%  { transform: scale(1.055, 0.86); }\n        12.5%      { transform: scale(1.075, 0.71); }\n        16.666667% { transform: scale(1.055, 0.79); }\n        20.833333% { transform: scale(1.03, 0.87); }\n        25%        { transform: scale(1.012, 0.925); }\n        29.166667% { transform: scale(1.002, 0.965); }\n        33.333333% { transform: scale(0.99, 0.995); }\n        37.5%      { transform: scale(0.982, 1.01); }\n        41.666667% { transform: scale(0.978, 1.022); }\n        45.833333% { transform: scale(0.974, 1.032); }\n        50%        { transform: scale(0.97, 1.04); }\n        54.166667% { transform: scale(0.982, 1.022); }\n        58.333333% { transform: scale(0.972, 1.036); }\n        62.5%      { transform: scale(0.984, 1.02); }\n        66.666667% { transform: scale(0.976, 1.03); }\n        70.833333% { transform: scale(0.995, 1.005); }\n        75%        { transform: scale(1.005, 0.99); }\n        79.166667% { transform: scale(1.015, 0.975); }\n        83.333333% { transform: scale(1.04, 0.9); }\n        87.5%      { transform: scale(1.08, 0.7); }\n        91.666667% { transform: scale(1.005, 0.905); }\n        95.833333% { transform: scale(1.012, 0.95); }\n        100%       { transform: scale(1.015, 0.99); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes press-arm-left {\n        0%         { transform: rotate(-14.98deg) scaleX(0.854); }\n        4.166667%  { transform: rotate(-17.59deg) scaleX(0.865); }\n        8.333333%  { transform: rotate(-18.38deg) scaleX(0.869); }\n        12.5%      { transform: rotate(-13.99deg) scaleX(0.85); }\n        16.666667% { transform: rotate(-17.46deg) scaleX(0.865); }\n        20.833333% { transform: rotate(-12.7deg) scaleX(0.846); }\n        25%        { transform: rotate(7.4deg) scaleX(0.832); }\n        29.166667% { transform: rotate(36.1deg) scaleX(1.021); }\n        33.333333% { transform: rotate(55.81deg) scaleX(1.468); }\n        37.5%      { transform: rotate(64.97deg) scaleX(1.95); }\n        41.666667% { transform: rotate(69.42deg) scaleX(2.347); }\n        45.833333% { transform: rotate(71.8deg) scaleX(2.641); }\n        50%        { transform: rotate(72.7deg) scaleX(2.774); }\n        54.166667% { transform: rotate(73.13deg) scaleX(2.843); }\n        58.333333% { transform: rotate(72.79deg) scaleX(2.788); }\n        62.5%      { transform: rotate(73.19deg) scaleX(2.853); }\n        66.666667% { transform: rotate(70.58deg) scaleX(2.481); }\n        70.833333% { transform: rotate(67.04deg) scaleX(2.115); }\n        75%        { transform: rotate(58.43deg) scaleX(1.576); }\n        79.166667% { transform: rotate(35.65deg) scaleX(1.015); }\n        83.333333% { transform: rotate(-10.71deg) scaleX(0.84); }\n        87.5%      { transform: rotate(-13.74deg) scaleX(0.849); }\n        91.666667% { transform: rotate(-22.2deg) scaleX(0.891); }\n        95.833333% { transform: rotate(-15.88deg) scaleX(0.858); }\n        100%       { transform: rotate(-14.98deg) scaleX(0.854); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes press-arm-right {\n        0%         { transform: rotate(14.98deg) scaleX(0.854); }\n        4.166667%  { transform: rotate(17.59deg) scaleX(0.865); }\n        8.333333%  { transform: rotate(18.38deg) scaleX(0.869); }\n        12.5%      { transform: rotate(13.99deg) scaleX(0.85); }\n        16.666667% { transform: rotate(17.46deg) scaleX(0.865); }\n        20.833333% { transform: rotate(12.7deg) scaleX(0.846); }\n        25%        { transform: rotate(-7.4deg) scaleX(0.832); }\n        29.166667% { transform: rotate(-36.1deg) scaleX(1.021); }\n        33.333333% { transform: rotate(-55.81deg) scaleX(1.468); }\n        37.5%      { transform: rotate(-64.97deg) scaleX(1.95); }\n        41.666667% { transform: rotate(-69.42deg) scaleX(2.347); }\n        45.833333% { transform: rotate(-71.8deg) scaleX(2.641); }\n        50%        { transform: rotate(-72.7deg) scaleX(2.774); }\n        54.166667% { transform: rotate(-73.13deg) scaleX(2.843); }\n        58.333333% { transform: rotate(-72.79deg) scaleX(2.788); }\n        62.5%      { transform: rotate(-73.19deg) scaleX(2.853); }\n        66.666667% { transform: rotate(-70.58deg) scaleX(2.481); }\n        70.833333% { transform: rotate(-67.04deg) scaleX(2.115); }\n        75%        { transform: rotate(-58.43deg) scaleX(1.576); }\n        79.166667% { transform: rotate(-35.65deg) scaleX(1.015); }\n        83.333333% { transform: rotate(10.71deg) scaleX(0.84); }\n        87.5%      { transform: rotate(13.74deg) scaleX(0.849); }\n        91.666667% { transform: rotate(22.2deg) scaleX(0.891); }\n        95.833333% { transform: rotate(15.88deg) scaleX(0.858); }\n        100%       { transform: rotate(14.98deg) scaleX(0.854); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes press-bar {\n        0%         { transform: translateY(0px); }\n        4.166667%  { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        8.333333%  { transform: translateY(0.5px); }\n        12.5%      { transform: translateY(0.75px); }\n        16.666667% { transform: translateY(0.625px); }\n        20.833333% { transform: translateY(0.25px); }\n        25%        { transform: translateY(-0.5px); }\n        29.166667% { transform: translateY(-1.625px); }\n        33.333333% { transform: translateY(-3px); }\n        37.5%      { transform: translateY(-4.25px); }\n        41.666667% { transform: translateY(-5.25px); }\n        45.833333% { transform: translateY(-6px); }\n        50%        { transform: translateY(-6.375px); }\n        54.166667% { transform: translateY(-6.375px); }\n        58.333333% { transform: translateY(-6.375px); }\n        62.5%      { transform: translateY(-6.375px); }\n        66.666667% { transform: translateY(-5.625px); }\n        70.833333% { transform: translateY(-4.625px); }\n        75%        { transform: translateY(-3.25px); }\n        79.166667% { transform: translateY(-1.625px); }\n        83.333333% { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        87.5%      { transform: translateY(0.75px); }\n        91.666667% { transform: translateY(0.375px); }\n        95.833333% { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        100%       { transform: translateY(0px); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes press-eye-left {\n        0%         { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        4.166667%  { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        8.333333%  { transform: scaleY(0.9); }\n        12.5%      { transform: scaleY(0.75); }\n        16.666667% { transform: scaleY(0.7); }\n        20.833333% { transform: scaleY(0.9); }\n        25%        { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        29.166667% { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        33.333333% { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        37.5%      { transform: scaleY(0.8); }\n        41.666667% { transform: scaleY(0.6); }\n        45.833333% { transform: scaleY(0.45); }\n        50%        { transform: scaleY(0.35); }\n        54.166667% { transform: scaleY(0.4); }\n        58.333333% { transform: scaleY(0.3); }\n        62.5%      { transform: scaleY(0.4); }\n        66.666667% { transform: scaleY(0.6); }\n        70.833333% { transform: scaleY(0.85); }\n        75%        { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        79.166667% { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        83.333333% { transform: scaleY(0.95); }\n        87.5%      { transform: scaleY(0.25); }\n        91.666667% { transform: scaleY(0.5); }\n        95.833333% { transform: scaleY(0.8); }\n        100%       { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes press-eye-right {\n        0%         { transform: translateY(0px) scaleY(1); }\n        4.166667%  { transform: translateY(0px) scaleY(0.95); }\n        8.333333%  { transform: translateY(0px) scaleY(0.9); }\n        12.5%      { transform: translateY(-0.4px) scaleY(0.85); }\n        16.666667% { transform: translateY(-0.8px) scaleY(0.85); }\n        20.833333% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.9); }\n        25%        { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.95); }\n        29.166667% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(1); }\n        33.333333% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(1); }\n        37.5%      { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(1); }\n        41.666667% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.9); }\n        45.833333% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.8); }\n        50%        { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.7); }\n        54.166667% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.75); }\n        58.333333% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.65); }\n        62.5%      { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.75); }\n        66.666667% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.85); }\n        70.833333% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(0.95); }\n        75%        { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(1); }\n        79.166667% { transform: translateY(-1.2px) scaleY(1); }\n        83.333333% { transform: translateY(-0.6px) scaleY(0.9); }\n        87.5%      { transform: translateY(0px) scaleY(0.45); }\n        91.666667% { transform: translateY(0px) scaleY(0.7); }\n        95.833333% { transform: translateY(0px) scaleY(0.9); }\n        100%       { transform: translateY(0px) scaleY(1); }\n      }\n\n      /* The tremble of a bar held overhead by something running out of arms.\n         Active only across the hold, one whole device pixel either way, and\n         only between stops one frame apart, so it shivers rather than swings.\n         The claws hold their derived position through it, which is what makes\n         it read as the bar moving in his grip rather than his arms moving. */\n      @keyframes press-bar-strain {\n        0%, 50%          { transform: translateY(0px); }\n        54.166667%       { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        58.333333%       { transform: translateY(-0.125px); }\n        62.5%            { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        66.666667%, 100% { transform: translateY(0px); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes press-grimace {\n        0%, 20.833333% { opacity: 0; transform: scaleX(0.6); }\n        25%            { opacity: 1; transform: scaleX(1); }\n        50%            { opacity: 1; transform: scaleX(1.3); }\n        70.833333%     { opacity: 1; transform: scaleX(1); }\n        75%, 100%      { opacity: 0; transform: scaleX(0.6); }\n      }\n\n      /* A flick, not a lift. It leaves the brow sideways — 2 units out for\n         every 1 up, upstream's ratio — because a drop that mostly climbs is\n         the only thing moving on the last two frames of the loop, it is the\n         highest-contrast object on the panel, and the eye follows it up and\n         out of the frame instead of watching the bar come down. Travelling\n         outward it reads as thrown off him.\n\n         It leaves at f20, not f19. A frame earlier the bar is still crossing\n         the brow and the drop launches on top of the shaft, which reads as a\n         blob stuck to the steel rather than as sweat.\n\n         It is also gone by f23 rather than at 100%. A particle that pops out\n         on the last frame puts its only hard cut exactly on the loop's seam,\n         where it is the one thing that changes between the last frame and the\n         first and so is the one thing you notice. Retired a frame early, the\n         wrap has nothing in it but the settle. */\n      @keyframes press-sweat {\n        0%, 79.166667%   { transform: translate(0px, 0px) scale(0.6); opacity: 0; }\n        83.333333%       { transform: translate(0px, 0px) scale(0.6); opacity: 1; }\n        87.5%            { transform: translate(-1.5px, -0.75px) scale(1); opacity: 1; }\n        91.666667%       { transform: translate(-3.6px, -1.8px) scale(0.9); opacity: 1; }\n        95.833333%, 100% { transform: translate(-3.6px, -1.8px) scale(0.9); opacity: 0; }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg, regrouped, never redrawn ─── -->\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <!-- Planted. Out of the body group on purpose: these fold about the feet\n         while the body squashes about the hips, and the two meet exactly. -->\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"body-group\">\n      <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n\n        <g id=\"shoulder-left\">\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <g id=\"shoulder-right\">\n          <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n        <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n\n        <!-- additive: the gritted mouth, present only under load -->\n        <rect id=\"fx-grimace\" x=\"6\" y=\"11\" width=\"3\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- ── additive props ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->\n  <!-- Drawn last, so the shaft crosses the mid-torso at the rack and the\n       plates overlap the claws. That overlap is the contact the lift reads\n       from. Bright shaft, grey discs: both sit far enough off the body\n       colour's ramp to black that an antialiased body edge still snaps to\n       body or to background, never to steel. The inner collars at x=1.5 and\n       x=13 mark where a grip belongs, and the claws are held between them and\n       the plates on every frame of the rep. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-barbell\">\n    <g id=\"fx-barbell-strain\">\n      <rect x=\"-1\" y=\"10\" width=\"17\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n      <rect x=\"-1\" y=\"8.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"4\" fill=\"#8B949E\"/>\n      <rect x=\"14.5\" y=\"8.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"4\" fill=\"#8B949E\"/>\n      <rect x=\"1.5\" y=\"9.5\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#8B949E\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13\" y=\"9.5\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#8B949E\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- Sits on the brow, not above it: the lower rect straddles the top row of\n       the torso at the frame it appears, so it is a drop coming off him. -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(3.6, 4.4)\">\n    <g id=\"fx-sweat\" fill=\"#40C4FF\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"1\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"1.5\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0.25\" y=\"0\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","wizard":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"12\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: wizard (WebSearch, WebFetch)\n\n    Something is being called in from somewhere else, and it is arriving. Plan:\n    assets/clawd/animations/PLANS.md §Wizard.\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg. Every base element appears below at\n    its original coordinates and colours except `ground-shadow`, which\n    `docs/ANIMATION.md` §The base geometry forbids carrying. All motion,\n    including the eyes'\n    one-pixel look to the left, is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    ── Two drawn colours must not meet inside a scaling group ────────────\n\n    This is the rule the first two drafts broke, and it is checklist §7.\n\n    `#breathe` is a scale, so every edge inside it lands off the pixel grid and\n    Chromium antialiases it. `snapToPalette` then resolves the blend to the\n    nearest declared colour by squared RGB, and for a pale-on-dark prop that\n    nearest colour is the *body*: `#C9D1D9` over `#000000` at half coverage is\n    (100,104,108), which is 15,909 from `#DE886D` against 32,480 from black and\n    33,107 from the pale. **Peach wins by more than twice.** Measured on the\n    first bake: 81 of 96 frames carried peach inside the hat, and 87 had\n    transparent rows cutting it into floating bars.\n\n    So the hat, the orb and the motes all sit outside `#breathe` and move only\n    by whole device pixels. **Not one of them is static** — a hat that ignored\n    the breath was the first fix and it read as pasted on. What protects them is\n    the whole-pixel translate, not stillness: a whole pixel is never partially\n    covered, so there is nothing to antialias.\n\n    What may stay inside `#breathe` is geometry whose edges face the\n    background. Not because such edges are exempt from snapping — `BACKGROUND`\n    in `tools/frame-palette.ts` is `[0,0,0]` and is added to the palette itself,\n    so those edges are composited toward black and snapped like everything\n    else. It is safe *here* because this document declares three colours and\n    nothing sits between peach and black, so the ramp resolves to one or the\n    other. `payoff.svg` declares `#6F4436`, which does sit in that ramp and\n    takes 50 of its 101 steps. Count the ramp before trusting it.\n\n    ── Where the magic goes, and why it is above him ─────────────────────\n\n    Measured off `paintEnvironment` rather than recalled: the rock occupies\n    units x -2.25 to 1.00, **y 6.63 to 8.75**; the pool is x 11.25 to 17.50,\n    y 12.50 to 15.63; the horizon is at y 6.50. The first draft's orb sat at\n    x -2 to 1, y 6 to 9 — four dark cells on dark stone. A sprite-only review\n    cannot catch that, because the sprite is composited afterwards. **Render it\n    on the four grounds before believing the staging.**\n\n    The left flank is 3 units wide and the rock is most of it. The clear air\n    above him runs from the -4 crop line to the torso top at y 6, which is 10\n    units, so the claw is raised and the motes fall into it.\n\n    ── The hat ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Stepped rects, not a polygon: `svg2frames`'s safe-area walk is\n    `querySelectorAll('rect')`, so a `<polygon>` is invisible to the one gate\n    that checks the -4 crop line, which is the line a hat is most likely to\n    cross. `paintRock` is the worked example.\n\n    Widths run 7, 5.5, 4.5, 3.5, 2.5, 1.5 from the brim up, tapering on both\n    sides, with the centres running 7.5, 7.25, 7.25, 7.25, 6.75, 6.75 so the\n    tip leans left toward the arriving motes. Four of the five steps move both\n    edges; the fourth holds the left at 5.5 and moves only the right, which is\n    the one place it does what the rejected draft did everywhere. Two earlier drafts got this\n    wrong in opposite directions: one left-aligned every row at x 4, giving a\n    vertical wall and one slope, which rendered as a shark fin; the next put a\n    hard lateral kink in a five-row cone and it rendered as a blotched lump.\n    A clean monotonic taper reads as a mound, which is what the second draft\n    built and what a critic called it. Upstream clawd-tank's `hat-mishap`\n    reaches the same conclusion from the other side — its `#hat-cone` is 8\n    rects running 4-6-7-6-8-9-11-11, non-monotonic at the 7-6 step.\n\n    Six pale specks carry the two-tone survival through the cone, and the brim\n    carries it under them. A pale *band* across the cone lands on its widest\n    step and reads as icing on a stack. Upstream does the same thing with a\n    6-cell `#hat-stars` and a brim lighter than its cone.\n\n    The brim spans x 2 to 14, one unit proud of the torso on the right, so it\n    adds to the silhouette rather than tracing the body's own width. It does\n    not overhang on the left because the raised claw is there.\n\n    The apex sits at unit y -0.5, which is 3.5 units clear of the crop line at\n    rest and 4.125 at maximum bob.\n\n    ── Two tones, because one is invisible at some hour ──────────────────\n\n    Measured with `tools/contrast.ts` against the band a thing at head height\n    actually sits in: `#C9D1D9` is 1.01:1 against day sky low, and `#000000` is\n    1.55:1 against night sky low and 1.08:1 against night sky top. Whichever\n    single tone you pick it disappears at one time of day, so every added\n    element here is a dark mass with pale in it.\n\n    No new colour: only `#000000`, `#C9D1D9` and `#DE886D` are declared, all\n    already in the corpus.\n\n    ── The claw holds something ──────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    §What a claw cannot do wants a claw rotated *and* extended, pivoted at the\n    shoulder, and adds a third clause: give it something to hold. `rotate(65deg)\n    scaleX(2.4)` about the shoulder at (2,10) puts the claw tip corners at\n    (0.88, 5.23) and (-0.94, 6.07) — above the rock, and overlapping the orb.\n    The claw is inside `#breathe` and the orb is not, so the lower corner\n    drifts out past the orb's left edge by up to a device pixel as the body\n    scales; the corner is not the contact. §Props need contact is discharged by\n    measurement instead, against a variant with the orb deleted: the claw's\n    peach is under the orb on all 96 frames, 22 to 92 pixels of its 256.\n    Sharing an edge is not contact, and neither is a corner that happens to\n    land inside a box.\n\n    80 degrees was tried first and rendered as nothing at all. Near-vertical the\n    arm hugs the torso's own edge, and peach on peach has no silhouette.\n\n    ── Three motes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Each is `dizzy`'s five-cell plus — a dark cross with a pale core — at\n    half scale: 0.5-unit cells, so 1.5 units across and 80 device pixels of\n    area, against `dizzy`'s 320 at whole-unit cells.\n\n    **Half scale is the fix for the defect that survived two drafts.** At whole\n    units three crosses converging on a 2-unit orb cannot avoid each other: two\n    of them were edge-adjacent on 24 of 96 frames, rasterising as one glyph,\n    which is exactly what `componentSizes` in `tools/bake-sprites.test.ts`\n    exists to catch and what its `NEIGHBOURS` comment describes. Quartering the\n    area buys the room. Verified from the bake over all 96 frames and all three\n    pairs — not from the keyframes, because a keyframe model assumes a uniform\n    two-frame dwell and that is not what renders (below) — and all three on one\n    metric — empty device pixels between nearest\n    cells, measured from the bake rather than from the keyframes: the closest\n    any two motes come is **3**, on frames 6, 30, 54 and 78, and none comes\n    closer than 15 to the hat or 6 to the orb. An earlier version of this\n    sentence read 5/15/6, which is three numbers on three metrics: 15 and 6 are\n    empty-pixel gaps and 5 was a Euclidean nearest-pixel distance. The 3 is the\n    one with the least headroom over the `componentSizes` gate, so it is the\n    worst of the three to round up.\n\n    Twelve positions on a 3s track — `dizzy`'s cadence exactly, and that\n    includes the part `dizzy` had to write down. **The dwell is not two frames\n    a position**; measured, it is 2, 3, 1 repeating. `dizzy.svg` §Why nothing\n    rotates has the cause: the twelve keyframe percentages are written to six\n    decimals, `8.333333%` of 3s is 249.99999ms and lands just before frame 2's\n    sample while `16.666667%` is 500.00001ms and lands just after frame 4's, so\n    a boundary falls either side of a sample depending on which way the\n    percentage rounded. This file inherits those percentages, so it inherits\n    the dwell — and PLANS.md §Dizzy records that the even-spacing claim was\n    asserted once and retracted, with the retraction written only into the SVG.\n    Asserting it here again would have made that three times. The second draft used six positions of four frames, and a 9 pixel\n    jump every half second reads as hopping rather than flowing.\n\n    The delays are inline in the `animation` shorthand rather than separate\n    `animation-delay` longhands. `overheated` found that ordering trap: a\n    longhand below the shorthand is overridden, and three effects collapse onto\n    one phase while every gate stays green.\n\n    The first keyframe of each is transparent — the gap between one mote and the\n    next — so either two or three are on stage at any time. The last sits just\n    short of the orb rather than on it, which keeps every visible mote its own\n    connected component and lets the bake assert exactly that.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* One frame of the twelve second loop is 1.0416667%. */\n\n      /* `idle`'s breath, about the hips. The legs are a sibling of this group\n         so they stay planted — inside it the whole sprite bobs and the feet\n         sink into their own contact shadow. 2s alternating is six iterations\n         of a twelve second loop, which is even — an odd count would end the\n         loop at the far end of the cycle and seam at the wrap. Measured, frame\n         95 to frame 0 changes 203 pixels against a mean neighbour delta of\n         326, so the wrap is the smallest transition in the loop. */\n      #breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 2s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        from { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        to   { transform: scale(1.02, 0.98) translate(0, 0.5px); }\n      }\n\n      /* The summoning arm. Rotation clears the silhouette, axial scale makes\n         it long enough to be an arm — the pair `gym` established. Static pose:\n         this raise is the screen, and `frameAt` is wall-clock modulo, so a\n         raise at the top of the loop would be a pump at loop frequency\n         forever. */\n      #left-arm-joint {\n        transform-origin: 2px 10px;\n        transform: rotate(65deg) scaleX(2.4);\n      }\n\n      /* One art pixel left, toward the arriving motes — as a transform on the\n         base id, so the rects keep `base.svg`'s coordinates. `dizzy` moves its\n         eyes without moving its rects either, though by wrapping them in a\n         new group rather than transforming a base id. A whole unit is 8 device\n         pixels, so this cannot land off the grid. */\n      #eyes-color-group {\n        transform: translateX(-1px);\n      }\n\n      /* Squashed about the eye line, never hidden — §Closing an eye. Linear,\n         because a lid that accelerates reads as a wince. The four percentages\n         are frames 76 to 79 of 96. */\n      #eyes-blink {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes blink {\n        0%, 79.166667%          { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        80.208333%              { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        81.250000%              { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        82.291667%, 100%        { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n\n      /* The hat and the orb ride the breath, but in whole device pixels.\n\n         The values are the per-frame rounding of `#breathe`'s own ease-in-out\n         curve: the head's top travels 6.00 to 6.63 units, which is 5.04 device\n         pixels, so this is that motion sampled at 8fps and snapped to the grid\n         — which is what a moving sprite *should* rasterise to. Every\n         percentage is a multiple of 100/32, so each lands on a whole frame,\n         and 4s tiles the 12s loop exactly three times. `steps(1)` holds each\n         value rather than sliding between them. */\n      #fx-bob {\n        animation: bob 4s infinite steps(1);\n      }\n\n      @keyframes bob {\n        0.000000%  { transform: translate(0, 0px); }\n        12.500000% { transform: translate(0, 0.125px); }\n        21.875000% { transform: translate(0, 0.25px); }\n        25.000000% { transform: translate(0, 0.375px); }\n        31.250000% { transform: translate(0, 0.5px); }\n        40.625000% { transform: translate(0, 0.625px); }\n        62.500000% { transform: translate(0, 0.5px); }\n        71.875000% { transform: translate(0, 0.375px); }\n        78.125000% { transform: translate(0, 0.25px); }\n        81.250000% { transform: translate(0, 0.125px); }\n        90.625000% { transform: translate(0, 0px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Three motes, each on its own path, phased by inline delays. `steps(1)`\n         so every rendered position is a whole device pixel — §Render scale —\n         and so opacity is only ever 0 or 1. A fractional opacity would blend\n         the pale core toward peach exactly as a scaled edge does.\n\n         Three paths, not one: three things a third of a cycle apart on a\n         single straight line are evenly spaced collinear dots, which rendered\n         as a bead chain. These fan in from the left, from above and from the\n         right, converging as they fall — the horizontal spread closes from\n         5.0 units to 2.0. (5.375 is the spread at the 0% keyframe, where all\n         three are `opacity: 0`; 5.0 is the widest anything on screen shows.)\n\n         -1s and -2s are 8 and 16 frames, which is a third of the track each.\n         Four positions apart is what that authors; what renders drifts by one\n         either way, because the dwell is 2/3/1 rather than uniform — see the\n         note above. The useful consequence is the one thing a uniform dwell\n         would not give: the three never step on the same frame. */\n      #fx-mote-a { animation: arrive-a 3s infinite steps(1); }\n      #fx-mote-b { animation: arrive-b 3s -1s infinite steps(1); }\n      #fx-mote-c { animation: arrive-c 3s -2s infinite steps(1); }\n\n      @keyframes arrive-a {\n        0.000000%  { transform: translate(-2.375px, -2.5px); opacity: 0; }\n        8.333333%  { transform: translate(-2.25px, -2px); opacity: 1; }\n        16.666667%  { transform: translate(-2.125px, -1.625px); opacity: 1; }\n        25.000000%  { transform: translate(-2.125px, -1.125px); opacity: 1; }\n        33.333333%  { transform: translate(-2px, -0.625px); opacity: 1; }\n        41.666667%  { transform: translate(-1.875px, -0.25px); opacity: 1; }\n        50.000000%  { transform: translate(-1.75px, 0.25px); opacity: 1; }\n        58.333333%  { transform: translate(-1.625px, 0.625px); opacity: 1; }\n        66.666667%  { transform: translate(-1.5px, 1.125px); opacity: 1; }\n        75.000000%  { transform: translate(-1.5px, 1.625px); opacity: 1; }\n        83.333333%  { transform: translate(-1.375px, 2px); opacity: 1; }\n        91.666667%  { transform: translate(-1.25px, 2.5px); opacity: 1; }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes arrive-b {\n        0.000000%  { transform: translate(0.75px, -2.75px); opacity: 0; }\n        8.333333%  { transform: translate(0.625px, -2.25px); opacity: 1; }\n        16.666667%  { transform: translate(0.625px, -1.875px); opacity: 1; }\n        25.000000%  { transform: translate(0.5px, -1.375px); opacity: 1; }\n        33.333333%  { transform: translate(0.375px, -0.875px); opacity: 1; }\n        41.666667%  { transform: translate(0.25px, -0.5px); opacity: 1; }\n        50.000000%  { transform: translate(0.25px, 0px); opacity: 1; }\n        58.333333%  { transform: translate(0.125px, 0.375px); opacity: 1; }\n        66.666667%  { transform: translate(0px, 0.875px); opacity: 1; }\n        75.000000%  { transform: translate(-0.125px, 1.375px); opacity: 1; }\n        83.333333%  { transform: translate(-0.125px, 1.75px); opacity: 1; }\n        91.666667%  { transform: translate(-0.25px, 2.25px); opacity: 1; }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes arrive-c {\n        0.000000%  { transform: translate(3px, -2.25px); opacity: 0; }\n        8.333333%  { transform: translate(2.75px, -1.875px); opacity: 1; }\n        16.666667%  { transform: translate(2.625px, -1.375px); opacity: 1; }\n        25.000000%  { transform: translate(2.375px, -1px); opacity: 1; }\n        33.333333%  { transform: translate(2.125px, -0.5px); opacity: 1; }\n        41.666667%  { transform: translate(2px, -0.125px); opacity: 1; }\n        50.000000%  { transform: translate(1.75px, 0.375px); opacity: 1; }\n        58.333333%  { transform: translate(1.625px, 0.75px); opacity: 1; }\n        66.666667%  { transform: translate(1.375px, 1.25px); opacity: 1; }\n        75.000000%  { transform: translate(1.125px, 1.625px); opacity: 1; }\n        83.333333%  { transform: translate(1px, 2.125px); opacity: 1; }\n        91.666667%  { transform: translate(0.75px, 2.5px); opacity: 1; }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"breathe\">\n      <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n        <g id=\"left-arm-joint\">\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"eyes-blink\">\n        <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n          <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <!-- The hat and what the claw holds. Outside `#breathe` and on whole\n         device pixels, so their pale-on-dark boundaries never antialias;\n         drawn after the body so the hat sits on the skull and the orb sits in\n         front of the claw. Both ride `#fx-bob`, so they breathe with him. -->\n    <g id=\"fx-bob\">\n      <g id=\"fx-hat\">\n        <g id=\"fx-hat-cone\" fill=\"#000000\">\n          <rect id=\"fx-hat-r0\" x=\"4\" y=\"4.5\" width=\"7\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect id=\"fx-hat-r1\" x=\"4.5\" y=\"3.5\" width=\"5.5\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect id=\"fx-hat-r2\" x=\"5\" y=\"2.5\" width=\"4.5\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect id=\"fx-hat-r3\" x=\"5.5\" y=\"1.5\" width=\"3.5\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect id=\"fx-hat-r4\" x=\"5.5\" y=\"0.5\" width=\"2.5\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect id=\"fx-hat-r5\" x=\"6\" y=\"-0.5\" width=\"1.5\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <!-- Six specks, so the cone carries at night without a band cutting\n             it in half. Every one sits a quarter unit clear of its row's edges\n             and of every other speck: a speck flush with the cone's edge is not\n             a speck, it is a notch in the silhouette, and one flush with the\n             brim welds into it and becomes part of the band. Both were shipped\n             once and caught by a critic. -->\n        <g id=\"fx-hat-specks\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\">\n          <rect x=\"5\" y=\"4.75\" width=\"0.75\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"9\" y=\"4.75\" width=\"0.75\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"7\" y=\"3.75\" width=\"0.75\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"5.5\" y=\"2.75\" width=\"0.75\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"8\" y=\"2.75\" width=\"0.75\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"6.75\" y=\"1.75\" width=\"0.75\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <!-- The brim. Pale across its whole width, overhanging the torso's\n             right edge by a unit.\n\n             The overhang is what makes this read as a brim rather than as the\n             head's own outline, and it has to be pale. A dark end-cap was\n             tried, to keep the overhang off the horizon, and a critic sent it\n             back: the cone's base is at x 4, two units inboard and a row up,\n             so a dark block at x 13 has no dark mass to bind to and reads as a\n             speck floating beside his head — and against night sky it is\n             1.55:1 and simply gone, so the silhouette it was protecting\n             existed on one ground of four. Upstream `hat-mishap`'s brim\n             overhangs by three units each side in one flat colour, and never\n             changes colour at the overhang.\n\n             So the horizon overlap is accepted rather than fixed, and this is\n             what that costs: `paintEnvironment` puts the horizon at unit y\n             6.50 and this brim spans 5.5 to 6.5, riding to 7.125 on the bob,\n             so its bottom row is flush with the sea's first row on 21 of 96\n             frames and covers it on 18 more. Judged at true size, it does not\n             read as a problem.\n\n             **Moving the brim would not fix it anyway.** The torso's own top\n             edge travels 6.00 to 6.63 as he breathes, so it crosses 6.50 by\n             itself; anything sitting on the head is at the horizon for part of\n             the loop. -->\n        <rect id=\"fx-hat-brim\" x=\"2\" y=\"5.5\" width=\"12\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <!-- A dark ring around a pale core: one object rather than a larger\n           mote, so a mote arriving at it is an event. -->\n      <g id=\"fx-orb\">\n        <g id=\"fx-orb-ring\" fill=\"#000000\">\n          <rect x=\"-1\" y=\"4.25\" width=\"2\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-1\" y=\"5.75\" width=\"2\" height=\"0.5\"/>\n          <rect x=\"-1\" y=\"4.75\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect x=\"0.5\" y=\"4.75\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <rect id=\"fx-orb-core\" x=\"-0.5\" y=\"4.75\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <!-- Three motes, authored at the origin so each path translates the same\n         geometry; the paths are in the stylesheet. -->\n    <g id=\"fx-mote-a\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"-0.5\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"-0.5\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0.5\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0.5\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-mote-b\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"-0.5\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"-0.5\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0.5\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0.5\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-mote-c\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"-0.5\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"-0.5\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0.5\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0.5\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","board-game":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"2\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: board game (Agent)\n\n    He is not doing the work; several small things are doing it for him. Plan:\n    assets/clawd/animations/PLANS.md §Board game.\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg. Every base element appears below at\n    its original coordinates and colours except `ground-shadow`, which\n    `docs/ANIMATION.md` §The base geometry forbids carrying. All motion,\n    including the eyes'\n    look down and left, is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    ── Two seconds, and why not four ─────────────────────────────────────\n\n    This screen is a punctuation mark, not a scene. Keyed on `PreToolUse` for\n    `Agent`, it is replaced the moment the subagent makes its own first tool\n    call — and those arrive on the *parent's* session, because sidechains do not\n    get their own id. Measured over 167 subagent runs: median 3.2s to that first\n    call, p90 4.3s, max 9.6s. Against that window:\n\n      2s loop (16 frames)  seen whole on 167 of 167\n      3s                                 110 of 167\n      4s                                  29 of 167\n     12s                                   0 of 167\n\n    So two seconds, and the screen says *handed off* before `gym` or\n    `bouldering` take the stage back to report what the subagent is doing.\n\n    ── The breath is half amplitude, and that is not a shortcut ──────────\n\n    Six of the nine animations that breathe use `2s alternate`, which is a\n    **four** second full cycle — in for two, out for two. On a two second loop\n    that ends the wrap at the far end of the cycle, and seams once per loop:\n    every two seconds, which §Timing is explicit about. So the breath here is\n    one full cycle in 2s with its own keyframes and no `alternate`.\n\n    That form is not novel — `asleep` and `thinking` already write the breath as\n    explicit `0%/50%/100%` keyframes without `alternate`, at 4s. What is new\n    here is only the amplitude.\n\n    That is twice the corpus rate, so the amplitude is halved to match its\n    *velocity* rather than its numbers: the corpus moves 0.02 of scale across\n    2s, and 0.01 across 1s is the same 0.01 a second. A faster breath at full\n    amplitude reads as panting; this reads as the same crab.\n\n    ── The board ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Left flank at ground level, which is the only flank free there:\n    `paintEnvironment` puts the pool at units x 11.25 to 17.50, y 12.50 to\n    15.63, so anything at ground level on the right is in the water. The rock is\n    on this side but at y 6.63 to 8.75 — mid-distance, well above a ground-level\n    prop.\n\n    **It overlaps the outer left leg, and that is the contact.** §Props need\n    contact wants a prop that touches, and this screen deliberately has no claw\n    raise — it is the one working screen where he does not act — so the legs\n    have to do it. Half a unit of overlap, which covers four of the outer left\n    leg's eight columns over its full height on every frame. It reads as the\n    board standing in front of the near foot, which is what a prop on the ground\n    in front of him should do.\n\n    Six and a quarter units wide and two tall — **wider than the payoff's\n    vehicle body, which is five**, and the plan expected the opposite. It said\n    to measure rather than inherit five units because a flat slab with pieces\n    \"may read at 3 or 4\". Measured, it read at neither: the first draft was four\n    and a half by one and rendered as a smudge on the sand. A board seen from a\n    desk is a surface with a thickness, and one row has no thickness — so the\n    height went to two and the width followed, to keep the mover a whole unit\n    clear of the still piece at both ends. The plan's instinct to measure was\n    right; its guess at the answer was low.\n\n    Board and pieces sit outside `#breathe`. Anything with two colours inside a\n    scaling group fringes to the body colour — checklist §7, and the `wizard`\n    hat's 81 of 96 frames.\n\n    ── Two tones, because one is invisible at some hour ──────────────────\n\n    Measured with `tools/contrast.ts` against sand, which is what this prop\n    actually sits on: `#000000` is 4.21 / 8.19 / 3.28 / **1.61** at dawn, day,\n    dusk and night, and `#C9D1D9` is 3.32 / **1.71** / 4.27 / 8.68. So the **pale\n    top** carries at night, where it is 8.68:1 against sand and the dark parts\n    are 1.61:1; and the **dark edge and pieces** carry by day, where they are\n    8.19:1 and the pale top is 1.71:1. Either alone disappears at some hour,\n    which is why it is both. (An earlier draft of this paragraph had the tones\n    the wrong way round, and named pale pieces this file does not have.)\n\n    No new colour: only `#000000`, `#C9D1D9` and `#DE886D`, all already in the\n    corpus.\n\n    ── One move, and no blink ────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    The piece steps one cell at frame 8 and back at the wrap. At sixteen frames\n    that is the whole beat and it has to be a single legible event, so it is a\n    whole art pixel under `steps(1)` — nothing continuous, nothing sub-unit.\n\n    No blink, which reverses the plan's earlier note. That note argued from\n    `confused`'s 26 distinct frames of 96 that a screen this calm needs one; at\n    sixteen frames a blink is three or four of them and lands as a twitch. The\n    motion budget belongs to the pieces.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* One frame of the two second loop is 6.25%. */\n\n      /* `idle`'s breath at half amplitude on a full 2s cycle — see the note\n         above on why it is not `2s alternate`. Legs are a sibling of this\n         group so they stay planted. */\n      #breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 2s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        0%, 100% { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        50%      { transform: scale(1.01, 0.99) translate(0, 0.25px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Down and left, onto the board, and **static**.\n\n         On its own wrapper, named for `idle`'s `#fx-eyes-look`. Not on\n         `#eyes-color-group`, whose documented job is carrying the eye fill —\n         though note `wizard` puts a transform straight onto that group, so\n         this is a preference the corpus does not hold uniformly — and not on `#eyes-blink`, where an\n         `animation` added later would silently replace a transform, which is\n         a real hazard on a file that declares it has no blink.\n\n         A draft stepped this one unit on the piece's beat, on the theory that a\n         static gaze is only legible against a rest pose the screen never shows.\n         Both halves of that were wrong. The eyes are 8x16 device pixels each\n         and moving them repaints 512 pixels against the piece's 128, so the\n         subject of the beat became his face at four to one — on a screen whose\n         whole premise is that he is *not* the one doing the work. And the rest\n         position had no x offset, so for eight of sixteen frames he was looking\n         straight ahead: half the loop of a watching animation, not watching.\n\n         Static reads on a single frame without needing a comparison, because\n         the head is symmetric and the offset is not: the left eye sits 8 device\n         pixels from the torso edge against the right eye's 24. */\n      #fx-eyes-look {\n        transform: translate(-1px, 1px);\n      }\n\n      /* The piece that moves. One cell left at frame 8, back at the wrap:\n         `steps(1)` so it is never between cells, and a whole unit so it is\n         never between pixels. The `100%` is written out rather than left to\n         the implicit keyframe, which would take the element's underlying\n         `transform: none` — identical under `steps(1)`, but said out loud. */\n      #fx-piece-moving {\n        animation: nudge 2s infinite steps(1);\n      }\n\n      @keyframes nudge {\n        0%   { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        50%  { transform: translate(-1px, 0); }\n        100% { transform: translate(-1px, 0); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"breathe\">\n      <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n        <g id=\"left-arm-joint\">\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"eyes-blink\">\n        <g id=\"fx-eyes-look\">\n          <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n            <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <!-- The board, drawn after the legs so it reads as nearer the viewer, and\n         overlapping the outer left leg by half a unit — which is the contact\n         this screen has instead of a claw.\n\n         Three parts, because one flat slab rendered as a smudge on the sand:\n         a pale playing surface, a dark front edge under it that gives the slab\n         depth, and dark pieces standing on the surface rather than lying in\n         it. That also fixes the contrast both ways — the pale top is 1.71:1\n         against day sand and would vanish alone, and the dark edge and pieces\n         are 1.61:1 against night sand and would vanish alone. Together one of\n         them always carries. -->\n    <g id=\"fx-board\">\n      <rect id=\"fx-board-top\" x=\"-2.75\" y=\"13\" width=\"6.25\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n      <rect id=\"fx-board-edge\" x=\"-2.75\" y=\"14\" width=\"6.25\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <!-- The mover steps *away* from the still piece. Stepping toward it, it\n           landed sharing an edge at x 0.5, and two rects of one fill sharing an\n           edge are one silhouette — §Articulation, and the same defect\n           `wizard`'s motes had. Rendered, frames 8-15 were a single 16px bar\n           where frames 0-7 had two 8px marks: for half the loop there was one\n           thing on a board whose whole point is that several things move.\n\n           The cells are constrained on the right, not the left: the board is\n           drawn after `#breathe`, and the torso reaches x 1.945 mid-breath, so\n           a piece further right is drawn over it as a black bite. The still\n           piece's right edge is 1.5, clearing that by 3.6 device pixels. -->\n      <g id=\"fx-pieces\" fill=\"#000000\">\n        <rect id=\"fx-piece-still\" x=\"0.5\" y=\"12\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <g id=\"fx-piece-moving\">\n          <rect x=\"-1.5\" y=\"12\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","thinking":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"8\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: thinking (UserPromptSubmit)\n\n    Claude has been handed a prompt and is working out what to do with it. The\n    read is deliberation, not effort: everything here is slow, and nothing is\n    urgent. Typing is the animation that hurries.\n\n    Structured after upstream clawd-tank's clawd-working-thinking.svg, which\n    solves the same scene with the same four parts: a slow contemplative lean,\n    one claw brought up beside the head, slow thoughtful blinks, and loading\n    dots filling in sequence.\n\n    Eight seconds, 64 frames. Four tracks at three periods, nested so their\n    transforms compose rather than fight:\n\n      sway 8s      leans left, through centre, leans right — never held\n      breathe 4s   two breaths a loop, on the torso base\n      tap 4s       two taps then a long pause, twice a loop\n      dots 2s      one, two, three, blank\n\n    Every period divides eight exactly, so the loop closes without a jump. And\n    every keyframe below lands on a whole frame — a multiple of 1.5625% on the\n    eight second tracks, 3.125% on the four second ones. That is not tidiness.\n    It is what lets a pose change happen between two adjacent frames with no\n    frame ever sampling the tween, which is the whole of how the tap works.\n\n    ── Why the lean is a translation and the legs are planted ─────────────\n\n    The version of this file that was reviewed leaned by rotating the whole\n    body three degrees about a floor pivot. That was measurably wrong, and the\n    numbers are worth keeping because the mistake is an attractive one.\n\n    The torso is a single 11x7 rect — 88x56 device pixels. The palette snap in\n    tools/svg2frames.ts removes intermediate *colours*; it does nothing about\n    sub-pixel *geometry*. A three degree diagonal across 88 pixels therefore\n    resolves to a staircase: three to five one-pixel steps, spread four to five\n    pixels across the top edge, on 49 of the 64 frames. At true size that reads\n    as a corrupted sprite rather than a leaning one, and it is the same defect\n    docs/ANIMATION.md §Scale by size records from `idle`.\n\n    Rotating the legs along with it tilted the foot line as well. The pivot sat\n    on the feet rather than under them, so a lean raised two feet and buried\n    the other two — four pixels of stagger across a floor that has to be flat.\n\n    So the body translates: three quarters of a unit each way, six device\n    pixels, held at each extreme. The legs sit outside the lean entirely and\n    stay planted, which is also what `idle` does. Every edge stays axis\n    aligned. Measured across all 64 frames, the torso's top edge is one flat\n    row and all four feet share one row — both were true on 15 of 64 before.\n\n    A quarter of a unit, passed through rather than held. Both halves of that\n    matter and the first version got both wrong.\n\n    The amount was three quarters of a unit — six device pixels — chosen\n    because the outer leg still had a quarter unit of body over it at that\n    offset, so nothing was standing off the edge. That reasoning is sound and\n    the conclusion was still wrong: what reads is not whether a leg has left\n    the body, it is how far the body's centre has left the feet. Six pixels is\n    most of the gap between the inner legs, and at that offset the legs look\n    bolted on askew rather than the body looking leant.\n\n    The holds made it worse than the amount did. The lean sat at each extreme\n    for six frames, and the eased approach parked several more nearby, so the\n    off-centre pose was on screen for 34 of the 64 frames. `idle` uses a\n    similar amplitude and does not read as broken, because it passes through\n    the extreme instead of sitting in it — 29 of 128 frames against more than\n    half. A lean is a moment, not a pose.\n\n    ── Why the breath compresses rather than lifts ────────────────────────\n\n    Breathing pivots on the torso base at (7.5,13) — the pivot the table in\n    docs/ANIMATION.md assigns to it — and compresses: two device pixels wider\n    for four shorter, then a pixel of sink. Volume roughly holds.\n\n    The direction matters more than the amount. A breath that *lifts* the\n    torso hauls it off the legs, and since the legs are planted they are left\n    as four free-floating stubs; the reviewed version opened up to eleven\n    device pixels of daylight under the hips on 50 of 64 frames. Compressing\n    onto a base pivot cannot do that: the bottom edge is the pivot, so it does\n    not move at all, and the extra sink pushes the hips a pixel further into\n    the legs. Measured across all 64 frames, no transparent row separates any\n    leg from the body.\n\n    ── Why neither claw rotates ───────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Neither claw rotates. Both drift a pixel or two vertically, a beat apart\n    from each other and a beat behind the body.\n\n    The reviewed version swung the right claw 45 degrees to tap the chin, and\n    the mechanics of it were sound. It held exactly two poses — a square at\n    rest and a diamond at 45 degrees, the only two angles a 2x2 block can hold\n    without staircasing — and each pose change was placed between two adjacent\n    frames, so the rasteriser sampled one or the other and never anything in\n    between. A shoulder rect tucked under the torso kept the joint from opening\n    as the corner swung clear. All of that worked.\n\n    The pose was still wrong. A 2x2 block turned 45 degrees is an arrowhead,\n    and hung off the side of an 11x7 slab it reads as a spike rather than a\n    hand. Measured at the peak of the swing, the claw tip sat fourteen device\n    pixels outside the torso's right edge and about thirty to the right of the\n    eye — it never came near the face, so what the loop actually showed was a\n    fin flicking beside the body twice a cycle. Correct mechanics executing the\n    wrong idea.\n\n    The bubble is what says \"thinking\", and it says it on its own: a tail\n    floating clear of his head and three dots filling in sequence. That was the\n    whole reason for choosing a bubble over the cogs it replaced. The claws did\n    not need to add anything, and when they tried, they subtracted.\n\n    The shoulder rects stay. They cost nothing, they change no silhouette, and\n    they keep the body a single connected component — verified by a\n    connected-component pass over all 64 frames, which finds one piece with\n    exactly two interior holes, the eyes.\n\n    ── Blinks ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Blinks squash the eye about its own centre rather than hiding it. A hidden\n    eye leaves a hole in the face and reads as a dropped frame. scaleY(0.15) on\n    a 16 pixel eye lands on two device rows reliably; scaleY(0.1) is 1.6 and\n    lands on one or two depending on where the eye happens to be, which is a\n    lid that flickers.\n\n    Linear, not eased: a lid that accelerates reads as a wince. And the two\n    blinks in the loop are now the same blink — two frames fully shut each,\n    with one frame of travel either side, placed on frame boundaries so no\n    frame samples a half-closed lid. The reviewed version held the first shut\n    for three frames and flicked the second through in two, which is a tic on\n    one eye-pass and a doze on the other.\n\n    ── The bubble and the dots ────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Why the bubble does not move. Everything else here is eased, and the\n    rasteriser snaps the antialiased edges that produces back to the palette\n    afterwards. That works because a half covered edge of the body sits nearer\n    black than anything else in the palette, so it resolves to one side or the\n    other. A half covered edge of the pale bubble does not: it sits nearer the\n    body orange than to either black or its own grey, so a bubble moved by a\n    fraction of a pixel grows an orange fringe. It stays on the grid, and the\n    dots carry its motion.\n\n    The dots are #40C4FF for the same reason and it is worth stating plainly,\n    because the obvious choice is wrong. They used to be #30363B, the dark grey\n    the laptop screen uses. A mid grey in the palette is a trap once anything\n    moves off the grid: every body edge between a fifth and three fifths\n    covered lands nearer that grey than to either colour it is actually\n    between, so the whole character picks up a dark outline that flickers as\n    the edges move. A colour far off the line between black and the body\n    cannot do that.\n\n    The bubble floats clear of his head and always did. It is a symbolic\n    overlay rather than a held prop, so docs/ANIMATION.md §Props need contact\n    does not apply to it; attached, it read as a lump growing out of his head.\n    The tail bottom sits on row 103 of every frame and the top of his head on\n    row 120, so the gap is a flat seventeen device pixels — two art pixels and\n    a device pixel — where it used to close to eight at the top of a breath\n    that lifted. It is a wider float than before because the body no longer\n    rises into it; the dots descending toward the crown are what connect them.\n\n    There is no shadow in this file, and there is none in any of the other\n    seven either. `#ground-shadow` used to be here, inherited from base.svg as\n    black at half opacity, animating and emitting nothing: over a black stage\n    it composites to black and the snap turns it transparent, so a palette\n    census over all 64 frames found #DE886D, #C9D1D9 and #40C4FF and no fourth\n    colour. It was kept anyway, on the theory that a pack setting a lighter\n    stage would get a shadow tracking the body.\n\n    That path was never taken, and the rock pool is what made it moot: the\n    renderer draws the shadow now, at these same coordinates, in a tone that\n    knows which of the four grounds it is falling on. See `paintContactShadow`\n    in packages/renderer/src/environment.ts.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* One `animation` declaration per element, and one element per job.\n         The shorthand replaces rather than merges, so an element given two\n         declarations silently keeps only the last. Nesting groups avoids the\n         question entirely, which is why there are more group wrappers here\n         than there are moving parts.\n\n         Angle brackets are banned from this block, comments included. Inside\n         SVG the HTML parser does not treat `style` as raw text, so a literal\n         tag name in a CSS comment opens a real element and silently discards\n         every rule after it. It rasterises to 64 identical frames. */\n\n      /* Slow lean, left then right, with a hold at each extreme. The holds are\n         what make it deliberation rather than a metronome. A translation, not\n         a rotation, and it carries the body only — see the header. */\n      #sway {\n        animation: sway 8s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* Two breaths a loop, on the torso base so the hips cannot leave the\n         legs. Wider as it shortens: the volume has to look conserved. */\n      #breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 4s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* The idle claw. It drifts vertically, a beat behind the body. No\n         rotation: 16 device pixels square has no shallow angle it can hold. */\n      #left-arm-joint {\n        animation: left-claw-drift 8s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* The thinking claw: up beside the face, twice, then holding. */\n      #right-arm-joint {\n        transform-origin: 13px 10px;\n        animation: tap-face 4s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* Up at the bubble, with one drift away and back. Never at the viewer. */\n      #eyes-look {\n        animation: look-up 8s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* Squashed about the eye line, never hidden, and linear because a lid\n         that accelerates reads as a wince. */\n      #eyes-blink {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 8s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n\n      /* steps(1) is right here and wrong everywhere else in this file. These\n         are discrete state changes, not motion: a dot is lit or it is not, and\n         a fractional opacity would blend the dot into the bubble and snap to\n         whichever it happened to be nearer. */\n      #dot-1 { animation: dot-1 2s infinite steps(1); }\n      #dot-2 { animation: dot-2 2s infinite steps(1); }\n      #dot-3 { animation: dot-3 2s infinite steps(1); }\n\n      /* 0.25px is two device pixels, 0.125px is one — both whole, so every\n         position in the sweep is as crisp as the upright. No holds: the lean\n         is passed through, never sat in. See the header. */\n      @keyframes sway {\n        0%         { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        20.3125%   { transform: translate(-0.25px, 0.125px); }\n        50%        { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        70.3125%   { transform: translate(0.25px, 0.125px); }\n        100%       { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      }\n\n      /* 1.0227 x 0.9643 about (7.5,13) puts the torso's edges on 39, 129 and\n         123 device pixels against 40, 128 and 120 at rest: a pixel wider each\n         side, three shorter, and the base pinned. The translate is outside the\n         scale, so the sink is exactly one device pixel. */\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        0%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0) scale(1, 1); }\n        50%      { transform: translate(0, 0.125px) scale(1.0227, 0.9643); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes left-claw-drift {\n        0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }\n        28.125%  { transform: translateY(-0.25px); }\n        68.75%   { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Square, diamond, square. Each change spans exactly one frame of the\n         32 in this track, so no frame lands between the two poses. */\n      /* A drift, not a tap. See the header: the tap was a 45-degree swing and\n         it rendered as an arrowhead spiking out of his side, never within\n         14 device pixels of the face it was supposed to reach. Offset from\n         the left claw so the two are never quite in step. */\n      @keyframes tap-face {\n        0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }\n        34.375%  { transform: translateY(0.125px); }\n        71.875%  { transform: translateY(-0.25px); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes look-up {\n        0%, 29.6875%       { transform: translate(0.5px, -1px); }\n        45.3125%, 60.9375% { transform: translate(-0.5px, -1.25px); }\n        79.6875%, 100%     { transform: translate(0.5px, -1px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Two frames fully shut per blink, one frame of travel either side, and\n         both blinks identical. Every value below is a whole frame of the 64. */\n      @keyframes blink {\n        0%, 20.3125%      { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        21.875%, 23.4375% { transform: scaleY(0.15); }\n        25%, 64.0625%     { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        65.625%, 67.1875% { transform: scaleY(0.15); }\n        68.75%, 100%      { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes dot-1 {\n        0%   { opacity: 1; }\n        75%  { opacity: 0; }\n        100% { opacity: 0; }\n      }\n      @keyframes dot-2 {\n        0%   { opacity: 0; }\n        25%  { opacity: 1; }\n        75%  { opacity: 0; }\n        100% { opacity: 0; }\n      }\n      @keyframes dot-3 {\n        0%   { opacity: 0; }\n        50%  { opacity: 1; }\n        75%  { opacity: 0; }\n        100% { opacity: 0; }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── the thought bubble, above and right of his head ───────────────── -->\n  <!-- Nine wide by five tall with the corners cut, and a two step tail\n       descending toward the crown. The top edge sits at y=-3, a unit below the\n       landscape crop line at y=-4, so there is room to breathe above it. The\n       tail stops at y=4 and the torso tops out at y=6. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-bubble\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\">\n    <rect x=\"7\" y=\"-3\" width=\"7\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <rect x=\"6\" y=\"-2\" width=\"9\" height=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"7\" y=\"1\" width=\"7\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <rect id=\"tail-large\" x=\"7\" y=\"2\" width=\"2\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <rect id=\"tail-small\" x=\"6\" y=\"3\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <g id=\"fx-bubble-dots\" fill=\"#40C4FF\">\n    <rect id=\"dot-1\" x=\"8\" y=\"-1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <rect id=\"dot-2\" x=\"10\" y=\"-1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <rect id=\"dot-3\" x=\"12\" y=\"-1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg, regrouped not redrawn ───── -->\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <!-- Planted. Outside the lean and outside the breath on purpose — the\n         first keeps the foot line flat, the second is what makes the breath\n         read as a breath rather than as a sprite being nudged. -->\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"sway\">\n      <g id=\"breathe\">\n        <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n          <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n\n          <g id=\"left-arm-joint\">\n            <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <rect id=\"left-shoulder\" x=\"2\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n\n          <!-- Drawn after the torso so the raised claw overlaps its edge and\n               stays attached to the shoulder through the whole swing. -->\n          <g id=\"right-arm-joint\">\n            <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <rect id=\"right-shoulder\" x=\"11\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n\n        <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n          <g id=\"eyes-look\">\n            <g id=\"eyes-blink\">\n              <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n              <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <\/g>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","permission-sign":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"8\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: permission sign (NEEDS_PERMISSION)\n\n    Claude has asked for approval and cannot proceed without it. Clawd holds a\n    sign up and waits.\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg. Every base element appears below\n    at its original coordinates and colours; all motion is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    ── The arm, and why rotation alone could not do this ──────────────────\n\n    A 2x2 claw pivoting at the shoulder cannot lift its top edge above y=7.76.\n    The pivot is at y=10 and the furthest corner is sqrt(5) away, so the torso\n    top at y=6 is out of reach by 1.76 units, always — a sign held there would\n    overlap the body, which is the lump `docs/ANIMATION.md` §What a claw cannot\n    do exists to forbid, arriving via the plate instead of the claw.\n\n    So this uses `gym`'s mechanism rather than a rotation: rotate *and*\n    `scaleX`, extending the claw along its own axis until it is an arm rather\n    than a lozenge. That is what gets the tip clear of the head. §What a claw\n    cannot do carries the arithmetic; the short version is that rotation gets\n    a claw out of the silhouette and length is what makes it read.\n\n    ── The sign ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Five units wide by seven tall, spanning y -2 to 5: one unit clear of the\n    torso top at y=6, and two units inside the landscape crop line at y=-4.\n    The extended claw crosses its lower edge, so the two overlap — §Props need\n    contact, an unheld prop reads as hovering and `gym`'s bar racked three\n    units clear read as a bar floating above a crab. The arm is drawn after\n    the plate so the claw is visibly in front of it, gripping.\n\n    The plate is #C9D1D9, which `thinking`'s bubble already proves readable\n    against both the day sky and the night one now that the rock pool is\n    behind him. The glyph is #000000 — the tone the eyes already declare, and\n    deliberately not a new near-black. See the note on the glyph below.\n\n    A `?` needs five rows: top bar, right descender, turn to the stem, a gap,\n    and the dot. At three the dot merges into the stem and it degenerates —\n    `asleep` records the same finding for the Z, which needs its diagonal to\n    cross two interior columns or it rasterises as an I. Five rows inside a\n    seven row plate leaves a whole unit of border top and bottom.\n\n    ── Why nothing raises it ──────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    The sign is up on frame 0 and stays up. `frameAt` in\n    `packages/cli/src/daemon.ts` is wall-clock modulo the frame count, so a\n    state never begins its animation at frame 0 — the panel joins whatever\n    phase the clock is in. A raise at the top of a loop is therefore not an\n    entrance, it is a pump at loop frequency, forever, on a screen watched for\n    minutes while someone decides. `NEEDS_PERMISSION` persists until a human\n    answers it.\n\n    The sign does *not* breathe with him. It and the arm holding it sit\n    outside the breath group, so he holds it steady while his body moves\n    underneath — which is what a person does with a placard, and the only way\n    the plate stays on whole device pixels. See the note beside it below; an\n    earlier version of this paragraph argued the opposite and was describing\n    the version that fringed.\n  -->\n\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* Whole art pixels throughout. One frame of the eight second loop is\n         1.5625%. */\n\n      /* `idle`'s breath: two seconds alternating, about the hips, so the body\n         settles onto planted legs rather than lifting off them. Four second\n         visual period, twice in the loop. */\n      #breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 2s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        from { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        to   { transform: scale(1.02, 0.98) translate(0, 0.5px); }\n      }\n\n      /* The holding arm. Rotation to clear the silhouette, axial scale to\n         make it long enough to be an arm — `gym` uses the same pair, at the\n         same shoulder pivot. Static: this pose is the screen. */\n      #right-arm-joint {\n        transform-origin: 13px 10px;\n        transform: rotate(-73deg) scaleX(2.85);\n      }\n\n      /* Squashed about the eye line, never hidden — §Closing an eye. Linear,\n         because a lid that accelerates reads as a wince. */\n      #eyes-blink {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 8s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* One blink. Every keyframe lands on a whole frame of the 64; measured,\n         frames 40 and 43 are fully open and 41-42 fully shut, so the travel\n         frames the percentages ask for do not survive the snap. It reads as a\n         250ms blink, which is what it is for. */\n      @keyframes blink {\n        0%, 62.5%      { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        64.0625%       { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        65.625%        { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        67.1875%, 100% { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg, regrouped not redrawn ───── -->\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <!-- Planted, and outside the breath: the foot line stays flat. -->\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"breathe\">\n      <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n\n        <g id=\"left-arm-joint\">\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <rect id=\"left-shoulder\" x=\"2\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n\n        <!-- The shoulder rect stays *outside* the scaled group, and that is\n             not a detail. `thinking` puts one inside its joint because that\n             joint only rotates; scaled by 2.85 as well, the shoulder — which\n             lies on the far side of the pivot — is stretched to 5.7 units and\n             swung the opposite way, straight down through the torso and out\n             below the legs. Rendered, and it looked like a spear through him.\n             `gym` has no *shoulder* rect inside its scaled group — only the\n             claw itself — which is why its arm works. This one keeps a static shoulder to fill the pivot; it\n             sits inside the torso's own footprint, so it is never seen. -->\n        <rect id=\"right-shoulder\" x=\"11\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n        <g id=\"eyes-blink\">\n          <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <!-- ── the sign, and the arm holding it — outside the breath ───────── -->\n    <!-- Held steady while the body breathes underneath, which is both what a\n         person does with a placard and the only way this stays on whole device\n         pixels. Inside `#breathe` the scale(1.02, 0.98) put every edge of the\n         plate and the glyph on fractional pixels for most of the loop, and the\n         midpoint of #C9D1D9 over a dark glyph is nearer #DE886D than it is to\n         either — so every soft edge snapped to Clawd's own body colour and\n         drew an orange keyline round the sign and through the `?`. Up to 573\n         peach pixels on a frame, against a glyph of 417. It also cost 2.8x on\n         the wire. The same defect, from the same cause, as the boot splash's\n         wordmark before that was drawn as rects. -->\n    <rect id=\"fx-sign-plate\" x=\"11\" y=\"-2\" width=\"5\" height=\"7\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n\n    <!-- `?`, three by five, inside the plate's one unit border.\n\n         #000000, not #0D1117. The dark tone here has to be one the document\n         already declares, and that one is the eyes. A new near-black is not\n         free: §Palette snapping warns that every declared colour becomes a\n         snap target for every other colour's edges, and #0D1117 sits *nearer*\n         a faint peach edge (21.4) than the background black does (41.8). The\n         snapper's transparency rule needs those edges to resolve to the\n         background so it can drop them; #0D1117 stole them, kept them opaque,\n         and stitched a black keyline round the arm, the torso and the legs on\n         every frame — invisible on a black stage, and a hard outline against\n         the rock pool. Up to 418 pixels a frame. -->\n    <g id=\"fx-sign-glyph\" fill=\"#000000\">\n      <rect x=\"12\" y=\"-1\" width=\"3\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"14\" y=\"0\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"13\" y=\"3\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <!-- Drawn last so the claw crosses the plate and reads as gripping.\n\n         The fill is declared here rather than inherited. `right-arm` sat\n         inside `#body-color-group` until it moved out of the breath, and that\n         group is where the peach came from — outside it the rect inherits from\n         `#master-group`, which is to say black. It rendered as a black bar\n         from the shoulder to the sign, and it took looking at a composed panel\n         to see it: on frames alone, against a black stage, a black arm is\n         simply absent rather than obviously wrong. -->\n    <g id=\"right-arm-joint\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","confused":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"12\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: confused (WAITING — 60s idle after a Notification)\n\n    Claude has stopped and is waiting for a human. Clawd looks straight at you\n    and a prompt caret blinks beside his head.\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg. Every base element appears below\n    at its original coordinates and colours; all motion is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    ── Why there is no tilt ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    The plan for this screen originally called for a 6 degree body tilt to\n    carry the head-cock. `docs/ANIMATION.md` §Scale by size, not by taste\n    already recorded a 2.5 degree torso tilt being tried in `idle` and reading\n    as a corrupted sprite, and a review measured the 6: nine one-pixel steps\n    across the torso's top edge and eight times the antialiased pixels. The\n    torso is 88x56 device pixels. Shallow diagonals on slabs that size\n    staircase, every time.\n\n    So the cock is carried entirely by asymmetry, which is `gym`'s technique\n    (§Eyes, in its plan): the left eye rides a unit low and the right a unit\n    high, and each claw goes the same way as the eye on its side. All four\n    together approximate one rotation in whole units. Nothing rotates and\n    nothing translates by a fraction.\n\n    Not the opposite sense — that was tried first and rejected; see the note\n    beside the offsets below. Two cues pulling against each other do not\n    compose into a cocked head on a body with no neck, they cancel into a\n    shear.\n\n    ── Why a caret and not a question mark ────────────────────────────────\n\n    `NEEDS_PERMISSION` is the other attention state, it can also be the hero,\n    and it holds up a sign with a `?` on it. If this screen also showed a `?`\n    the two would be one picture at a glance, which is the failure mode the\n    spec's whole §1 exists to prevent — those are the two screens it is\n    written to serve.\n\n    A prompt caret says \"type something\" to anyone who has ever used a\n    terminal, and this is a device for a person who lives in one. It also\n    separates cleanly from `thinking`, whose 9x5 bubble with a tail and three\n    cyan dots is a completely different mass. That mass is what a glance\n    actually reads: eye direction is the supporting cue, not the separator,\n    because nobody can judge \"eyes one art pixel lower than they were forty\n    seconds ago\" against an 11x7 slab with no brow to reference.\n\n    ── Why it survives being stared at ────────────────────────────────────\n\n    This may be the most-seen screen on the device — the spec says it may be,\n    which is not a measurement, and no counting has been done on how often\n    `Notification` fires. Either way `WAITING` is a resting state, so this is\n    built like `idle` rather than like a beat: twelve seconds, one slow breath\n    cycle three times, two blinks well apart, and a caret that is the only\n    thing moving at any speed.\n\n    **26 of 96 frames are distinct, and that is correct.** Most animations here\n    measure 91-100% and this looks like a defect beside them, but the class is\n    not as clean as it sounds — `thinking` is 90.6% and `permission-sign`, which\n    shipped alongside this, is 34%. Both are resting screens too. The caret\n    runs 8 frames, the breath 32, their LCM is 32, and the loop is three\n    repeats of that plus two blinks. The check exists to catch a lost keyframe\n    block; nothing is lost, and 77 of the 96 frame transitions change pixels.\n\n    Worth knowing before anyone tries to raise it: an intermediate version of\n    this file did reach 34 distinct frames, and the extra motion was a keyline\n    flickering on and off with the breath — a defect, not animation. The number\n    is not a quality signal in either direction.\n  -->\n\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* Whole art pixels throughout. Every value below lands on one of the 96\n         frames of the twelve second loop: one frame is 1.041667%. */\n\n      /* The caret. `steps(1)` because a caret is a discrete state, not a\n         motion — a fade would render as a grey block on the frames between,\n         and at 8fps a one-second cycle is four frames on, four off. */\n      #fx-caret {\n        animation: caret 1s infinite steps(1);\n      }\n\n      @keyframes caret {\n        0%   { opacity: 1; }\n        50%  { opacity: 0; }\n        100% { opacity: 1; }\n      }\n\n      /* `idle`'s breath exactly: two seconds alternating, about the hips, so\n         the body settles onto planted legs rather than lifting off them. A\n         four second visual period tiles three times in the twelve second loop\n         and the seam is clean.\n\n         The magnitude is idle's too, and it matters more than it looks. A\n         first draft used scale(1.012, 0.988) with no translate: on a torso 56\n         device pixels tall that is under one pixel of travel, so 76 of the 96\n         frames rasterised identically and the animation was 21% distinct\n         against 91-100% for every animation that ships. The translate is what\n         actually moves it. */\n      #breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 2s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        from { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        to   { transform: scale(1.02, 0.98) translate(0, 0.5px); }\n      }\n\n      /* The head-cock: static offsets, not an animation. Every appendage\n         tilts the *same* way — left side down, right side up — so the offsets\n         compose into one rotation approximated in whole units.\n\n         The first version had the claws opposing the eyes, on the reasoning\n         that a counter-tilt would read as a cocked head against a level body.\n         It does not: Clawd's head and torso are a single 11x7 slab with no\n         neck to absorb an opposing shoulder line, so the two cues cancel into\n         a shear and it reads as a lopsided crab rather than a curious one.\n         Rendered both ways and compared at true size. */\n      #left-eye-cock  { transform: translateY(1px); }\n      #right-eye-cock { transform: translateY(-1px); }\n      #left-arm-joint  { transform: translateY(1px); }\n      #right-arm-joint { transform: translateY(-1px); }\n\n      /* Squashed about each eye's own line, never hidden — §Closing an eye.\n         Linear, because a lid that accelerates reads as a wince. The origins\n         are in each eye's own coordinates, so the cock above still applies. */\n      #left-eye-blink {\n        transform-origin: 4.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #right-eye-blink {\n        transform-origin: 10.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* Two blinks, far enough apart that they never read as a pair. Every\n         keyframe lands on a whole frame of the 96. Measured, the eyes are open\n         on frames 24 and 66 and shut on 25-26 and 67-68: the travel frames the\n         percentages ask for do not survive the snap, because the breath is at\n         a different phase for each blink and multiplies the lid differently.\n         Both read as a 250ms blink, which is what matters. */\n      @keyframes blink {\n        0%, 25%              { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        26.041667%           { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        27.083333%           { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        28.125%              { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        68.75%               { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        69.791667%           { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        70.833333%           { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        71.875%, 100%        { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── the prompt caret, above and right of his head ─────────────────── -->\n  <!-- Five units by three, spanning y=2 to 5 — one unit clear of the torso\n       top at y=6 at rest, 1.63 at full breath, and six units inside the\n       landscape crop line at y=-4. It never touches him.\n\n       It is a block rather than the bare 3x1 bar it started as, and the block\n       is the part that works: a lone pale dash measured 1.31:1 against the day\n       sky, which is the path the daemon actually takes, so the one element\n       carrying this screen's meaning was invisible on it. Two tones fixes it\n       in both directions — the dark block reads against the three pale skies,\n       the pale core against the dark one.\n\n       The block is #000000, and that is not interchangeable with the near\n       black it started as. #0D1117 sits closer to a faint peach edge (21.4)\n       than the background black does (41.8), so declaring it hands those edge\n       pixels a snap target that is *not* the background — they stay opaque and\n       stitch a hard keyline round the torso and legs. Measured at 21,870\n       pixels across all 96 frames, on every frame. `permission-sign` shipped\n       exactly this defect in its `?` and had it removed; the fix here was\n       written an hour later and reintroduced it in the sibling file. §Palette\n       snapping is right: before adding a colour, ask what edge it now sits\n       between.\n\n       A `>` chevron was tried in front of it during authoring, to make the\n       terminal reading explicit. It does not survive this scale: a chevron\n       small enough to fit is a diagonal of single pixels touching only at\n       their corners, and it rasterises as three scattered dots rather than an\n       arrowhead. Rendered and rejected. The bare caret is cleaner, and the\n       blink is what carries the meaning anyway — static things do not blink,\n       cursors do. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-caret\">\n    <rect x=\"10\" y=\"2\" width=\"5\" height=\"3\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n    <rect x=\"11\" y=\"3\" width=\"3\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg, regrouped not redrawn ───── -->\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <!-- Planted, and outside the breath: the foot line stays flat. -->\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"breathe\">\n      <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n\n        <!-- The claws tilt with the eyes, and they are also what stops this\n             being `idle` with a glyph over it — `asleep` needed the same thing\n             and solved it the same way, with claws hanging low.\n\n             No shoulder rects. `thinking` carries them because its joint\n             rotates and would open; these only translate a single unit and\n             never leave the torso's own rows (y=6..13), so a shoulder cannot\n             be exposed. Kept, they drew nothing but a one-pixel antialias\n             flicker at the torso's right edge on six frames of the loop. -->\n        <g id=\"left-arm-joint\">\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n\n        <g id=\"right-arm-joint\">\n          <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n        <g id=\"left-eye-cock\">\n          <g id=\"left-eye-blink\">\n            <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n        <g id=\"right-eye-cock\">\n          <g id=\"right-eye-blink\">\n            <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","dizzy":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"12\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: dizzy (StopFailure)\n\n    A turn failed. Clawd has taken a knock and is seeing stars.\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg. Every base element appears below\n    at its original coordinates and colours; all motion is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    ── Twelve seconds, because FAILED does not decay ──────────────────────\n\n    `effectiveState` in packages/daemon/src/session.ts promotes only from\n    `IDLE`, so this stays on screen until another event arrives or the session\n    is evicted ten minutes later. It is a resting screen, not a beat, and it\n    has to survive being stared at — which is why the plan's first draft of a\n    four second loop was wrong, and why it is built like `idle` instead.\n\n    ── Why nothing rotates ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Not the body: `docs/ANIMATION.md` §Scale by size, not by taste records a\n    2.5deg torso tilt reading as a corrupted sprite. The 6deg measurement — nine\n    one-pixel steps across the top edge — is in `PLANS.md` §Confused and in\n    `confused.svg`, not in `ANIMATION.md`; an earlier version of this line put\n    both at the same citation and half of it is not there. The wobble is\n    translation against planted legs. Its keyframe values are eighths of a unit\n    — whole device pixels — but it is eased, so the track passes through\n    fractional positions between them and most frames land on one; what makes\n    that safe is the palette snap resolving the edge after the fact, per\n    `docs/ANIMATION.md` §Palette snapping, and not the values. The whole-pixel\n    rule an earlier version of this line invoked was repealed by §Ease, don't\n    step.\n\n    **Measured range: -5 to +4 device pixels**, the minimum on frames 63-64.\n    Which frames hold the maximum is not quoted, because the answer moves with\n    the neutral position you measure against and three passes produced three\n    different lists. Two earlier versions of this line said -6 to +4 and\n    quarter-unit keyframes, both true of the track before §the deep lurch below\n    was cut — so the file stated two different amplitudes for one animation, a\n    hundred lines apart. This is the paragraph to update when the stagger\n    changes; it has been missed twice.\n\n    Not the stars either. A one-pixel prop is entirely edge, and rotating one\n    is a still pixel with a fringe: at a fractional position the snap resolves\n    it toward the body colour, which is the defect `thinking` documents for its\n    bubble and the boot splash hit again in its wordmark. So the orbit is\n    twelve discrete cells on whole units, stepped with `steps(1)`.\n\n    Twelve is the number that makes the phasing work. The orbit is 3s — 24\n    frames — so twelve cells average two frames each, and the three stars are\n    authored four cells apart.\n\n    Neither of those is quite what renders, and the reason is worth writing\n    down because it is one edit away from being undone. **The dwell is 2, 3, 1\n    frames repeating, not two each**, and the spacing alternates 4/4/4 on odd\n    frames with a permutation of 3/4/5 on even ones. The cause is not the\n    negative delays: -1s and -2s are exact binary floats and a third of 3s is\n    exactly 1s, which an earlier version of this note had wrong. It is the\n    **six-decimal keyframe percentages** below. `8.333333%` of 3s is 249.99999ms\n    and lands just before frame 2's sample at 250ms, while `16.666667%` is\n    500.00001ms and lands just after frame 4's at 500ms — so a boundary falls\n    either side of a sample depending on which way that percentage rounded.\n    **What would undo it is not the number of digits.** Seven or eight decimals\n    round the same way and change nothing, because the direction is a property\n    of the repeating digits rather than the precision. What collapses the orbit\n    to an even 2/2/2 — and takes the animation with it — is truncating instead\n    of rounding: `16.666666%` is 499.99998ms, the other side of the sample.\n\n    So does enough precision. At sixteen significant digits\n    `8.333333333333334%` of 3s is 250.00000000000003ms — past the sample, not\n    before it — and rendering that variant gives a different animation: 92\n    distinct frames instead of 89, an identical adjacent pair, and even-frame\n    gaps that stop being uniformly 3/4/5. Ten decimals and fifteen significant\n    digits do not cross, and are byte-identical to what ships.\n\n    **Do not adjust these percentages at all.** Three versions of this note have\n    tried to state a rule about which edits are safe — any extra digit, then ten\n    decimals, then no terminating decimal at all — and all three were wrong,\n    each one written to correct the last. The behaviour depends on which side of\n    a sample time each of twelve rounded values falls, and that is not something\n    a rule of thumb gets right. If they have to change, re-render and diff the\n    frames.\n\n    It reads better this way. Measured over the loop: the gaps are 4/4/4 on\n    all 48 odd frames and 3/4/5 on all 48 even ones, and one or two of the three\n    stars change cell on every frame step — never none, never all three at once,\n    which is what an even 2/2/2 would have given. Three stars on a four second\n    loop cannot be evenly phased at all, because 32/3 is not an integer.\n\n    ── The eyes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Crossed, by moving each two units inward. Whole art pixels, existing IDs,\n    translation only. A held `scaleY` squash was the obvious alternative and is\n    exactly what `asleep` holds for its entire loop, so it would have made the\n    two screens one picture.\n\n    ── Not distress ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    `StopFailure` is a failed turn, not a catastrophe, and the strip tint\n    already carries the severity. Nothing here flashes, shakes or reddens.\n  -->\n\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* Whole art pixels throughout. One frame of the twelve second loop is\n         1.041667%; the 3s orbit inside it is 24 frames, and 12s / 3s is 4, so\n         it tiles exactly and the loop seam is clean. */\n\n      #fx-star-a {\n        animation: orbit 3s infinite steps(1);\n      }\n\n      /* Four cells apart, which is a third of the orbit. Negative delays start\n         each star already in position rather than waiting to enter. */\n      #fx-star-b { animation: orbit 3s -1s infinite steps(1); }\n      #fx-star-c { animation: orbit 3s -2s infinite steps(1); }\n\n      @keyframes orbit {\n        0.000000% { transform: translate(5px, 0px); }\n        8.333333% { transform: translate(4px, -2px); }\n        16.666667% { transform: translate(3px, -3px); }\n        25.000000% { transform: translate(0px, -3px); }\n        33.333333% { transform: translate(-2px, -3px); }\n        41.666667% { transform: translate(-4px, -2px); }\n        50.000000% { transform: translate(-5px, 0px); }\n        58.333333% { transform: translate(-4px, 2px); }\n        66.666667% { transform: translate(-3px, 2px); }\n        75.000000% { transform: translate(0px, 2px); }\n        83.333333% { transform: translate(3px, 2px); }\n        91.666667% { transform: translate(4px, 2px); }\n      }\n\n      /* The wobble: translation against planted legs, never rotation, and on\n         the full twelve seconds rather than a two second cycle repeated six\n         times.\n\n         **This is what earns the loop length.** With a 2s sway the file\n         rendered four seconds: the star set at frame *f* depends only on *f*\n         mod 8 — measured off the bake, not derived — so the orbit's visual\n         period is eight frames, and 32/16/8 all divide 32. Three identical\n         stars a third of a lap apart is the reason, though not by a clean\n         four-cell rotation: they are four cells apart on odd frames only, and\n         3/4/5 apart on even ones. §Why nothing rotates has the cause.\n         Frames 32-95 were byte-for-byte copies of 0-31 in a 402KB bake, while\n         the comment above claimed a resting screen built like `idle`. `idle`\n         earns sixteen seconds with blinks, two looks and a yawn; a stagger\n         that repeats every four is not a stagger, it is a metronome.\n\n         So the amplitude varies across the twelve: two ordinary sways, two\n         smaller ones, then a deeper lurch, a hard rebound and a slow settle.\n         The values are\n         whole device pixels at the keyframes — eighths of a unit — though the\n         eased track passes through fractions between them; the palette snap\n         resolves those, per `docs/ANIMATION.md` §Palette snapping.\n\n         **The deep lurch is 5 device pixels of body-over-feet offset, passed\n         through in two frames, and both halves of that are the fix.** It was 6,\n         held four, and `animation-critic` ruled it needs-work on a measurement\n         nobody had taken: not the offset, but how much torso is left outboard\n         of the outer leg.\n\n           minimum outer-leg inset, device pixels, measured at the torso's\n           bottom row against the four planted feet:\n\n             thinking   7    (never below)\n             idle       6    (never below)\n             dizzy      2    before this fix, on frames 63-66 at row 174 —\n                              63-67 taking each frame's own bottom row, the same\n                              sub-pixel corner case noted below\n             dizzy      3    after, on frames 63-64 at every torso row\n                              (frame 65 also reads 3 on its bottom row alone,\n                              where the sub-pixel edge drops one corner pixel)\n\n         At 2 the leg does not read as under the body — it reads as dropping\n         off the corner while the torso overhangs empty air on the other side.\n         And the deep pose was *held*: four frames at the extreme before the\n         fix, two after. That pair is the whole of what is worth quoting here.\n\n         Three drafts of this line tried to say more — \"ten frames within 1.5\n         pixels\", \"the longest static run in the loop\", \"seven within 1.5\n         pixels\" — and each was wrong in a different way: the count was eight\n         not ten, the superlative was false, and the seven mixed a whole-loop\n         tally into a sentence whose other half was contiguous. Near-miss\n         tallies depend on a tolerance and on where you put the neutral\n         position, and nothing in this file needs one. Frames at the extreme is\n         a count with no free parameters.\n\n         The precedent is `thinking.svg`, and it is exact rather than\n         approximate. That file chose three quarters of a unit — six device\n         pixels — on the grounds that \"the outer leg still had a quarter unit of\n         body over it at that offset, so nothing was standing off the edge\",\n         and then recorded that the reasoning was sound and the conclusion still\n         wrong. This file had reproduced that value *and* that argument, word\n         for word, in the comment defending it.\n\n         Two earlier versions of this paragraph made it worse by citing `idle`\n         and `thinking` as having shipped six device pixels themselves. Neither\n         did; their sway tracks are a quarter unit, and the 6 and 0.95 in those\n         files are the rejected values each records as a defect. A third version\n         compared torso-edge excursion across the three, which conflates a\n         scale with a displacement — `idle`'s wide frames are its yawn getting\n         broader, not its body leaving its feet.\n\n         What survives all of that is the arrival, which was always the good\n         part: the offset builds from frame 59 to its extreme at 63, legs under\n         the torso throughout.\n\n         **It does not return faster than it came — it rebounds hard and then\n         settles slowly**, which a draft of this note had backwards.\n\n         The rebound is the part that is safe to quote, because it needs no\n         neutral position: the two frames after the extreme cover 2 of the 5\n         device pixels, and the remaining 3 are spread over the frames after\n         that. How many depends entirely on how close to zero counts as back,\n         and successive drafts of this paragraph published nine-and-eleven and\n         then five-and-nine from the same bake by choosing differently. Both had\n         the return longer than the descent, which is the claim and is what was\n         backwards before. Neither pair belongs in a comment.\n\n         The constraint to hold on any future edit is the one the critic named:\n         **the outer leg keeps at least 3 device pixels of body outboard of it,\n         and the extreme is passed through in no more than two or three\n         frames.** Both are measurable from the bake; neither is a matter of\n         taste. */\n      #wobble {\n        animation: stagger 12s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes stagger {\n        0%        { transform: translate(-0.5px, 0); }\n        8.333333% { transform: translate(0, -0.25px); }\n        16.66667% { transform: translate(0.5px, 0); }\n        25%       { transform: translate(0, -0.25px); }\n        33.33333% { transform: translate(-0.25px, 0); }\n        41.66667% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        50%       { transform: translate(0.25px, 0); }\n        58.33333% { transform: translate(0, -0.25px); }\n        66.66667% { transform: translate(-0.625px, 0); }\n        69.79167% { transform: translate(-0.375px, -0.125px); }\n        75%       { transform: translate(-0.125px, -0.25px); }\n        83.33333% { transform: translate(0.5px, 0); }\n        91.66667% { transform: translate(0.25px, -0.25px); }\n        100%      { transform: translate(-0.5px, 0); }\n      }\n\n      /* `idle`'s breath, about the hips, so the body settles onto planted legs\n         rather than lifting off them.\n\n         Not on every frame. On 6 of the 96 — 5, 27, 59, 66, 67 and 91 — the\n         breath and the stagger's vertical component together clear **one**\n         device-pixel row between the torso and all four feet.\n\n         Worth the exact number, because a draft of this note said `idle` \"does\n         the same on 14 of 128\" and that understates `idle` by an order of\n         magnitude. Measured: `idle` opens 1 to **9** device pixels across\n         frames 81-94, eight or more of them for seven consecutive frames.\n         Dizzy's is one row, on isolated single frames. `thinking`, `confused`,\n         `permission-sign` and `asleep` are at zero, and so are `gym` and\n         `bouldering` — measured across all ten, dizzy is the only one\n         with any gap at all — `idle`'s nine device pixels were closed on 22 Aug\n         and its own comment records the fix. (`typing` looks like a third and is not: he is\n         seated, so his legs start eight device rows lower than everyone else's\n         with body drawn continuously above them.) `thinking.svg` states its\n         zero as a measured property.\n\n         So the corpus is inconsistent and this is its mildest instance, which\n         is why it is recorded rather than fixed. `docs/ANIMATION.md` §Animating\n         the whole sprite is not animating is on this side of it — \"the gap that\n         opens between body and legs is what the eye reads as a breath\" — and\n         `thinking.svg` takes the opposite line. That disagreement wants\n         settling in the doc before either animation is edited, and `idle` is\n         the one that would change. */\n      #breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 2s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        from { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        to   { transform: scale(1.02, 0.98) translate(0, 0.5px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Crossed. Static offsets, not an animation. */\n      #left-eye-cross  { transform: translateX(2px); }\n      #right-eye-cross { transform: translateX(-2px); }\n\n      /* Squashed about each eye's own line, never hidden — §Closing an eye.\n         Linear, because a lid that accelerates reads as a wince.\n\n         **This is also what makes the loop twelve seconds.** Without it the\n         file rendered four: the star set repeats on an eight-frame period (see\n         §the wobble), so frames 32-95 were byte-for-byte copies of 0-31 — a\n         402KB bake, two thirds of it duplicate, claiming to be a resting screen\n         built like `idle`. A blink on a twelve-second track is the cheapest\n         beat that makes the length real, and most of the awake animations have\n         one. Not all: `bouldering` holds its eyes for the whole loop by a\n         recorded decision — a squashed eye there buys an expression at the\n         price of frames with an eye missing — and `gym`'s eyes squint under the\n         bar rather than blink. Both are animations where something crosses the\n         face, which is the distinction, not a rule that every screen blinks. */\n      #left-eye-blink {\n        transform-origin: 4.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #right-eye-blink {\n        transform-origin: 10.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* Two blinks of 96 frames, far enough apart that they never read as a\n         pair. Two frames shut, every value a whole frame.\n\n         Shut on frames 31-32 and 71-72. Frames 30 and 70 are the last fully\n         open ones — they are where the close begins, which is what an earlier\n         version of this comment called the blink. Off by one in the direction\n         that matters least, and still worth being right about: the numbers here\n         are what a reader checks a re-bake against. */\n      @keyframes blink {\n        0%, 31.25%       { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        32.291667%       { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        33.333333%       { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        34.375%          { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        72.916667%       { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        73.958333%       { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        75.000000%       { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        76.041667%, 100% { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── three stars, orbiting above the head ──────────────────────────── -->\n  <!-- A pale pip in a dark cross, drawn at the orbit's centre; the keyframes move\n       the group — the keyframes translate by (cell - centre), so the rects\n       have to sit at the centre and not at a cell, or every star is offset by\n       the difference and two of the three end up touching.\n\n       **Two tones, and it has to be.** A single pale pixel measured\n       1.31:1 against the day sky and 1.80:1 against dawn — invisible, on the\n       path the daemon takes — which is the third time this exact defect has\n       landed: the boot splash's wordmark, `confused`'s caret, and this. No one\n       flat tone reads against both a pale sky and a dark one. The cross\n       carries the pale skies and the pip carries night.\n\n       Measured on the composed panel, worst star over the whole loop, taking\n       each star's best-reading pixel against the sky right behind it: **dawn\n       3.75:1**, dusk 5.06:1, day 8.05:1, night 10.62:1. Dawn is the floor and\n       it is the number to quote. A commit message on this branch said \"7.6:1\n       or better at all four\", which was one sky band against black generalised\n       to four skies and every orbit position; the real floor is half that.\n\n       Which tone carries it is the part worth keeping, and the two swap over\n       the course of the day rather than dividing cleanly. Counting every star\n       instance across the loop — 288 a sky — the cross is the better-reading\n       half on 288 of 288 at day, 216 at dawn, 120 at dusk and 0 at night. So\n       dusk is already the pip's, not a pale sky the cross owns.\n\n       And each tone bottoms out somewhere. The worst single cross pixel over\n       the loop is 6.03 at day, 2.05 at dawn, 1.50 at dusk, 1.08 at night; the\n       worst pip pixel is 1.31 at day, 1.80 at dawn, 2.75 at dusk, 10.62 at\n       night. Both fall under 2:1 on some sky. What makes the glyph legible is\n       that a five-cell cross only needs one pixel to read, and on every sky one\n       of the two tones is well clear — which is the argument for two tones\n       rather than for a better single one.\n\n       Black, not a near-black: `permission-sign` records what happens when a\n       new dark tone is declared — it captures the antialiased edges the\n       transparency rule needs and stitches a keyline round the character.\n\n       The lowest cells are raised a unit so the cross's bottom arm clears the\n       torso. The first version measured the *pip* at y=4 against a torso\n       topping out at y=6 and concluded there was clearance; the arm is a unit\n       below the pip, so it sat flush against the torso on 33 of 96 frames —\n       a dark notch welded to the top of his head, which is on this plan's own\n       \"Not wanted\" list. `asleep` shipped a glyph crossing the face once and\n       it read as display corruption. Measure the prop, not its centre. -->\n  <g>\n    <g id=\"fx-star-a\">\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"0\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"6\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"8\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"2\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-star-b\">\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"0\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"6\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"8\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"2\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-star-c\">\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"0\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"6\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"8\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"2\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg, less its shadow ─────────── -->\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <!-- Planted, and outside the wobble: the foot line stays flat while the\n         body moves over it. -->\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"wobble\">\n      <g id=\"breathe\">\n        <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n          <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n\n        <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n          <g id=\"left-eye-cross\">\n            <g id=\"left-eye-blink\">\n              <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <\/g>\n          <\/g>\n          <g id=\"right-eye-cross\">\n            <g id=\"right-eye-blink\">\n              <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <\/g>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","overheated":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"6\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: overheated (StopFailure with error_type rate_limit or overloaded)\n\n    He has worked himself flat and is lying there fanning himself. Not hurt and\n    not asleep — spent, and fine in a bit.\n\n    ── Why this is a pose and not a prop ──────────────────────────────────\n\n    The first plan for this screen kept the standing silhouette and separated it\n    from `asleep` by eye state and rising heat. Both separators were false.\n    `asleep`'s `@keyframes eyelid` holds a `scaleY` of 0.5 for its entire loop,\n    which is a half-height eye and not a shut one, so \"half-closed against shut\"\n    is not a difference; and `dizzy.svg` already records rejecting a held\n    `scaleY` squash on this same state because it \"would have made the two\n    screens one picture\". Meanwhile something rising off a still Clawd is what\n    `asleep` *is*.\n\n    `PLANS.md` §Confused states what a glance actually reads: prop mass and\n    silhouette, not eye direction. So the silhouette changes, and once it does\n    the prop is free to be anything. `docs/ANIMATION.md` §The generation\n    contract names this exact case — \"a sploot for sleeping… give the variant\n    its own id (`torso-sploot`)\".\n\n    The scene is upstream's `clawd-working-overheated.svg`: \"sitting down\n    (splooted), completely exhausted, fanning himself with one hand\". None of\n    its execution transfers — its smoke fades from 0.6 opacity to 0 and scales\n    x2, its tones are two new greys with rounded corners, its eyes are 0.4 units\n    tall and its breath is 2.5s. Every one of those breaks something here.\n\n    ── The pose, in numbers ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    `#torso-sploot` is x=2 y=10 11x5, against the standing x=2 y=6 11x7.\n\n    **Same x and same width on purpose.** `paintContactShadow` draws a fixed\n    9-unit band from unit 3, so a wider body would overhang its own shadow. At\n    this width the overhang is 1 unit each side, exactly as when he stands, so\n    nothing in the renderer changes and `castsShadow` needs no new entry — he is\n    on the ground, more so than usual. The bottom edge stays on the ground line\n    at y=15. What changes is 2 units of height and a 4-unit drop.\n\n    `#legs-sploot` holds four 1x1 rects at y=9, in the same four columns as the standing\n    legs (3, 5, 9, 11), so they read as his legs — splayed out above a body that\n    has spread beneath them. They sit directly on the torso's top edge:\n    the legs' bottom row is the torso's top row minus one on all 48 frames,\n    measured on isolated renders of each.\n\n    **`hipGap` reading 0 is not evidence of that.** That gate walks up from the\n    bottom-most drawn row *through the legs*; here the bottom-most row is the\n    torso and there is nothing below it, so the walk runs the whole 48-row body\n    and then trips the gate's own `LEG_BAND` bound, returning 0 without ever\n    looking for a gap. It does that for any pose whose contiguous bottom band is\n    deeper than 24 rows. It is vacuous for a sploot, and the\n    contiguity measured above is what actually holds.\n\n    The eyes are 1x1 at y=12, in the standing columns, half the standing height.\n    Squares, not slits — hence `#left-eye-squint` and `#right-eye-squint`, which\n    is also the id the generation contract uses as its example. An earlier\n    version called them slits in the prose and then minted `-slit` ids for them,\n    so the identifier asserted the shape the paragraph beside it denied.\n    Upstream draws 2x0.4; widening ours walks straight at `asleep`'s\n    `scale(2, 0.5)`, which is the shape this screen has to stay away from. They are not carrying the separation from `asleep` — the body is\n    — so they only have to not contradict it.\n\n    ── Why the fanning claw extends as well as turning ────────────────────\n\n    `docs/ANIMATION.md` §What a claw cannot do is explicit: a 2x2 block turned\n    is \"an arrowhead, and an arrowhead hung off an 11x7 slab is not a limb\". A\n    45deg swing was built, judged at true size and rejected. What makes a claw\n    read is length, and `gym` is the proof — `rotate(-73deg) scaleX(2.843)`\n    turns the same 2x2 into an arm.\n\n    So upstream's plain `rotate(-60deg)` would fail here. `#arm-fan` sweeps and\n    extends together, pivoting at its **shoulder** corner (13, 13) — the end\n    attached to the body. Pivoting at the far corner instead, which a first\n    version did, makes `scaleX` stretch the claw back *into* the torso and\n    leaves the tip sweeping three device pixels: a stub twitching against his\n    side rather than a limb fanning. `scaleX(2.2)` takes the 2x2 to 4.4x2 and the\n    sweep runs -25deg to -58deg. Both numbers are bounded by the canvas, not by\n    taste: at 2.5 and -18deg the extended claw reached column 167, the last one\n    there is, and a limb touching the edge of the raster is a limb that has been\n    cropped. Nothing may be drawn in row 0, row 199, column 0 or column 167.\n\n    ── Timing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Six seconds is 48 frames at 8fps, and every period divides it.\n\n    **The breath is a 6s track, not `1.5s alternate`, and that distinction is\n    the whole animation.** An earlier version of this paragraph specified\n    `1.5s alternate` — 12 frames each way, 4 alternations — which is exactly the\n    arrangement that made the fan's 8-frame period, the steam's 24 and the\n    breath's 24 all divide 24, so frames 24-47 came out byte-for-byte copies of\n    0-23. Anyone re-deriving the loop from that sentence reinstates the defect,\n    which is why it is written down rather than quietly replaced. The breath\n    keeps four beats at the same rate; they are just keyframes on a full-loop\n    track with four different depths.\n\n    The fan is `alternate` and does have to run an even number of times: at 0.5s\n    it is 4 frames each way and 12 alternations. The steam runs twice on a 3s\n    track, three puffs at delays 0, -1s and -2s, which is `dizzy`'s phasing and\n    lands them 8 frames apart.\n\n    **Six and not twelve.** `dizzy` needed twelve because `FAILED` does not\n    decay and it had to survive being stared at, and it earned them with a blink\n    on a 12s track. This has three tracks at three speeds and no beat that wants\n    a twelfth second. `dizzy.svg` records what a loop longer than its content\n    costs: frames 32-95 byte-for-byte copies of 0-31, in a 402KB bake.\n\n    ── The steam ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    2x2 puffs, three dark cells and one pale, in `#000000` and `#C9D1D9` — the\n    two tones `dizzy`'s stars already carry, so the palette gains no entry and\n    no new snap target.\n\n    **Measured landscape/panel, which is the production config** — `daemon.ts`\n    sets `ORIENTATION = 'landscape'` and `ENVIRONMENT_EXTENT = 'panel'`. Worst\n    puff, best-reading pixel, against the real composed sky:\n\n      steam        7.76 dawn   10.64 day   5.06 dusk    8.96 night\n      dizzy stars  3.75 dawn    8.05 day   5.06 dusk   10.62 night\n\n    Better on dawn and day, level on dusk, and **lower on night** — the pale cell\n    carries night for both, and one pale cell in four reads less than one in\n    five. The floor is what matters and it is 5.06 at dusk, which is exactly\n    `dizzy`'s floor on the same sky: the corpus figure, already accepted.\n\n    **State the orientation with any contrast figure.** Landscape crops 32 raster\n    rows off the top, which slides the sprite a whole sky band relative to\n    `paintSky`, so the same art measured portrait moves on three of the four\n    skies — and *which* sky survives depends on the prop. Measured both ways:\n    this steam is invariant on **night** (8.96 either way), `dizzy`'s stars on\n    **dusk** (5.06 either way). Two harnesses disagreed over `dizzy` and matched\n    only on its invariant sky, so the single agreeing number was hiding a\n    systematic error rather than corroborating a method. An earlier version of\n    this paragraph reported that dusk-agreement as though it were a property of\n    *this* animation; it is not. A bare ratio with no config is not\n    reproducible. Declaring a colour makes it a snap target for every\n    other colour's edges, which is the defect the boot splash's wordmark,\n    `confused`'s caret and `dizzy`'s stars each hit in turn.\n\n    Opacity is only ever 0 or 1, flipped hard at the top of the rise the way\n    `asleep`'s Zs flip. Never a fade: partial opacity composites over the\n    background and snaps to transparent here, which is exactly what upstream's\n    smoke does and why it cannot come across.\n\n    ── Safe area ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    The pose creates headroom rather than spending it. Measured on the bake, the\n    topmost body-coloured pixel over the loop is at **unit y=8.25** — the fanning\n    claw at its -58deg extreme on frames 12 and 36 — a duplicate pair, not one\n    frame — and not a leg. The legs cannot be it:\n    they sit at y=9 and `heave` scales about y=15 with `scaleY` never above 1,\n    so they only ever move down. The steam tops out at **y=+1**, five units\n    inside the -4 line, where `dizzy` sits at -3 on 72 frames of 96.\n\n    Two earlier versions of this paragraph were wrong: one said the steam\n    reached y=2 with six units of margin, the other said the topmost body pixel\n    was a leg at y=9. Both were read off the keyframes instead of the bake, and\n    the second is 6 device pixels out.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* One puff, drawn at the origin; the keyframes move the group. */\n\n      /* Delays inside the shorthand, never as separate `animation-delay`\n         declarations. `docs/ANIMATION.md` §Timing, on carrying the offset inside\n         the shorthand: it *resets* delay, so a longhand only survives while it\n         sits below. This file had the separate form and it worked\n         only by source order — moving the two delay rules above the shorthand,\n         which is a cosmetic tidy and nothing else, collapses all three puffs\n         onto one phase and they rise in lockstep.\n         `svg2frames` goes from \"3 distinct delays\" to \"1\".\n\n         Nothing else notices. The reordered version still renders 46 of 48\n         distinct frames with no adjacent identical pair, the same duplicate\n         offsets, the same byte cost, and passes every gate in the repo. Only\n         looking at it shows it. Every other animation here carries the delay\n         inline; this was the one that did not. */\n      #fx-steam-a { animation: rise 3s infinite steps(1); }\n      #fx-steam-b { animation: rise 3s -1s infinite steps(1); }\n      #fx-steam-c { animation: rise 3s -2s infinite steps(1); }\n\n      /* Whole art pixels, and the last two cells are off rather than faded.\n         3s is 24 frames, so each of the eight steps holds 3 frames. */\n      @keyframes rise {\n        0%      { transform: translate(0, 0px);  opacity: 1; }\n        12.5%   { transform: translate(0, -1px); opacity: 1; }\n        25%     { transform: translate(0, -2px); opacity: 1; }\n        37.5%   { transform: translate(1px, -3px); opacity: 1; }\n        50%     { transform: translate(1px, -4px); opacity: 1; }\n        62.5%   { transform: translate(2px, -5px); opacity: 1; }\n        75%     { transform: translate(2px, -6px); opacity: 0; }\n        87.5%   { transform: translate(2px, -6px); opacity: 0; }\n        100%    { transform: translate(0, 0px);  opacity: 1; }\n      }\n\n      /* The labouring breath. Faster than `idle`'s 2s, which is what makes it\n         a labour rather than a rest, and about the torso's own bottom edge so\n         the body never leaves the ground — `docs/ANIMATION.md` §Animating the\n         whole sprite is not animating, and the rule `idle` had to be fixed\n         against. */\n      #heave {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 15px;\n        animation: heave 6s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* Four breaths in the loop, and no two the same: deepening to the\n         third and settling on the fourth. `asleep` does the same thing with\n         three breaths in twelve seconds and a sigh in the middle.\n\n         **Do not oversell that.** Measured on the bake, the torso goes 40 down\n         to 36, 35, 34 and 37 device pixels — so the breaths differ from one\n         another by one to three pixels, a second and a half apart, which is\n         under what a viewer reads as \"catching your breath\". What the varying\n         depth actually earns is frame distinctness, which is real and is the\n         paragraph below. The breath itself reads on its own terms: 6 device\n         pixels of squash at forty a minute is laboured, and that is the whole\n         of the claim.\n\n         It is also what earns the full six seconds, and it took two goes.\n         Written as `1.5s alternate` the track's period was 24 frames, the fan's\n         8 and the steam's 24 — every period divided 24, so frames 24-47 came\n         out byte-for-byte copies of 0-23 and only 24 of 48 frames were\n         distinct. Moving to a 6s track with one deeper breath fixed half of\n         that: 35 distinct, with all 13 remaining duplicate pairs at an offset\n         of exactly 24, because breaths two and four were still identical to\n         each other. Making every breath a different depth is what finally\n         separates the halves. Half a loop of duplicate frames is the defect\n         `dizzy` shipped and documented. */\n      @keyframes heave {\n        0%      { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n        12.5%   { transform: scale(1.04, 0.9); }\n        25%     { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n        37.5%   { transform: scale(1.05, 0.88); }\n        50%     { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n        62.5%   { transform: scale(1.06, 0.86); }\n        75%     { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n        87.5%   { transform: scale(1.03, 0.92); }\n        100%    { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n      }\n\n      /* Rotate *and* extend. §What a claw cannot do: a turned 2x2 is an\n         arrowhead, and length is what makes a claw read. `gym` reaches its\n         raised claw with rotate(-73deg) scaleX(2.843); this is the same move\n         at a fanning amplitude. */\n      #arm-fan {\n        transform-origin: 13px 13px;\n        animation: fan 0.5s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes fan {\n        from { transform: rotate(-25deg) scaleX(2.2); }\n        to   { transform: rotate(-58deg) scaleX(2.2); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── steam, rising off him ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->\n  <!-- Three cells dark, one pale. The dark carries the pale skies and the pale\n       carries night, which is the two-tone `dizzy` measured: no single flat\n       tone reads against both a day sky and a night one. -->\n  <g>\n    <g id=\"fx-steam-a\">\n      <rect x=\"4\" y=\"6\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"6\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"4\" y=\"7\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"7\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-steam-b\">\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"6\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n      <rect x=\"8\" y=\"6\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"7\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"8\" y=\"7\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"fx-steam-c\">\n      <rect x=\"10\" y=\"6\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n      <rect x=\"11\" y=\"6\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"10\" y=\"7\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <rect x=\"11\" y=\"7\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <g id=\"heave\">\n      <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <!-- The sploot: same x and width as the standing torso, 2 units\n             shorter, dropped 4 so its bottom sits on the ground line. -->\n        <rect id=\"torso-sploot\" x=\"2\" y=\"10\" width=\"11\" height=\"5\"/>\n        <!-- Splayed above the spread body, in the standing leg columns. Their\n             own ids, not the base `outer-left-leg` and friends: those carry\n             1x2 at y=13 in every other animation and in `base.svg`, and a\n             reader diffing a pose against the base has to be able to trust\n             that an id means the geometry it has always meant. -->\n        <g id=\"legs-sploot\">\n          <rect id=\"outer-left-leg-sploot\" x=\"3\" y=\"9\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect id=\"inner-left-leg-sploot\" x=\"5\" y=\"9\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect id=\"inner-right-leg-sploot\" x=\"9\" y=\"9\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n          <rect id=\"outer-right-leg-sploot\" x=\"11\" y=\"9\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <!-- Flat on the ground beside him, doing nothing. Base geometry is\n             2x2 at y=9 and this sits at y=13, so it is a variant and takes a\n             variant id like the rest. An earlier version kept the base id on\n             the grounds that a dropped rect is \"the same rect\" — but the base\n             ids are what a reader diffs a pose against, and 4 units is not the\n             same rect. `#right-arm` had the identical problem, undisclosed. -->\n        <rect id=\"left-arm-sploot\" x=\"0\" y=\"13\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"arm-fan\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <rect id=\"right-arm-fan\" x=\"13\" y=\"11\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n        <rect id=\"left-eye-squint\" x=\"4\" y=\"12\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n        <rect id=\"right-eye-squint\" x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","sweeping":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"12\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: sweeping (COMPACTING)\n\n    The context is filling up and he is tidying it. Plan:\n    assets/clawd/animations/PLANS.md §Sweeping.\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg. Every base element appears below at\n    its original coordinates and colours except `ground-shadow`, which\n    `docs/ANIMATION.md` §The base geometry forbids carrying. All motion,\n    including the eyes' look down and left, is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    ── Twelve seconds, because the window is minutes ─────────────────────\n\n    Measured from `compact_boundary` records: compaction ran 109s median on the\n    newest release present, 123s at its maximum there — a census of five, and\n    already six by the time it was re-checked, at 111.8s. PLANS.md §Sweeping is\n    the authority and flags its table as the census as taken; the point survives\n    a moving number, which is why the loop is sized on the order of magnitude. So the\n    loop is seen about **nine** times over. An earlier draft said thirteen, which\n    is 109/8 — left over from when this file was eight seconds — and also what\n    the pooled 150s median gives, a figure PLANS.md §Sweeping retires in as many\n    words because it describes no version.\n\n    That is the opposite of `board-game`, which is replaced at a **median** of\n    3.2s (p90 4.3s, max 9.6s — PLANS.md §Board game) and had to become a two\n    second punctuation mark. The rule both make: measure the window before\n    choosing the loop.\n\n    Twelve rather than eight because `docs/ANIMATION.md` says length is a cheap\n    way to buy variety, and the long-exposure screens sit there — `idle` at 16s,\n    `asleep`, `confused`, `dizzy` and `wizard` at 12s, against `thinking`,\n    `payoff` and `permission-sign` at 8s. The short loops are `board-game` and\n    `typing` at 2s, `gym` at 3s, `bouldering` at 4s and `overheated` at 6s.\n\n    ── Two periods, because one period is not a beat structure ───────────\n\n    **The length is only bought if something has a second period, and the first\n    draft of this file did not notice that it had none.** Its breath was the\n    corpus `2s alternate`, whose full cycle is four seconds — exactly the stroke,\n    exactly in phase. Rendered: frames 0-31 were byte-identical to 32-63, and\n    64-95 differed only at the two blink frames. **19 distinct images of 96**,\n    the lowest *ratio* in the corpus at 19.8%. Not the lowest count —\n    `permission-sign` holds 16 of 64 and `confused` 26 of 96, both deliberately\n    still screens. The comparison that matters is against the screens that also\n    move continuously: `asleep` 96/96, `idle` 123/128, `dizzy` 89/96, `wizard`\n    87/96. Twelve seconds of loop bought two frames of\n    content over four.\n\n    Two changes fix it, and the count is now **88 of 96**:\n\n      the breath is `1.5s alternate` — a three second cycle against a four\n      second stroke, so the combined period is the full twelve; **eight**\n      iterations in the loop, which is even, so the wrap lands where the cycle\n      started. `3s` was tried first and rejected; the rule on `#breathe` below\n      has the measurements and is the authority for this bullet. Check any\n      arithmetic about `data-loop-seconds` against 1.5s and eight, not 3s and\n      four — an earlier draft of this bullet certified the even-iteration check\n      against the period the file does not use.\n\n      the stroke is asymmetric — 1.6s out and 2.4s back, rather than a mirror\n      about its own midpoint. A symmetric stroke is the push played backwards,\n      so its second half is the first half re-rendered even once the breath is\n      decoupled. Measured on the bake: the head leads at **3 device pixels a\n      frame on the push and 2 on the recover**, mean 1.25.\n\n      Be clear what that buys and what it does not. The position samples are the\n      *same nine values* in both directions, so the recover is the push's path at\n      two thirds speed rather than a different action: it reads as directional,\n      not yet as effortful. The lever for the rest is the handle angle on the\n      return — lifting the broom off its line — which is a redraw rather than a\n      timing change.\n\n    ── Three strokes in the twelve, not one ──────────────────────────────\n\n    The first draft spent all twelve seconds on a single out-and-back, arguing\n    that a screen nobody asked for is an argument for calm — which is measured\n    rather than assumed: 18 of the 19 compactions in the census fired with\n    `compactMetadata.trigger` set to `auto`, and PLANS.md §Sweeping carries it. Right about the mood,\n    wrong about the arithmetic: a critic measured peak head speed at 1.24 device\n    pixels per frame, so the twelve seconds were spent slowing the only thing\n    that moved, and at true size the broom did not read as travelling.\n\n    So the stroke is four seconds and runs three times. Four divides twelve\n    exactly, and the whole out-and-back is written into one keyframe block rather\n    than left to `alternate`, so the loop cannot wrap mid-cycle whatever the loop\n    length becomes. Head travel is 2.5 units — 20 device pixels a half-stroke.\n\n    Calm is bought by the blink and by the breath being the slowest thing on the\n    screen, not by making the stroke hard to see.\n\n    ── A broom, and it does not share the arm's pivot ────────────────────\n\n    §Sweeping says to pick a broom or the swept material because both is two\n    props. The broom is authored **in floor coordinates and pivoted at its own\n    head**, which is the whole of why it works:\n\n      `#fx-broom`      translates along x only. The head is a rect whose bottom\n                       edge is y = 15, and translation cannot change that, so\n                       contact is exact by construction. Measured: the head\n                       occupies device rows 184 to 191 on all 96 frames, and 191\n                       is the legs' own lowest row.\n      `#fx-broom-tilt` rotates the handle about the head's centre, so the handle\n                       leans as the head travels while the head stays flat.\n      `#left-arm-joint` rotates to exactly `tilt - 90deg`, so the arm lies along\n                       the handle at every sample. That identity is why the claw\n                       cannot come off the handle: the two angles are one number.\n                       Check the pair before changing either.\n\n    A first draft instead hung the broom off the shoulder at a fixed radius and\n    claimed a shared pivot made contact free. It did the opposite — a head on a\n    fixed radius swings on an arc that lifts as it extends, so it was buried four\n    device pixels for 57 frames and up to five above the sand for 35 more, an\n    unbroken airborne run of 4.38 seconds. Sliding the broom along its own handle\n    fixes the tip and not the corners: a 1.5-unit bristle block tilted 40° off\n    vertical dips its lower corner 0.48 units, the same four pixels back again.\n    The pivot had to move, not the maths.\n\n    ── Where everything sits ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    The broom is a **sibling of `#breathe`, before it**, for the reason the legs\n    are: it touches the ground, and the ground does not breathe. Inside the\n    breathing group the head would sink 3.6 device pixels at each breath peak\n    while the feet beside it stayed planted.\n\n    The cost is that the grip drifts, because the arm breathes and the handle\n    does not. Measured perpendicular to the handle — the only direction that can\n    uncover it — the shoulder's 0.56-unit breath displacement resolves to **0.10\n    device pixels at the near end of the stroke and 2.01 at the far end**,\n    against a claw 16 pixels wide covering a handle 4 pixels thick. It stays\n    covered by a wide margin. An earlier draft asserted the grip \"cannot drift by\n    construction\" while the two were in different groups, which was false; this\n    states the number instead.\n\n    Draw order is document order: legs, broom, body. So the handle passes in\n    front of the legs and behind the torso and the claw, which is what makes the\n    grip read as a grip. The handle is a unit longer than it needs to be and its\n    **tip** is inside the torso on every frame — x 2.21 to 2.52 at the left top\n    corner and 2.97 at the right, against a torso whose left edge is 1.890 at\n    full breath. The overrun is not hidden at all, which two drafts got wrong in\n    opposite directions — the first claimed the torso covers it, the second put\n    numbers on it that no definition reproduces. Counting dark pixels in the four\n    columns beside the torso's left edge, above the claw *in that column*: **96 of 96 frames carry\n    some, at most 8, spanning unit x 1.500 to 2.000**, across up to three columns\n    rather than the one an earlier draft claimed. Composited on sand it reads\n    as the handle continuing up behind the grip, which is what it is — but a\n    sentence that has been wrong twice is worth stating exactly.\n\n    The sweep stays left of the legs at x 3 and well clear of the pool at\n    x 11.25, and the rock sits at y 6.63 to 8.75 — mid-distance, above all of\n    this. Nothing is drawn in row 0, row 199, column 0 or column 167; the drawn\n    extent is x 2 to 144, y 120 to 191, and the tightest margin is the head's\n    leading edge at column 2.\n\n    ── The keyframes are sampled, not eased ──────────────────────────────\n\n    Head position, handle angle and arm angle are related by an arctangent, so an\n    `ease-in-out` applied to all three independently would pull them apart\n    mid-stroke — the handle would lean at an angle its own head was not under.\n    Instead the true geometry is sampled at seventeen points with the ease\n    already baked into the sampled values, and the timing function is `linear`.\n\n    The error that matters is whether the handle still points at the shoulder,\n    which is the perpendicular distance from (2, 10) to the handle's centreline —\n    zero when `tan(tilt) = (2 - tx) / 4.5` holds, as it does at every sample. Its\n    worst value **between** samples is **0.038 device pixels**, a hundredth of the\n    handle's thickness. Because the arm is the tilt minus a constant, the arm\n    stays exactly parallel to the handle at every instant, so \"at the grip\" and\n    \"at the shoulder\" are one number rather than two.\n\n    An earlier draft published 0.38 px here for a nine-sample track whose real\n    figure was 0.131, and the interesting part is *why*, because the first\n    explanation offered for it was also wrong. It is not the lever arm. 0.38 is\n    a hybrid: it measures the linear track against the **ideally eased** curve —\n    a timing difference — and then converts it with the shortest lever. Under\n    that reading 0.38 is not even the worst value, since the same comparison\n    gives 0.97 px at the handle tip. And it cannot show up as a gap at the grip\n    at all, because the arm is the tilt minus a constant, so the claw's axis is\n    exactly parallel to the handle's and the two share a polyline.\n\n    The head's own path does part from the ideal cosine, by 0.70 px on that\n    track. That is a ripple in speed, not a joint coming apart, which is why the\n    figure quoted above is the coherence one.\n\n    ── Two tones, because one is invisible at some hour ──────────────────\n\n    Head `#C9D1D9`, handle `#000000`, measured against sand, which is what this\n    prop sits on: the pale is 3.32 / 1.71 / 4.27 / 8.68 at dawn, day, dusk and\n    night, and the dark is 4.21 / 8.19 / 3.28 / 1.61. The head carries at night\n    where the handle is 1.61:1; the handle carries by day where the head is\n    1.71:1. Either alone disappears at some hour.\n\n    No new colour: only `#000000`, `#C9D1D9` and `#DE886D`.\n\n    ── Only the head can fringe, and it is both of its edges ─────────────\n\n    An antialiased edge blends toward the composited background, which is black,\n    and a pale-to-black blend lands nearer `#DE886D` than either parent. The\n    **handle cannot do this at all**: it is `#000000` on a black background, so\n    its edge blends black into black however it turns. Only the head is pale.\n\n    The mechanism is worth naming exactly, because two drafts described it\n    wrongly. When the head's translate is off the pixel grid it covers **21 columns instead of 20**, of\n    which 19 render pale and the partly-covered one at each end snaps to\n    `#DE886D`. It is the head's own end columns going body-coloured, not a rim\n    added outside it — which is why the count below is 8 pixels per affected\n    edge and 16 when both go.\n\n    Measured against a variant with the broom deleted: **57 of 96 frames carry\n    peach the broom creates, at most 16 pixels** — 30 frames where **both**\n    vertical edges of the head go peach at 8 pixels each, and 27 where only one\n    does. The worst frame is 1: columns at unit x -0.375 and 2.125, rows unit\n    y 14.00 to 15.00. An earlier draft called this \"a single column ... one pixel\n    wide and eight tall\", which is 8 pixels and cannot be the 16 the same\n    sentence claimed; the reporting script indexed a list of non-empty frames\n    with an index into all 96.\n\n    Judged at 1x and 2x over all four grounds and left alone. Two remedies exist\n    and both cost more than the defect:\n\n      `#6F4436` in the palette puts a colour in the peach-to-black ramp so the\n      blend snaps there instead — which is why `payoff` and `typing` declare it —\n      but every peach-over-background edge on the crab then snaps there too. A\n      contour on the whole body to fix a ragged end on one prop.\n\n      whole-pixel `steps(1)` on the head, which `docs/ANIMATION.md` gives as the\n      general remedy, takes the fringe to zero. It needs a keyframe per frame,\n      because at 1.25 px a frame, nine keyframes over a four-second stroke\n      are one every four frames — and the old nine-sample track moved the head\n      2.93 to 7.07 pixels between samples, mean 5, which reads as stepping. That couples the file to the 8 fps\n      bake rate — which `wizard`'s `#fx-bob` already accepts, and\n      `docs/ANIMATION.md` names as the worked example of this very remedy. So\n      the objection is cost, not novelty: `#fx-bob` is eight positions on a\n      breath nobody tracks, and this would be thirty-two on the one element the\n      screen is about.\n\n    The rim reads as a slightly ragged end on the prop, not as the line\n    `bouldering` was reverted for — that was a one-pixel violet line across 80 of\n    the torso's 88 pixels, a foreign colour on the character rather than a\n    contour on its own edge.\n\n    ── The eyes do not follow, and that is a judgement ───────────────────\n\n    §Sweeping asks for a gaze that follows the stroke. Two earlier drafts gave\n    two wrong reasons for not doing it.\n\n    The first cited `board-game`, where stepping the eyes repainted 512 pixels\n    against a beat of 128 and made the face the subject of its own screen. **That\n    arithmetic does not apply here**: the broom is the largest moving thing on\n    the panel and two stepped eyes would be lost against it.\n\n    The second said the arm forbids it. That is true of one direction only.\n    `#fx-eyes-look` puts the left eye at unit x 3 to 4, and the arm's near corner\n    reaches x 2.976 — so tracking *further left* has 0.02 of a unit to spend and\n    walks the eye into the arm. But tracking the other way is free: hold the eye\n    at its base x 4 to 5 at the near end and step it left to 3 to 4 at the far\n    end, which clears the arm by 1.02 units and 0.21 respectively, on a whole-unit\n    step at a period that divides the loop.\n\n    So it was built and rendered. **It reads worse.** At the near end the eye sits\n    centred in the head and Clawd looks straight ahead — at the one moment in the\n    stroke when the broom is closest to him and the gaze has least excuse. A gaze\n    that tracks the prop for two thirds of a stroke and abandons it for the rest\n    is worse than one that never leaves it. Static, down and left onto the head,\n    reads on every frame.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* One frame of the twelve second loop is 1.0416667%; one frame of the\n         four second stroke is 3.125%. The stroke's samples are not evenly\n         spaced — 5% apart on the push and 7.5% on the recover — because the\n         stroke is asymmetric. See the comment on `sweep`. */\n\n      /* **1.5 seconds, not the corpus two, and that is the whole beat\n         structure.** `2s alternate` is a four second cycle, which is the stroke\n         exactly and in phase with it, and the file rendered 19 distinct frames\n         of 96 — see §Two periods above. 1.5s is a three second cycle against a\n         four second stroke, so the combined period is the full twelve; eight\n         iterations in the loop, which is even, so the wrap lands where the cycle\n         started.\n\n         **3s was tried first, and it is the wrong direction.** It decouples the\n         periods just as well and scores marginally better on distinct frames —\n         92 against 88 — but it makes this the slowest breath in the corpus by\n         half again, and `asleep` is a four second cycle. A crab doing manual\n         work would have been breathing slower than the sleeping one. Measured on\n         the torso's top row: at 1.5s this breathes at **3.33 device pixels a\n         second against `asleep`'s 2.67**, with a **5 pixel excursion against its\n         8**. Faster than sleep, which is what exertion looks like.\n\n         The depth comparison needs a caveat: `asleep`'s 8 pixels is its *sigh*,\n         a second track at frames 34-50, and its resting breath is 4 pixels on a\n         four second cycle. Against that, this is a pixel **deeper**, not\n         shallower — a one-track breath set against a two-track one. The rate\n         comparison is the one the decision rests on, and it is\n         conservative rather than like for like: `asleep`'s 2.67 is a loop mean\n         that *includes* the sigh, so its resting rate is 2.0 and the real margin\n         is wider than this sentence claims.\n         Four frames of distinctness are worth less than getting that the right\n         way round.\n\n         Legs and broom are siblings of this group so they stay planted. */\n      #breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 1.5s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        from { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        to   { transform: scale(1.02, 0.98) translate(0, 0.5px); }\n      }\n\n      /* The head slides along the floor. Translation only — this is what makes\n         contact exact rather than approximate: the head rect's bottom edge is\n         y = 15 as authored, and no translate in x can change that.\n\n         The samples are asymmetric on purpose: 0 to 40% is the push and 40 to\n         100% the recover, so the second half of the stroke is not the first half\n         played backwards. A mirror-symmetric stroke costs half the loop's\n         distinct frames, which is how this file came to render 19 of 96. */\n      #fx-broom {\n        animation: sweep 4s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes sweep {\n        0.000000% { transform: translateX(1px); }\n        5.000000% { transform: translateX(0.9048px); }\n        10.000000% { transform: translateX(0.6339px); }\n        15.000000% { transform: translateX(0.2284px); }\n        20.000000% { transform: translateX(-0.25px); }\n        25.000000% { transform: translateX(-0.7284px); }\n        30.000000% { transform: translateX(-1.1339px); }\n        35.000000% { transform: translateX(-1.4048px); }\n        40.000000% { transform: translateX(-1.5px); }\n        47.500000% { transform: translateX(-1.4048px); }\n        55.000000% { transform: translateX(-1.1339px); }\n        62.500000% { transform: translateX(-0.7284px); }\n        70.000000% { transform: translateX(-0.25px); }\n        77.500000% { transform: translateX(0.2284px); }\n        85.000000% { transform: translateX(0.6339px); }\n        92.500000% { transform: translateX(0.9048px); }\n        100.000000% { transform: translateX(1px); }\n      }\n\n      /* The handle leans, about the head's own centre, so the head does not\n         care what the handle is doing. */\n      #fx-broom-tilt {\n        transform-origin: 0px 14.5px;\n        animation: tilt 4s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes tilt {\n        0.000000% { transform: rotate(12.5288deg); }\n        5.000000% { transform: rotate(13.678deg); }\n        10.000000% { transform: rotate(16.8873deg); }\n        15.000000% { transform: rotate(21.4895deg); }\n        20.000000% { transform: rotate(26.5651deg); }\n        25.000000% { transform: rotate(31.2285deg); }\n        30.000000% { transform: rotate(34.8541deg); }\n        35.000000% { transform: rotate(37.1124deg); }\n        40.000000% { transform: rotate(37.875deg); }\n        47.500000% { transform: rotate(37.1124deg); }\n        55.000000% { transform: rotate(34.8541deg); }\n        62.500000% { transform: rotate(31.2285deg); }\n        70.000000% { transform: rotate(26.5651deg); }\n        77.500000% { transform: rotate(21.4895deg); }\n        85.000000% { transform: rotate(16.8873deg); }\n        92.500000% { transform: rotate(13.678deg); }\n        100.000000% { transform: rotate(12.5288deg); }\n      }\n\n      /* Every angle here is its `tilt` partner minus 90 degrees, which is what\n         lays the arm along the handle. Check the pair before changing either:\n         they are one number written twice, and a stray digit is a claw holding\n         nothing. No `scaleX` — the arm is 2 units and the handle is over 4 at\n         its shortest, so the grip is inside it without stretching. */\n      #left-arm-joint {\n        transform-origin: 2px 10px;\n        animation: sweep-arm 4s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes sweep-arm {\n        0.000000% { transform: rotate(-77.4712deg); }\n        5.000000% { transform: rotate(-76.322deg); }\n        10.000000% { transform: rotate(-73.1127deg); }\n        15.000000% { transform: rotate(-68.5105deg); }\n        20.000000% { transform: rotate(-63.4349deg); }\n        25.000000% { transform: rotate(-58.7715deg); }\n        30.000000% { transform: rotate(-55.1459deg); }\n        35.000000% { transform: rotate(-52.8876deg); }\n        40.000000% { transform: rotate(-52.125deg); }\n        47.500000% { transform: rotate(-52.8876deg); }\n        55.000000% { transform: rotate(-55.1459deg); }\n        62.500000% { transform: rotate(-58.7715deg); }\n        70.000000% { transform: rotate(-63.4349deg); }\n        77.500000% { transform: rotate(-68.5105deg); }\n        85.000000% { transform: rotate(-73.1127deg); }\n        92.500000% { transform: rotate(-76.322deg); }\n        100.000000% { transform: rotate(-77.4712deg); }\n      }\n\n      /* Down and left, onto the head, and static — see the note above on why\n         it does not follow, which is a judgement and not a constraint.\n\n         On its own wrapper, which is `idle`'s convention, rather than on\n         `#eyes-color-group`. The usual reason given is that the colour group is\n         the pack recolour hook, and that reason does not survive checking twice\n         over: `wizard` puts its own eye-look transform straight onto\n         `#eyes-color-group`, so the corpus does not hold the preference\n         uniformly; and the hook itself is documented but not built — `packPalette`\n         maps a manifest's band colours and nothing else, sprites ship as baked\n         RGB565, and PLANS.md says outright that there is no mechanism by which a\n         pack changes a sprite's colours. So this is a naming convention worth\n         keeping for consistency, not a mechanism being protected.\n\n         A whole unit, so it cannot land off the grid. */\n      #fx-eyes-look {\n        transform: translate(-1px, 1px);\n      }\n\n      /* One slow blink in twelve seconds. `#eyes-blink` existed with an id and\n         no rule at all, so nothing blinked across a screen that holds for two\n         minutes — the stroke was the only motion, and one repeating stroke is\n         not a beat structure. Squashed about the eyes' own centre, never\n         hidden, and\n         `linear`, because a lid that accelerates reads as a wince. The\n         percentages are frames 76 to 79 of 96, which lands the blink inside the\n         third stroke rather than on a stroke boundary. */\n      /* **10px, not the corpus 9px**, and the difference is the nesting.\n         `#fx-eyes-look` sits *inside* this group and translates the eyes a unit\n         down, so they occupy y 9 to 11 and the corpus eye line at y=9 is their\n         top edge here rather than their centre. Left at 9 the lid collapsed\n         upward into the brow: measured, the closed eye sat at device rows 148-149\n         against an open socket of 148-163, a 7 pixel drift. At 10 the drift is\n         0.0 and it closes about itself. Any file that both translates the eyes\n         and blinks them has to do this arithmetic; `wizard` escapes it because\n         its look transform is x only. */\n      #eyes-blink {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 10px;\n        animation: blink 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes blink {\n        0%, 79.166667%   { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        80.208333%       { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        81.250000%       { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        82.291667%, 100% { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <!-- The broom, in floor coordinates: the head's bottom edge is y = 15 as\n         written, and nothing above ever changes it. The handle is drawn first\n         and the head second, so the head covers the half unit of handle\n         embedded in it — that embedded half is what stops the handle's corner\n         opening a notch of sand when it leans. -->\n    <g id=\"fx-broom\">\n      <g id=\"fx-broom-tilt\">\n        <rect id=\"fx-broom-handle\" x=\"-0.25\" y=\"7.799\" width=\"0.5\" height=\"6.701\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n      <\/g>\n      <rect id=\"fx-broom-head\" x=\"-1.25\" y=\"14\" width=\"2.5\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"breathe\">\n      <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n        <g id=\"left-arm-joint\">\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"eyes-blink\">\n        <g id=\"fx-eyes-look\">\n          <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n            <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","payoff":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"8\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: the payoff (DONE)\n\n    A task actually finished. The vehicle is parked beside him and he rests a\n    claw on it. Plan: assets/clawd/animations/PLANS.md §The payoff screen.\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg, with two departures from the\n    boilerplate the other files carry: `ground-shadow` is not here, because\n    `docs/ANIMATION.md` §The base geometry forbids carrying it, and names the\n    renderer as what draws the one you actually see; and\n    the eyes are one unit left of their base coordinates, looking at the\n    vehicle. Everything else is at its original coordinates and colours, and\n    all motion is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    ── Where the vehicle goes, and why it is not where the plan said ──────\n\n    The plan sized it at 5 units from `L + 15 + c <= 21`, which holds only if\n    the character is flush against a stage edge. He is not: the stage spans\n    x -3 to 18 and he occupies 0 to 15, so the free width is 3 units each\n    side, non-contiguous. Three units was rendered at true size and reads as a\n    block with two dots.\n\n    The right flank is water — `environment.ts` puts the rock pool at units\n    x 11.25 to 17.50, in both orientations — so anything parked there is\n    parked in it. The left rock\n    sits at y 6.6-8.75, well clear of the ground, so the left flank is the\n    only one free.\n\n    And he cannot move: docs/ANIMATION.md says \"Keep the character's own\n    coordinates untouched. Grow the stage instead\", and there is nowhere to\n    grow it to: the stage is 21 units and the panel's ceiling is 21.5 at 8px a\n    unit, so the half unit of slack is the 2px margin each side, and `environment.ts` draws the contact\n    shadow at a hard-wired offset from the slot — shift him and he floats.\n\n    ── The brown, and the trap the plan named ────────────────────────────\n\n    `fx-vehicle-shade` is #6F4436 and it is a drawn element on purpose.\n    `paletteOf` matches `/fill\\s*[:=]\\s*\"?(#[0-9a-fA-F]{6})/`, so a hex in a\n    comment is not a declaration — PLANS.md names that trap, and the first\n    version of this file fell into a worse form of it, writing the hex in\n    prose where the regex could never see it.\n\n    What that cost, measured: without the brown, an antialiased torso edge\n    between 36% and 71% coverage snaps to the *vehicle's* colour — 36 of the\n    101 steps — because that is then the nearest thing in the document's\n    palette. \"Body colour\" would mean Clawd's peach here, which is the id\n    `base.svg` gives it. And 59 of 64 frames\n    carried red outside the vehicle — a line across the whole torso top, both\n    flanks, boxes around each eye, and a stripe severing the right arm from\n    the body. It is the `bouldering` violet-line defect at four times the\n    scale. #6F4436 is half of the peach in red and green exactly and within a\n    half-step in blue, so it takes the mid-coverage band and reads as shading,\n    which is why `typing.svg` declares it too.\n\n    **And it does land on him — say so, because the fix is not \"no rim\".**\n    56 of 64 frames carry #6F4436 outside the vehicle, up to 311 pixels, along\n    the same torso top and flanks the red traced. That is the trade, not a\n    residue of it: the brown takes 50 of the 101 coverage steps itself, so the\n    snapping does not stop, it moves. Judged at true size on all four sands it\n    reads as a contour, which is an ordinary pixel-art device, where the red\n    read as a foreign object drawn over the creature. The legs are clean\n    either way, because static geometry never antialiases.\n\n    ── The claw, and what makes a prop read ──────────────────────────────\n\n    The pivot is the shoulder at (2,10), which is where the joint is. The\n    first version pivoted at (0,10) — the claw's own free tip — so `scaleX`\n    extended it *inward*, hiding all 1.2 units of the extension under the\n    torso and turning the block into a wedge that read as a crack in the\n    sprite.\n\n    Rotating -38 degrees about the shoulder, extended, puts the tip at\n    (-0.52, 11.97), which is inside the cabin. That is the point:\n    §Props need contact says a prop that touches reads and a prop that hovers\n    does not, and the first version touched nothing on any frame — the claw\n    and the vehicle shared neither a row nor a column, and the only \"overlap\"\n    was eight device pixels of shared boundary between two edges.\n\n    ── The loop ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    The vehicle never moves. `frameAt` is wall-clock modulo the frame count,\n    so a state never begins at frame 0 and an arrival would re-arrive every\n    loop at a uniformly random phase — `permission-sign` found this and wrote\n    it down. Parked on frame 0, and the loop is his reaction.\n\n    The legs sit outside `#breathe`, as `idle` and `permission-sign` do.\n    Inside it the whole sprite bobbed three device pixels and the feet\n    descended into their own contact shadow twice a second, which is\n    §Animating the whole sprite is not animating.\n\n    Every keyframe lands on a whole frame: 1.5625% each at 64 frames, so the\n    gesture uses 18.75, 31.25, 45.3125 and 56.25 — frames 12, 20, 29 and 36.\n\n    Eyes one pixel left, toward it. One slow blink, `permission-sign`'s\n    pattern and its timing.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* Whole art pixels throughout. One frame of the eight second loop is\n         1.5625%. */\n\n      /* `idle`'s breath: two seconds alternating, about the hips, so the body\n         settles onto planted legs rather than lifting off them. The legs are\n         a sibling of this group, so they stay planted. */\n      #breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 2s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        from { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        to   { transform: scale(1.02, 0.98) translate(0, 0.5px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Squashed about the eye line, never hidden — §Closing an eye. Linear,\n         because a lid that accelerates reads as a wince. Timings copied from\n         `permission-sign`, and checked against this loop's frame grid. */\n      #eyes-blink {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 9px;\n        animation: blink 8s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes blink {\n        0%, 62.5%      { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        64.0625%       { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        65.625%        { transform: scaleY(0.1); }\n        67.1875%, 100% { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n\n      /* The reaction beat: a claw laid on the vehicle and taken back. Pivoted\n         at the shoulder, rotating *and* extending — §What a claw cannot do\n         rules out a bare rotation, and `gym` shows the pair is what makes a\n         limb read as a limb rather than a lozenge sliding on the ribs. */\n      #left-arm-joint {\n        transform-origin: 2px 10px;\n        animation: gesture 8s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      @keyframes gesture {\n        0%, 18.75%      { transform: rotate(0deg) scaleX(1); }\n        31.25%, 45.3125% { transform: rotate(-38deg) scaleX(1.6); }\n        56.25%, 100%    { transform: rotate(0deg) scaleX(1); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- The vehicle. Parked, never moves, drawn before the body so the claw\n       laid on it is visibly in front. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-vehicle\">\n    <rect id=\"fx-vehicle-body\" x=\"-3\" y=\"12\" width=\"5\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#B22222\"/>\n    <rect id=\"fx-vehicle-cabin\" x=\"-2.75\" y=\"10.5\" width=\"2.5\" height=\"1.5\" fill=\"#B22222\"/>\n    <rect id=\"fx-vehicle-window\" x=\"-2.4\" y=\"11\" width=\"1.8\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <!-- The declared brown, drawn: a shaded sill along the vehicle's\n         underside. See the note above on why this is an element and not a\n         comment. -->\n    <rect id=\"fx-vehicle-shade\" x=\"-3\" y=\"13.5\" width=\"5\" height=\"0.5\" fill=\"#6F4436\"/>\n    <rect id=\"fx-vehicle-front-wheel\" x=\"-2.5\" y=\"14\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n    <rect id=\"fx-vehicle-rear-wheel\" x=\"0.5\" y=\"14\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"breathe\">\n      <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n        <g id=\"left-arm-joint\">\n          <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n        <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <\/g>\n\n      <g id=\"eyes-blink\">\n        <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n          <!-- One art pixel left, toward the vehicle. §Eyes. -->\n          <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"3\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"9\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","idle":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"16\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: idle (connected, no session working)\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg. Every base element appears below\n    at its original coordinates and colours; all motion is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    This is the screen that is on most of the time — Claude is idle far more\n    than it is running — so it is seen more than every working animation\n    combined, and it is the one that has to survive being looked at for hours.\n\n    Structured after upstream clawd-tank's clawd-idle-living.svg, which solves\n    the same problem the same way: one long loop with occasional beats rather\n    than a pool of separate files. That needs no state machine at all — the\n    variety lives in the animation instead of in the daemon. Sixteen seconds,\n    128 frames: breathe throughout, blink three times, look right, look left,\n    and once per loop a proper yawn.\n\n    ── Why this file was rebuilt ──────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    The previous version banned rotation, non-uniform scale and easing so that\n    every edge would land on a whole device pixel, and it read as a sprite\n    being nudged about rather than a creature moving. That ban is gone.\n    `tools/svg2frames.ts` now snaps every rasterised pixel to the declared\n    palette, which collapses an antialiased diagonal back onto one side or the\n    other after the fact. The edge still lands on a whole pixel; it is simply\n    no longer the author's job to guarantee it. So this animates with easing,\n    rotation and squash-and-stretch, exactly as upstream does.\n\n    The old file also carried the bug this rebuild exists to kill: #fx-mouth\n    was named in the breathing rule *and* given its own rule underneath. The\n    `animation` shorthand replaces, it does not merge, so the later rule won\n    and the mouth silently lost the breathing — during the yawn it hung eight\n    device pixels below the torso. The fix is structural rather than careful:\n    nothing here carries two animations. Concurrent motion is expressed by\n    nesting groups, so the transforms compose and no declaration can shadow\n    another.\n\n    ── The rule the silhouette is held to ─────────────────────────────────\n\n    The body stays in one piece. The legs are the only part that stands\n    outside the moving groups, so every other track is measured against them:\n    the torso may deform above its planted feet but may not float off them,\n    and a claw may swing but may not leave the torso's edge. A crab rendered\n    as several disconnected slabs does not read as a crab mid-gesture, it\n    reads as a corrupted sprite — which on a panel that also shows genuine\n    display faults is the one misreading that costs something.\n\n    The single beat that breaks contact on purpose is the yawn stretch, where\n    the body lengthens up off the feet for about a second. That is the whole\n    point of a stretch, upstream does the same, and it is short. Everywhere\n    else the deformation is anchored: `breathe` compresses and *sinks* rather\n    than lifting, so the hips press into the legs at the far end of the breath\n    instead of parting from them, and every yawn arm pose keeps the claw\n    overlapping the torso rather than thrown clear of it.\n\n    ── How it is put together ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Three nested groups, slowest outermost, so the tracks multiply instead of\n    fighting over one `transform`:\n\n      #fx-lean     16s   weight shift for the looking-about beats, passed\n                         through rather than held; the body\n                         carries over while the legs stay where they are\n      #fx-action   16s   the yawn: stretch up, then the heavy drop, pivoted at\n                         the torso base (7.5,13)\n      #fx-breathe   2s   continuous, alternating, so a full breath is four\n                         seconds and four of them fit the loop exactly\n\n    The legs sit outside all three, planted. What reads as breathing is the\n    torso deforming above them — 2% wider for 2% shorter, settling half a unit\n    into its own feet — not the whole sprite moving, which would shift a rigid\n    block and deform nothing. These are upstream's numbers unchanged, because\n    the sign is what matters: a breath that lifted the torso instead left the\n    legs behind as four floating stubs for most of the loop.\n\n    This used to describe a shadow narrowing and paling with the breath, kept\n    on the theory that a pack setting a lighter stage would get it for free.\n    That never could arrive: the bake is taken against black and the tone is\n    dropped there, so a lighter stage at runtime has nothing left to reveal.\n    The renderer draws the shadow now — see `paintContactShadow`.\n\n    Arms hang off shoulder pivots at (2,10) and (13,10), each with its own\n    slow sway nested inside the yawn throw, half a period apart so the claws\n    are never quite in step. Blinks squash the eyes to 0.15 of their height\n    about the eye line at y=9 rather than hiding them — a vanished eye reads\n    as a rendering fault, a flattened one reads as a lid. A tenth was tried\n    and lands a 16px eye on a single device row, which is the floor of\n    legibility rather than a lid; 0.15 resolves to two rows reliably.\n\n    Two elements are additive, both borrowed from upstream. The yawn mouth is\n    a 3x2 rect at (6,10) scaled out of nothing and back, because the base\n    geometry has no mouth and this is how you give him one without touching\n    it. The tear is a single pixel at the outer corner of the left eye, in\n    upstream's #40C4FF, declared as a literal fill so the palette snap keeps\n    it — and parented *inside* #fx-eyes-look, so it falls from wherever the\n    eye currently is. As a sibling it stayed put while the eyes tracked and\n    read as a loose cyan pixel on the cheek.\n\n    ── Loop discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    16s at 8fps = 128 frames, so one frame is 0.78125% and every keyframe\n    below is a whole multiple of it. Both continuous tracks divide the loop an\n    even number of times — breathing 2s x 8, sway 1.6s x 10 — which `alternate`\n    requires: an odd count would end the loop on the far end of the cycle and\n    seam at the wrap. Opacity flips are placed on adjacent frames so no frame\n    ever samples a half-faded mouth. Frames are sampled exactly on frame\n    boundaries, which is also what lets the claw rotation skip the angles it\n    cannot render cleanly — see arm-l-yawn.\n\n    Beats, in seconds: blink at 1.5; look right 3.0-5.0 with a blink at 4.0\n    inside it; look left 7.0-8.5 with a blink at 7.75; yawn 9.5-13.0 — arms up\n    at 9.75, eyes shut at 9.875, mouth wide at 10.625, a tear at 10.875, the\n    drop at 12.0, settled by 13.0. The blinks sit inside the look beats on\n    purpose: the gaze holds for two seconds, and a hold with nothing in it is\n    the one stretch of this loop that reads as a stalled frame rather than a\n    calm one.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* One animation per element, one element per rule. See the header. */\n\n\n      #fx-lean {\n        animation: lean-look 16s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      #fx-action {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: yawn-body 16s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      #fx-breathe {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 13px;\n        animation: breathe 2s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      #fx-arm-l {\n        transform-origin: 2px 10px;\n        animation: arm-l-yawn 16s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      #fx-arm-r {\n        transform-origin: 13px 10px;\n        animation: arm-r-yawn 16s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* Nested inside the throw, and half a period out of step with each\n         other, so the claws idle independently of the breath and of one\n         another. The offset rides in the shorthand rather than in a separate\n         `animation-delay`, which the shorthand would otherwise reset. */\n      #fx-arm-l-sway {\n        animation: claw-sway 1.6s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      #fx-arm-r-sway {\n        animation: claw-sway 1.6s -0.8s infinite alternate ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      #fx-eyes-look {\n        animation: eye-track 16s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* Linear, not eased: a lid that accelerates reads as a wince. */\n      #fx-eyes-blink {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 9px;\n        animation: eye-blink 16s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #fx-mouth {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 11px;\n        opacity: 0;\n        animation: yawn-mouth 16s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      #fx-tear {\n        opacity: 0;\n        animation: tear-fall 16s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* ── keyframes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\n      /* Upstream's breath, sign included: 2% wider for 2% shorter, settling\n         half a unit down. Pivoted at the torso base, so the half unit presses\n         the hips *into* the legs — the far end of the breath overlaps them by\n         four device pixels rather than parting from them. Inverting this to a\n         lift is what left the legs behind as free-floating stubs on 78 of the\n         128 frames, and it is not a subtle failure at 8px per unit: 0.3 units\n         of lift is a 2-3 pixel band of black under a body that has nothing\n         holding it up. */\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        from { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        to   { transform: scale(1.02, 0.98) translate(0, 0.5px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Looking about is a weight shift: the body carries over while the legs\n         stay put, and keeps drifting a little further through the hold rather\n         than freezing where it landed.\n\n         This deliberately does not rotate. A 2.5deg tilt was tried and looked\n         broken: the torso is a 88x56 device-pixel slab, and snapping a shallow\n         diagonal to the palette turns its outline into a lumpy staircase that\n         reads as a corrupted sprite rather than as a lean. Rotation earns its\n         place on the claws, which are small enough and turned far enough for\n         the result to be a shape — see arm-l-yawn. */\n      /* Two device pixels across, one down, both whole. The lean is passed\n         through rather than parked in: the previous version ramped to 0.95\n         units — 7.6 device pixels, not a whole one — and then sat within a\n         pixel of that extreme for fifteen frames, nearly two seconds. What\n         reads at that offset is not a lean, it is legs bolted on askew, and\n         `thinking` shipped the same mistake more visibly. A lean is a moment. */\n      @keyframes lean-look {\n        0%, 12.5%       { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        21.875%         { transform: translate(0.25px, 0.125px); }\n        37.5%           { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        46.875%         { transform: translate(-0.25px, 0.125px); }\n        59.375%, 100%   { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      }\n\n      /* The yawn. Rise slowly over a second and a quarter, hold the stretch,\n         then drop in half a second — the asymmetry is the weight. The 1.07 x\n         0.92 landing widens as it shortens; the small overshoot after it is\n         the body settling rather than stopping dead. That overshoot lengthens\n         the torso without translating it, so the settle happens above feet the\n         body is still standing on. */\n      /* No upward translate anywhere in the stretch, and that is the whole\n         fix. `transform-origin` is already 7.5px 13px — the torso's own bottom\n         edge — so `scaleY` above 1 grows the body upward from a bottom that\n         does not move, which is a stretch. The three `translate(0, -Npx)`\n         values that used to sit beside those scales moved the whole body up\n         instead, one device pixel per 0.125 units, and the legs are outside\n         this group: they stayed put. That opened up to nine device pixels of\n         daylight under the hips on 14 of 128 frames — four free-floating stubs\n         under a levitating crab, for nearly two seconds of every loop.\n\n         `thinking` shipped this same defect at eleven device pixels on 50 of\n         64 frames, diagnosed it, and fixed it exactly this way; its comment\n         records \"the bottom edge is the pivot, so it does not move at all\".\n         `docs/ANIMATION.md` disagreed until 22 Aug, on a sentence that\n         contradicted itself in one breath, and this file was the last thing\n         still following it.\n\n         The 0.92/1.12 scales are untouched: at 1.12 the 7-unit torso grows to\n         7.84, which is most of an art pixel of stretch and is what a yawn\n         actually is. The downward `+0.5px` at 75% stays — sinking pushes the\n         hips *into* the legs, which is the safe direction. */\n      @keyframes yawn-body {\n        0%, 59.375%     { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n        62.5%           { transform: scale(0.98, 1.05) translate(0, 0); }\n        66.40625%       { transform: scale(0.94, 1.12) translate(0, 0); }\n        71.875%         { transform: scale(0.95, 1.1) translate(0, 0); }\n        75%             { transform: scale(1.07, 0.92) translate(0, 0.5px); }\n        79.6875%        { transform: scale(0.99, 1.02) translate(0, 0); }\n        83.59375%, 100% { transform: scale(1, 1) translate(0, 0); }\n      }\n\n\n      /* Up to 45deg, where a 2x2 claw renders as a diamond and reads as a\n         shape rather than as a square that has moved. Then it flops the same\n         45deg the other way, because a stretch that stops exactly where it\n         started has no weight in it — and because the two angles a 2x2 claw\n         can hold cleanly are the square and the diamond. Everything between\n         them staircases, so the turn is keyed one frame at a time through the\n         angles it cannot hold: 0 -> 22 -> 45 on the way up, 45 -> 0 -> -45 on\n         the drop. Frames are sampled on frame boundaries, so a keyframe one\n         frame from its neighbour is rendered and never interpolated, and only\n         two frames of the loop carry an angle that staircases.\n\n         The claw stays welded to the torso throughout. The reach was\n         `translate(-1.2px, -4px)` and that put a 4-pixel black gap between\n         claw and shoulder: seven disconnected pieces on screen for two full\n         seconds, with the claws level with the top of the head, which reads\n         as ears rather than arms. The diamond now overlaps the torso's upper\n         corner by half a unit at every pose, and the rise that survived the\n         cut is carried by the translate while the rotation holds at 45deg. */\n      @keyframes arm-l-yawn {\n        0%, 59.375%     { transform: translate(0, 0) rotate(0deg); }\n        60.15625%       { transform: translate(-0.1px, -1px) rotate(22deg); }\n        60.9375%        { transform: translate(-0.2px, -1.2px) rotate(45deg); }\n        66.40625%       { transform: translate(-0.2px, -1.6px) rotate(45deg); }\n        71.875%         { transform: translate(-0.2px, -1.5px) rotate(45deg); }\n        72.65625%       { transform: translate(-0.15px, -1.15px) rotate(45deg); }\n        73.4375%        { transform: translate(0.05px, -0.6px) rotate(0deg); }\n        74.21875%       { transform: translate(0.2px, 0.8px) rotate(-45deg); }\n        79.6875%        { transform: translate(0.05px, 0.3px) rotate(-45deg); }\n        80.46875%       { transform: translate(0.02px, 0.15px) rotate(-22deg); }\n        81.25%, 100%    { transform: translate(0, 0) rotate(0deg); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes arm-r-yawn {\n        0%, 59.375%     { transform: translate(0, 0) rotate(0deg); }\n        60.15625%       { transform: translate(0.1px, -1px) rotate(-22deg); }\n        60.9375%        { transform: translate(0.2px, -1.2px) rotate(-45deg); }\n        66.40625%       { transform: translate(0.2px, -1.6px) rotate(-45deg); }\n        71.875%         { transform: translate(0.2px, -1.5px) rotate(-45deg); }\n        72.65625%       { transform: translate(0.15px, -1.15px) rotate(-45deg); }\n        73.4375%        { transform: translate(-0.05px, -0.6px) rotate(0deg); }\n        74.21875%       { transform: translate(-0.2px, 0.8px) rotate(45deg); }\n        79.6875%        { transform: translate(-0.05px, 0.3px) rotate(45deg); }\n        80.46875%       { transform: translate(-0.02px, 0.15px) rotate(22deg); }\n        81.25%, 100%    { transform: translate(0, 0) rotate(0deg); }\n      }\n\n      /* The claws lift and settle on a 1.6s alternating cycle — a 3.2s round\n         trip that does not divide the 4s breath, so the two drift in and out\n         of step and no two seconds of the idle look quite alike. One track,\n         two users: the right claw takes it half a period late, which is where\n         the asymmetry comes from.\n\n         A shallow rotation was tried here too and had the same problem the\n         body lean did, at 16 device pixels square. This is a translate — and\n         a vertical one only: a sideways reach of a fifth of a unit opened a\n         two-pixel black gap between claw and torso, which reads as the arm\n         having come off. Sliding along the torso's edge keeps the shoulder\n         covered whatever the claw does. */\n      @keyframes claw-sway {\n        from { transform: translateY(0.12px); }\n        to   { transform: translateY(-0.2px); }\n      }\n\n      /* One unit — a whole eye's width — is the travel. It was 1.5, which put\n         the outer eye half a unit from the edge of the head: four device\n         pixels of cheek against twenty-eight on the other side, which reads\n         as an eye sliding off the face rather than as a glance. A whole unit\n         still moves the eye its own width, and leaves a whole art pixel of\n         head outside it. */\n      @keyframes eye-track {\n        0%, 14.0625%     { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        18.75%, 31.25%   { transform: translate(1px, 0); }\n        35.9375%         { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        43.75%, 53.125%  { transform: translate(-1px, 0); }\n        57.8125%         { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n        62.5%, 71.875%   { transform: translate(0, -0.4px); }\n        75%, 100%        { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      }\n\n      /* Two frames shut per blink. One is a flicker at 8fps, three is a doze.\n         Two of the three land inside a look hold, which is where the loop is\n         otherwise stillest. The long shut in the middle is the yawn, and it\n         is the only one keyed to close over two frames rather than one — a\n         yawn's eyes close slowly. */\n      @keyframes eye-blink {\n        0%, 8.59375%          { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        9.375%, 10.15625%     { transform: scaleY(0.15); }\n        10.9375%, 24.21875%   { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        25%, 25.78125%        { transform: scaleY(0.15); }\n        26.5625%, 47.65625%   { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        48.4375%, 49.21875%   { transform: scaleY(0.15); }\n        50%, 60.15625%        { transform: scaleY(1); }\n        61.71875%, 72.65625%  { transform: scaleY(0.15); }\n        74.21875%, 100%       { transform: scaleY(1); }\n      }\n\n      /* Opacity flips sit on adjacent frames — 78 to 79, 95 to 96 — so no\n         rendered frame ever samples a half-faded mouth, which would snap to\n         whichever palette entry happened to be nearer. */\n      @keyframes yawn-mouth {\n        0%, 60.9375% { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.2, 0.1); }\n        61.71875%    { opacity: 1; transform: scale(0.55, 0.3); }\n        66.40625%    { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1.15, 1.45); }\n        71.875%      { opacity: 1; transform: scale(0.85, 0.65); }\n        74.21875%    { opacity: 1; transform: scale(0.4, 0.18); }\n        75%, 100%    { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.2, 0.1); }\n      }\n\n      @keyframes tear-fall {\n        0%, 67.1875% { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(0); }\n        67.96875%    { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }\n        71.875%      { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(1.4px); }\n        73.4375%     { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(1.9px); }\n        74.21875%, 100% { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(2.1px); }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg ───────────────────────────── -->\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <!-- Planted. Outside every moving group on purpose — see the header. -->\n    <g id=\"legs-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"fx-lean\">\n      <g id=\"fx-action\">\n        <g id=\"fx-breathe\">\n          <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n            <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n\n            <g id=\"fx-arm-l\">\n              <g id=\"fx-arm-l-sway\">\n                <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n              <\/g>\n            <\/g>\n\n            <g id=\"fx-arm-r\">\n              <g id=\"fx-arm-r-sway\">\n                <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n              <\/g>\n            <\/g>\n          <\/g>\n\n          <!-- additive: a mouth, which the base geometry does not have -->\n          <rect id=\"fx-mouth\" x=\"6\" y=\"10\" width=\"3\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#000000\"/>\n\n          <g id=\"fx-eyes-look\">\n            <g id=\"fx-eyes-blink\" fill=\"#000000\">\n              <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n              <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n            <\/g>\n\n            <!-- additive: one pixel of tear at the outer corner of the left\n                 eye, inside the tracking group so it falls from the eye -->\n            <rect id=\"fx-tear\" x=\"3.5\" y=\"9.3\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#40C4FF\"/>\n          <\/g>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","asleep":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"12\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: asleep (no session for five minutes)\n\n    Twelve seconds, 96 frames. Long, because a sleeping creature should be the\n    calmest thing on the panel and a short loop makes a resting breath into a\n    pant. Three breaths fit the loop, and the middle one is a sigh.\n\n    ── How it is put together ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Two nested groups on the same floor pivot at (7.5,15), so their squashes\n    compose into one scale rather than shearing against each other:\n\n      #sleeper   12s   the sigh — flat over the first breath, deep over the\n                       second, flat again for the third\n      #chest      4s   the breath itself: exhale, hold, inhale, pause\n\n    `scaleY` stays below 1 in both tracks. That is the whole trick and it is\n    the opposite of what looks obvious. A breath that *lifts* the torso hauls\n    it off the legs, which are planted outside both groups, and leaves them as\n    four floating stubs — that shipped here, and in `idle`, before it was\n    understood. Compressing about a floor pivot cannot do it: the underside is\n    the pivot, so it is pushed down into the leg tops and never lifts off them.\n\n    ── The pose ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Both claws hang two whole art pixels below where the base geometry puts\n    them, which is what distinguishes this from `idle` at a glance — the arms\n    have gone slack. Only the left one dreams, drifting on its own track.\n\n    They translate; they do not rotate. A 2x2 block turned a few degrees is a\n    staircase, and it lifts its inner corner off the torso and opens a hairline\n    of background at the shoulder. Both artefacts shipped here before. A\n    whole-unit slide keeps the inner edge welded to the body at every frame.\n\n    The eyes squash about their OUTER edge rather than their centre. About the\n    centre a 1x2 rect scaled (2, 0.5) lands half an art pixel off the grid in\n    both axes and straddles four cells; pinned at the outer edge it lands on\n    whole cells and stays symmetric about the torso's centre line.\n\n    ── The Zs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    Three of them, one keyframe track and three delays two seconds apart. The\n    columns differ in geometry rather than in animation, so there is nothing to\n    keep in sync.\n\n    Two things about them are hard-won. They start *above* the head and only\n    ever rise: an earlier version took upstream's y=7 start literally, but\n    upstream's crab is splooted flat and ours is standing, so the same\n    coordinate is mid-chest — grey Zs sat across his face on 76 of 96 frames\n    and read as display corruption. And each glyph cell is a whole art pixel.\n    Capping the growth to protect the spacing had them rendering at half a\n    pixel a cell, where the diagonal degenerates and a Z becomes an I.\n  -->\n  <defs>\n    <style>\n      /* The sigh wraps the breath on the same pivot, so the two squashes\n         compose into one scale about the floor rather than shearing. */\n      #sleeper {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 15px;\n        animation: sigh 12s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      #chest {\n        transform-origin: 7.5px 15px;\n        animation: breathe 4s infinite ease-in-out;\n      }\n\n      /* Both claws hang two whole art pixels below where the base puts them;\n         only the left one dreams. Translation, not rotation: a 2x2 block\n         turned a few degrees is a staircase and lifts its inner corner off\n         the torso, and both artefacts shipped here before. A whole-unit slide\n         keeps the inner edge welded to x=2 (and to x=13) at every frame. */\n      #left-arm {\n        animation: left-claw 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #right-arm {\n        transform: translate(0px, 2px);\n      }\n\n      /* Squash about each eye's OUTER edge, not its centre. About the centre\n         a 1x2 rect scaled (2, 0.5) lands on x 3.5..5.5, y 8.5..9.5 — the right\n         size and half an art pixel off the grid in both axes, so the dash\n         straddles four art cells. Pinned at the outer edge it lands on whole\n         cells and stays symmetric about the torso's centre line. */\n      #left-eye {\n        transform-origin: 5px 8px;\n        animation: eyelid 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      #right-eye {\n        transform-origin: 10px 8px;\n        animation: eyelid 12s infinite linear;\n      }\n\n      /* Shared setup for all three Zs. Deliberately carries no animation\n         property: `#fx-zs > g` is an id plus a type and so outranks a bare\n         `#z-2`, and an `animation` shorthand here would reset the delays that\n         do the staggering. The zero opacity is the resting state for a viewer\n         with animation disabled, where all three would otherwise sit at the\n         glyph's untransformed origin. */\n      #fx-zs > g {\n        opacity: 0;\n      }\n\n      /* One keyframe set, three delays. The three columns differ in the\n         geometry, not in the animation, so there is nothing to keep in sync. */\n      #z-1 { animation: float 6s 0s infinite linear; }\n      #z-2 { animation: float 6s -2s infinite linear; }\n      #z-3 { animation: float 6s -4s infinite linear; }\n\n      /* Exhale, hold, inhale, pause. scaleY is below 1 throughout, so the\n         underside is pushed DOWN into the leg tops and never lifts off them. */\n      @keyframes breathe {\n        0%             { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n        31.25%, 43.75% { transform: scale(1.04, 0.95); }\n        78.125%        { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n        100%           { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n      }\n\n      /* Flat over the first breath, deep over the second, flat again for the\n         third. 43.75%-47.917% of 12s is 5.25s-5.75s, which is exactly the\n         second breath's own hold. */\n      @keyframes sigh {\n        0%, 33.333%     { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n        43.75%, 47.917% { transform: scale(1.04, 0.93); }\n        66.667%, 100%   { transform: scale(1, 1); }\n      }\n\n      /* Up fast, back slowly — a twitch, not a wave. Two art pixels of\n         travel, all of it along the torso's own edge. */\n      @keyframes left-claw {\n        0%, 75%       { transform: translate(0px, 2px); }\n        77.083%       { transform: translate(0px, 0px); }\n        80.208%       { transform: translate(0px, 1px); }\n        83.333%, 100% { transform: translate(0px, 2px); }\n      }\n\n      /* Shut throughout; a whole art pixel narrower on the dream beat. Never\n         scale(_, 0), and never narrower than one art pixel. The two steps are\n         one frame apart, so no rendered frame catches an intermediate width. */\n      @keyframes eyelid {\n        0%, 75%         { transform: scale(2, 0.5); }\n        76.042%, 81.25% { transform: scale(1, 0.5); }\n        82.292%, 100%   { transform: scale(2, 0.5); }\n      }\n\n      /* Four art pixels of climb in thirty-two frames: exactly one device\n         pixel per frame, so every rendered position is a whole device pixel\n         and the glyph never antialiases. Opacity is 0 or 1 and nothing else —\n         the step between them is one frame wide. */\n      @keyframes float {\n        0%       { transform: translateY(1px); opacity: 1; }\n        66.667%  { transform: translateY(-3px); opacity: 1; }\n        68.75%   { transform: translateY(-3px); opacity: 0; }\n        100%     { transform: translateY(-3px); opacity: 0; }\n      }\n    <\/style>\n  <\/defs>\n\n  <!-- ── base geometry: assets/clawd/base.svg, less its shadow ───────── -->\n\n  <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n    <!-- Outside both animated groups: the feet are on the floor and stay\n         there while the body settles onto them. -->\n    <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n\n    <g id=\"sleeper\">\n      <g id=\"chest\">\n        <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n        <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n\n        <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n          <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n          <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n        <\/g>\n      <\/g>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- Zs, one art pixel per glyph cell. Four columns wide and four rows tall,\n       drawn at each column's own x so that one `float` keyframe set can drive\n       all three. The diagonal crosses two interior columns, which is what\n       separates a Z from an I; at three rows the single interior cell sits on\n       the centre line and the glyph rasterises as an I instead. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-zs\" fill=\"#CFD8DC\">\n    <g id=\"z-1\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"4\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"2\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"1\" y=\"2\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"3\" width=\"4\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"z-2\">\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"0\" width=\"4\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"6\" y=\"2\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"3\" width=\"4\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <g id=\"z-3\">\n      <rect x=\"10\" y=\"0\" width=\"4\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"12\" y=\"1\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"11\" y=\"2\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"10\" y=\"3\" width=\"4\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n","birthday":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"-3 -9 21 25\" width=\"168\" height=\"200\"\n     data-loop-seconds=\"2\">\n  <!--\n    Clawd: birthday (the 23 September screen)\n\n    `isBirthday` is true and the pack carries a date, so once a year this is\n    what the panel shows. Clawd jumps, both claws up, in a hat, throwing\n    confetti.\n\n    Generated against assets/clawd/base.svg. Every base element appears below\n    at its original coordinates and colours; all motion is CSS applied by ID.\n\n    ── Both claws, and why they extend ────────────────────────────────────\n\n    §What a claw cannot do carries the arithmetic: a 2x2 claw pivoting at the\n    shoulder cannot get its top edge above y=7.76, so the torso top at y=6 is\n    out of reach by 1.76 units and no rotation alone raises a claw over the\n    head. `gym` and `permission-sign` both answer it the same way — rotate\n    *and* `scaleX`, extending the claw along its own axis until it is an arm\n    rather than a lozenge — and this does too, mirrored, because the pose is\n    two arms up rather than one.\n\n    Held at 62deg and scaleX(2.3) through the airborne frames. Measured at the\n    hang: the tips clear the head by half a unit and reach three units outboard\n    of the torso on each side. It is the outboard length that carries the pose,\n    not the height — `gym` gets 1.74 units of lift from a steeper angle and a\n    longer scale, and this is deliberately shallower so both claws stay clear\n    of the hat. Neither sits against the body edge, which §What a claw cannot\n    do says reads as the slab growing a lump.\n\n    ── The shadow, decided rather than left ───────────────────────────────\n\n    There is no `#ground-shadow` here, because §The base geometry says it is\n    the one base element an animation must not carry — black at half opacity\n    snaps to the background and arrives part-transparent, so it draws nothing.\n    A first draft carried it anyway, with a keyframe block shrinking it on the\n    jump, and the whole thing rendered zero pixels while reading as a beat that\n    existed.\n\n    The renderer paints its own contact shadow instead, and `birthday` is\n    deliberately not in `UNGROUNDED`: it is constant, so it does not shrink\n    while he is airborne, but a jumping crab does still cast one and a\n    shadowless crab standing on sand is the worse of the two errors. Composed\n    on the day sky it reads. If the shadow is ever made to follow the sprite,\n    this is the animation that wants it.\n\n    ── The hat ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    A four-step cone on the torso's centre line (x=7.5, the midpoint of the\n    11-wide torso), sitting on the head at y=6 and topping out at y=2. §Props\n    need contact: its base row overlaps the torso's top row rather than\n    floating a unit clear.\n\n    **The hat is #A371F7, and it was #B22222 until the render was counted.**\n    §Palette snapping says to ask what edge a new colour now sits between, and\n    the honest answer for a red was: the body. A peach edge fading toward the\n    background passes through mid-dark tones, and (89,54,44) is 8421 from\n    #B22222 against 12773 from black — so red won, and 46 pixels of the raised\n    claws' edges came back as a red rim. Purple is nowhere near that ramp, so\n    those edges resolve to black the way every other animation's do. The count\n    is the check: at rest the confetti is hidden inside him, so a resting\n    frame carries exactly the hat — 9 units, 576 pixels — and any excess is\n    the claws' edges being captured. It was 622 against 576 when the hat was\n    red. Frames where the hat is mid-squash carry fewer, and frames mid-burst\n    carry more for the two pieces that share the colour; the resting frames\n    are the ones to count.\n\n    #A371F7 is `bouldering`'s purple and #C9D1D9 the plate tone `thinking` and\n    `permission-sign` use, so neither is a tone the corpus has not already\n    carried against all four skies.\n\n    ── The confetti, and why it bursts rather than falls ──────────────────\n\n    §Safe area: the stage is authored 21x25 but landscape crops to the bottom\n    20, y -4 to 16. Confetti raining from off-screen top would spend its first\n    frames in five units that do not exist on the shipping panel, so it bursts\n    outward from the claws instead — which reads better at 8 device pixels per\n    unit anyway, where falling specks are noise.\n\n    Six pieces, 1x1, in four colours the corpus already declares: #7EE787 and\n    #58A6FF (`typing`), #A371F7 (`bouldering`) and #C9D1D9. §Palette\n    snapping makes every declared colour a snap target for every other\n    colour's antialiased edges, so new tones are not free; these four are\n    already proved to coexist with the body peach and the eye black.\n\n    They translate only — no opacity ramp. A fill at partial opacity is\n    admitted to the palette composited over the background, which would add\n    four more targets sitting between the confetti and the sky. Motion alone\n    costs nothing.\n\n    ── Timing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n    2s at 8fps is 16 frames, and every keyframe below is on a multiple of\n    6.25% so each lands on a frame boundary rather than between two. One jump\n    per loop: crouch, launch, hang, land, squash, recover, settle. The\n    confetti runs the same 2s so there is one burst per jump and no seam at\n    the wrap.\n  -->\n  <style>\n    /* ── the jump ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n       The whole body, hat and confetti origin move together, so the hat\n       stays on his head and the confetti leaves from where the claws are\n       rather than from where they started. */\n    #master-group,\n    #fx-hat {\n      transform-origin: 7.5px 15px;\n      animation: hop 2s infinite steps(1, end);\n    }\n\n    /* **The crouch and the landing are squash, not displacement**, about the\n       floor line at the feet. Translating down for them drove all four legs a\n       full unit into the sand on two frames of sixteen — measured, 32 pixels\n       in the bottom row — and `svg2frames`' safe-area walk cannot see it,\n       because it only checks the top edge. §Movement makes the general case:\n       it is the deformation that sells a beat, not the displacement. The body\n       only leaves the ground while it is genuinely in the air. */\n    @keyframes hop {\n      0%     { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(1); }\n      6.25%  { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(0.94); }\n      12.5%  { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(0.86); }\n      18.75% { transform: translateY(-1.25px) scaleY(1.06); }\n      25%    { transform: translateY(-2.75px) scaleY(1); }\n      31.25% { transform: translateY(-3px) scaleY(1); }\n      37.5%  { transform: translateY(-2.5px) scaleY(1); }\n      43.75% { transform: translateY(-1.25px) scaleY(1.03); }\n      50%    { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(1); }\n      56.25% { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(0.84); }\n      62.5%  { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(0.93); }\n      68.75% { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(1); }\n      75%    { transform: translateY(-0.5px) scaleY(1); }\n      81.25% { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(0.97); }\n      93.75% { transform: translateY(0) scaleY(1); }\n    }\n\n    /* ── the claws ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n       Rotation plus axial scale, on each arm's own shoulder pivot. Up and\n       outward on the launch, held through the hang, down on the landing. */\n    #left-arm {\n      transform-origin: 2px 10px;\n      animation: cheer-left 2s infinite steps(1, end);\n    }\n    #right-arm {\n      transform-origin: 13px 10px;\n      animation: cheer-right 2s infinite steps(1, end);\n    }\n\n    /* **Positive raises the left claw, negative the right**, and the sign is\n       worth stating because a first draft had both backwards and rendered a\n       crab shrugging at the floor. The left claw's centre sits at (-1, 0)\n       from its pivot, so `rotate(a)` sends it to y = -sin(a): up needs a\n       positive angle. `gym` presses to +73deg on the same arm for the same\n       reason.\n\n       Nothing sits between 0 and 42deg. A 2x2 block at a shallow angle is a\n       jagged lozenge — §What a claw cannot do calls the 45deg version an\n       arrowhead, and below that it is worse — so the raise crosses that band\n       inside one frame rather than posing in it. */\n    @keyframes cheer-left {\n      0%, 12.5% { transform: rotate(0deg) scaleX(1); }\n      18.75%    { transform: rotate(46deg) scaleX(1.85); }\n      25%       { transform: rotate(58deg) scaleX(2.25); }\n      31.25%    { transform: rotate(62deg) scaleX(2.3); }\n      37.5%     { transform: rotate(60deg) scaleX(2.28); }\n      43.75%    { transform: rotate(56deg) scaleX(2.2); }\n      50%       { transform: rotate(48deg) scaleX(1.9); }\n      56.25%    { transform: rotate(0deg) scaleX(1); }\n      68.75%    { transform: rotate(52deg) scaleX(2.05); }\n      75%       { transform: rotate(58deg) scaleX(2.25); }\n      87.5%     { transform: rotate(50deg) scaleX(1.95); }\n      93.75%    { transform: rotate(0deg) scaleX(1); }\n    }\n\n    @keyframes cheer-right {\n      0%, 12.5% { transform: rotate(0deg) scaleX(1); }\n      18.75%    { transform: rotate(-46deg) scaleX(1.85); }\n      25%       { transform: rotate(-58deg) scaleX(2.25); }\n      31.25%    { transform: rotate(-62deg) scaleX(2.3); }\n      37.5%     { transform: rotate(-60deg) scaleX(2.28); }\n      43.75%    { transform: rotate(-56deg) scaleX(2.2); }\n      50%       { transform: rotate(-48deg) scaleX(1.9); }\n      56.25%    { transform: rotate(0deg) scaleX(1); }\n      68.75%    { transform: rotate(-52deg) scaleX(2.05); }\n      75%       { transform: rotate(-58deg) scaleX(2.25); }\n      87.5%     { transform: rotate(-50deg) scaleX(1.95); }\n      93.75%    { transform: rotate(0deg) scaleX(1); }\n    }\n\n    /* ── the confetti ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n       Each piece leaves the claws on the launch frame and arcs outward.\n       Translate only, so nothing is added to the palette. */\n    #c1 { animation: c1 2s infinite steps(1, end); }\n    #c2 { animation: c2 2s infinite steps(1, end); }\n    #c3 { animation: c3 2s infinite steps(1, end); }\n    #c4 { animation: c4 2s infinite steps(1, end); }\n    #c5 { animation: c5 2s infinite steps(1, end); }\n    #c6 { animation: c6 2s infinite steps(1, end); }\n\n    @keyframes c1 {\n      0%, 12.5% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      18.75%   { transform: translate(-1.5px, -4px); }\n      25%      { transform: translate(-3px, -6.5px); }\n      31.25%   { transform: translate(-4px, -7.5px); }\n      37.5%    { transform: translate(-5px, -7px); }\n      43.75%   { transform: translate(-6px, -5.5px); }\n      50%      { transform: translate(-6.5px, -3px); }\n      56.25%   { transform: translate(-7px, 0.5px); }\n      62.5%    { transform: translate(-7.5px, 5px); }\n      68.75%   { transform: translate(-8px, 10px); }\n      75%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n    }\n    @keyframes c2 {\n      0%, 12.5% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      18.75%   { transform: translate(1.5px, -4px); }\n      25%      { transform: translate(3px, -6.5px); }\n      31.25%   { transform: translate(4px, -7.5px); }\n      37.5%    { transform: translate(5px, -7px); }\n      43.75%   { transform: translate(6px, -5.5px); }\n      50%      { transform: translate(6.5px, -3px); }\n      56.25%   { transform: translate(7px, 0.5px); }\n      62.5%    { transform: translate(7.5px, 5px); }\n      68.75%   { transform: translate(8px, 10px); }\n      75%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n    }\n    @keyframes c3 {\n      0%, 12.5% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      18.75%   { transform: translate(-1px, -4.5px); }\n      25%      { transform: translate(-2px, -7px); }\n      31.25%   { transform: translate(-2.5px, -8.5px); }\n      37.5%    { transform: translate(-3px, -8.5px); }\n      43.75%   { transform: translate(-3.5px, -7px); }\n      50%      { transform: translate(-4px, -4.5px); }\n      56.25%   { transform: translate(-4.5px, -1px); }\n      62.5%    { transform: translate(-5px, 4px); }\n      68.75%   { transform: translate(-5.5px, 9px); }\n      75%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n    }\n    @keyframes c4 {\n      0%, 12.5% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      18.75%   { transform: translate(1px, -4.5px); }\n      25%      { transform: translate(2px, -7px); }\n      31.25%   { transform: translate(2.5px, -8.5px); }\n      37.5%    { transform: translate(3px, -8.5px); }\n      43.75%   { transform: translate(3.5px, -7px); }\n      50%      { transform: translate(4px, -4.5px); }\n      56.25%   { transform: translate(4.5px, -1px); }\n      62.5%    { transform: translate(5px, 4px); }\n      68.75%   { transform: translate(5.5px, 9px); }\n      75%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n    }\n    @keyframes c5 {\n      0%, 12.5% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      18.75%   { transform: translate(-0.5px, -5px); }\n      25%      { transform: translate(-1px, -7.5px); }\n      31.25%   { transform: translate(-1.5px, -9px); }\n      37.5%    { transform: translate(-2px, -9px); }\n      43.75%   { transform: translate(-2.5px, -7.5px); }\n      50%      { transform: translate(-3px, -5px); }\n      56.25%   { transform: translate(-3.5px, -1.5px); }\n      62.5%    { transform: translate(-4px, 3.5px); }\n      68.75%   { transform: translate(-4.5px, 8.5px); }\n      75%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n    }\n    @keyframes c6 {\n      0%, 12.5% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n      18.75%   { transform: translate(0.5px, -5px); }\n      25%      { transform: translate(1px, -7.5px); }\n      31.25%   { transform: translate(1.5px, -9px); }\n      37.5%    { transform: translate(2px, -9px); }\n      43.75%   { transform: translate(2.5px, -7.5px); }\n      50%      { transform: translate(3px, -5px); }\n      56.25%   { transform: translate(3.5px, -1.5px); }\n      62.5%    { transform: translate(4px, 3.5px); }\n      68.75%   { transform: translate(4.5px, 8.5px); }\n      75%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0); }\n    }\n  <\/style>\n\n  <!-- Drawn before the crab, which is what makes the rest position work: all\n       six sit inside the torso's footprint and are occluded by it. They are\n       only ever seen between the launch and falling off the bottom of the\n       stage, so the reset at the end of the loop happens where nothing can\n       see it. An earlier version parked them in open sky, where they hung\n       motionless for seven frames of sixteen and then vanished for one. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-confetti\">\n    <rect id=\"c1\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#7EE787\"/>\n    <rect id=\"c2\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#A371F7\"/>\n    <rect id=\"c3\" x=\"5\" y=\"10\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#58A6FF\"/>\n    <rect id=\"c4\" x=\"9\" y=\"10\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n    <rect id=\"c5\" x=\"6\" y=\"9\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#A371F7\"/>\n    <rect id=\"c6\" x=\"8\" y=\"9\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#7EE787\"/>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <g id=\"master-group\">\n    <g id=\"body-color-group\" fill=\"#DE886D\">\n      <rect id=\"torso\" x=\"2\" y=\"6\" width=\"11\" height=\"7\"/>\n      <rect id=\"left-arm\" x=\"0\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"right-arm\" x=\"13\" y=\"9\" width=\"2\" height=\"2\"/>\n\n      <rect id=\"outer-left-leg\" x=\"3\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-left-leg\" x=\"5\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"inner-right-leg\" x=\"9\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"outer-right-leg\" x=\"11\" y=\"13\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n\n    <g id=\"eyes-color-group\" fill=\"#000000\">\n      <rect id=\"left-eye\" x=\"4\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n      <rect id=\"right-eye\" x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"1\" height=\"2\"/>\n    <\/g>\n  <\/g>\n\n  <!-- Drawn after the body so the brim sits in front of the head, and given\n       its own copy of the jump so it rides with him. -->\n  <g id=\"fx-hat\">\n    <g fill=\"#A371F7\">\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"5\" width=\"5\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"6\" y=\"4\" width=\"3\" height=\"1\"/>\n      <rect x=\"7\" y=\"3\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"/>\n    <\/g>\n    <rect x=\"7\" y=\"2\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" fill=\"#C9D1D9\"/>\n  <\/g>\n<\/svg>\n"};
  // The page stylesheet stops at the shadow boundary, so the sizing has to go
  // in with the clip — without it each SVG renders at its intrinsic 168x200
  // and overflows the frame.
  //
  // The motion kill is gated on an attribute rather than written as a bare
  // `@media (prefers-reduced-motion)`. On this page the animations are not
  // decoration — they are the entire subject — so honouring the preference by
  // freezing them silently leaves fifteen still crabs and nothing saying why.
  // The default still respects the preference; the difference is that there is
  // a way back, and the page says so.
  const fit =
    '<style>:host{display:block}svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;' +
    'image-rendering:pixelated}' +
    ':host([data-still]) *{animation:none!important}</style>';
  const hosts = [...document.querySelectorAll('.glass[data-clip]')];
  for (const host of hosts) {
    const clip = clips[host.dataset.clip];
    if (clip) host.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' }).innerHTML = fit + clip;
  }

  const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
  const note = document.querySelector('.motion');
  const toggle = note.querySelector('button');
  let playing = false;

  function syncMotion() {
    const still = reduce.matches && !playing;
    for (const host of hosts) host.toggleAttribute('data-still', still);
    note.hidden = !reduce.matches;
    toggle.textContent = playing ? 'Hold them still' : 'Play them anyway';
    toggle.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(playing));
  }

  toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
    playing = !playing;
    syncMotion();
  });
  // The preference can change while the page is open — a phone entering low
  // power mode turns it on.
  reduce.addEventListener('change', syncMotion);
  syncMotion();
</script>

